ADDART({id:"elections",t:"Elections and Voting",c:"Economics and Society",i:"🗳️",w:"Election",r:["democ","phgov","law","hrights","edsa"],
p:[["Choosing leaders","An election is a formal way for people to choose their leaders or decide questions by voting."],["One person, one vote","In democracies, adult citizens usually have an equal vote, cast in secret."],["Hard-won rights","The right to vote was won step by step, by working people, women and minorities, over many years."]],
l:"An election is a process in which citizens choose people to hold public office, or decide on an issue, by casting votes. Free and fair elections are at the heart of democracy: they let people choose their governments and peacefully replace them. Today most countries hold elections, though not all are free. The right to vote, once limited to a few wealthy men in many places, has gradually been extended to all adult citizens. In 2024, more people voted around the world than in any year before.",
s:[["Early elections","In ancient Athens, from the 500s BCE, male citizens voted by raising hands or casting pebbles, and chose many officials by lot. Each year they could vote to exile a powerful politician for ten years by writing his name on a piece of pottery called an ostrakon, the origin of the word ostracism. Ancient Rome also had voting assemblies."],["The secret ballot","For a long time, people voted openly, which allowed bribery and intimidation. In 1856 the Australian colonies of Victoria and South Australia introduced the secret ballot, with government-printed ballot papers marked privately. Known as the Australian ballot, it spread around the world. Secret voting is now a basic principle of free elections."],["Who can vote","In many countries, voting was once limited to men who owned property. Over time, working men, women and racial minorities won the vote. New Zealand, in 1893, became the first self-governing country where women could vote in national elections. In the Philippines, women voted for the first time after a special plebiscite in 1937. Most countries now set the voting age at 18."],["Voting systems","Countries count votes in different ways. In first-past-the-post systems, the candidate with the most votes wins. In proportional representation, parties win seats in proportion to their share of the vote. Ranked-choice systems let voters rank candidates in order of preference. Each system has advantages and drawbacks."]],
k:[["Basic principle","One person, one vote"],["Secret ballot introduced","Australia, 1856"],["First women’s national vote","New Zealand, 1893"],["Philippine women’s suffrage plebiscite","1937"],["Largest election","India, 2024: over 640 million voters"]],
d:[["c. 487 BCE","Athenians first use ostracism"],["1856","The secret ballot is introduced in Australia"],["1893","Women in New Zealand win the vote"],["1937","Filipino women vote in a plebiscite for suffrage"],["1948","The Universal Declaration of Human Rights includes the right to vote"],["2010","The Philippines holds its first nationwide automated elections"]],
s2:[["The world’s largest election","India holds the largest elections in the world. In its 2024 general election, nearly a billion people were eligible to vote, and more than 640 million did so over six weeks, using electronic voting machines at about a million polling stations. Election officials even set up polling stations in remote forests and mountains so that every voter could be reached."],["Elections in the Philippines","The Philippines holds national and local elections on the second Monday of May, every three years for many offices, with presidents elected every six years for a single term. The Commission on Elections, COMELEC, manages them. Since 2010, votes have been counted by machines. Voters mark ballots and have their finger marked with indelible ink to prevent voting twice."],["Compulsory voting","In some countries, including Australia, Belgium, Brazil and Argentina, voting is compulsory, and citizens who do not vote may be fined. Australia introduced compulsory voting in 1924, and turnout there is usually above 90 percent. Supporters say it makes governments represent everyone; critics say people should be free not to vote."],["Free and fair elections","For an election to be free and fair, voters must be able to choose without fear, candidates must be able to campaign, the media must report freely and votes must be counted honestly. International and local observers watch elections in many countries to check that rules are followed. Some governments hold elections that are not genuinely free."],["Referendums","In a referendum, citizens vote directly on a question rather than for a person, such as approving a new constitution or a major change. Switzerland holds referendums several times a year on many issues. The Philippines approved its 1987 constitution in a plebiscite, a kind of referendum."],["Voter education","Democracy works best when voters are informed. Schools teach how government works, and organizations explain how to register, where to vote and how to check facts about candidates. Young people are encouraged to register as soon as they are eligible."]],
sp:["Ink on the finger","Many countries mark voters’ fingers with indelible ink to stop people from voting more than once. The ink contains silver nitrate, which stains the skin when exposed to light and lasts for days. India began using it in 1962, and it has since been used in elections across Asia, Africa and Latin America, including in the Philippines. A purple or black fingertip has become a proud symbol of having voted."],
g:[["Ballot","A paper or electronic form used to vote"],["Suffrage","The right to vote"],["Referendum","A direct vote by citizens on a question"],["Turnout","The share of eligible voters who vote"]],
f:["The secret ballot began in Australia in 1856.","New Zealand women won the vote in 1893.","Filipino women voted in a suffrage plebiscite in 1937."],
fun:"The word ostracism comes from the pottery shards Athenians used to vote someone into exile.",
tl:"Ang halalan ay paraan ng pagpili ng mga pinuno sa pamamagitan ng pagboto. Sa demokrasya, may pantay na boto ang bawat mamamayan at lihim ang pagboto. Unang bumoto ang kababaihang Pilipino sa plebisito noong 1937."});

ADDART({id:"constitution",t:"Constitutions",c:"Economics and Society",i:"📜",w:"Constitution",r:["democ","law","hrights","phgov","malolos"],
p:[["The rules of a country","A constitution is the basic set of rules that sets up a government and limits its powers."],["Rights and powers","Constitutions describe how leaders are chosen and protect the rights of citizens."],["Supreme law","In most countries, the constitution is the highest law, above all other laws."]],
l:"A constitution is a set of fundamental principles and rules that establishes how a country is governed. It sets out the main institutions of government, such as the legislature, executive and courts, divides power among them and usually protects the rights of citizens. Most countries have a written constitution, a single document adopted at a particular time, though a few, such as the United Kingdom, rely on a mix of laws, court decisions and customs. Constitutions can be changed, but usually only through special procedures.",
s:[["What a constitution does","A constitution answers basic questions: who makes the laws, who carries them out and who settles disputes. It explains how leaders are chosen and how long they serve. It often includes a bill of rights, listing freedoms such as speech, religion and a fair trial. By limiting the government’s power, it protects people from abuse."],["Separation of powers","Many constitutions divide government into three branches: a legislature that makes laws, an executive that carries them out and a judiciary that interprets them. The French thinker Montesquieu argued in 1748 that dividing power this way protects liberty. Each branch can check the others, a system called checks and balances."],["Early constitutions","Magna Carta, agreed by King John of England in 1215, limited the king’s power and influenced later ideas of law. The Constitution of the United States, written in 1787, is the oldest written national constitution still in use. Many other countries wrote constitutions in the 1800s and 1900s, especially after independence."],["Changing a constitution","Constitutions are usually harder to change than ordinary laws, so that basic rules are not altered lightly. Changes, called amendments, may require large majorities in the legislature or approval by citizens in a referendum. Some constitutions are amended often; others rarely."]],
k:[["Purpose","Establish government and protect rights"],["Oldest written national constitution in use","United States, 1787"],["Longest national constitution","India (adopted 1949)"],["Current Philippine constitution","1987"],["Countries without a single written constitution","United Kingdom, New Zealand, Israel"]],
d:[["1215","Magna Carta is sealed in England"],["1748","Montesquieu publishes The Spirit of the Laws"],["1787","The United States Constitution is written"],["1899","The Malolos Constitution establishes the First Philippine Republic"],["1950","India’s constitution comes into force"],["1987","Filipinos ratify the current Philippine constitution"]],
s2:[["Constitutions of the Philippines","The Philippines has had several constitutions. The Malolos Constitution of 1899 created the First Philippine Republic, one of the first republican constitutions in Asia. The 1935 Constitution prepared the country for independence; the 1973 Constitution was adopted under martial law. After the EDSA People Power Revolution, Filipinos ratified the 1987 Constitution in a plebiscite on 2 February 1987."],["India’s constitution","The Constitution of India, adopted on 26 November 1949 and in force from 26 January 1950, is the longest written national constitution in the world. Its drafting committee was chaired by B. R. Ambedkar, a lawyer and social reformer from a Dalit background. It created a democratic republic for hundreds of millions of people of many languages and religions."],["Unwritten constitutions","The United Kingdom has no single constitutional document. Instead, its constitution is made up of laws passed by Parliament, court decisions, traditions and important documents such as Magna Carta and the Bill of Rights of 1689. Supporters say this makes it flexible; critics say it makes rights less secure."],["Courts and constitutions","In many countries, a supreme or constitutional court can strike down laws that conflict with the constitution, a power called judicial review. The Supreme Court of the Philippines and courts in countries such as Germany, South Africa and the United States have made landmark decisions protecting rights."],["New constitutions","Countries often write new constitutions after independence, revolution or major change. South Africa adopted a new constitution in 1996 after apartheid ended, with one of the world’s most extensive bills of rights. Japan’s 1947 constitution renounced war. Writing a constitution often involves debate among many groups."],["Constitution Day","Many countries celebrate the adoption of their constitution with a national holiday. India marks Republic Day on 26 January, Japan celebrates Constitution Memorial Day on 3 May and Norway celebrates 17 May with parades of children. These days remind citizens of their rights and responsibilities."]],
sp:["The Malolos Constitution","In 1898, during the revolution against Spain, Filipino delegates met at Barasoain Church in Malolos, Bulacan, to write a constitution. Approved and promulgated in January 1899, it established a republic with a strong legislature and a bill of rights, separated church and state and declared that sovereignty resides in the people. The First Philippine Republic it created was short-lived because of the war with the United States, but it remains a symbol of Filipino self-government."],
g:[["Constitution","The basic rules that set up and limit a government"],["Amendment","A change to a constitution"],["Bill of rights","A list of basic rights and freedoms"],["Judicial review","A court’s power to strike down unconstitutional laws"]],
f:["The US Constitution of 1787 is the oldest written national constitution in use.","India has the longest constitution.","The Philippines ratified its current constitution in 1987."],
fun:"The United Kingdom has no single written constitution document.",
tl:"Ang saligang batas o konstitusyon ang pangunahing hanay ng mga tuntunin na nagtatatag ng pamahalaan at naglilimita sa kapangyarihan nito. Pinoprotektahan din nito ang karapatan ng mamamayan. Ang kasalukuyang Saligang Batas ng Pilipinas ay pinagtibay noong 1987."});

ADDART({id:"monarchy",t:"Monarchy",c:"Economics and Society",i:"👑",w:"Monarchy",r:["democ","constitution","sulu","japan","tongacountry"],
p:[["Kings and queens","A monarchy is a form of government with a king, queen, emperor or other monarch as head of state."],["Usually inherited","In most monarchies, the crown passes down within a royal family."],["Old and new","Some monarchs hold great power, while in many democracies the monarch’s role is mainly ceremonial."]],
l:"A monarchy is a system in which a single person, the monarch, is head of state, usually for life, and the position is usually inherited. For most of history, monarchy was the most common form of government. Today about 40 countries have monarchs, ranging from absolute monarchies, where the ruler holds great power, to constitutional monarchies, where elected governments rule and the monarch serves as a symbol of the nation. Many former monarchies have become republics.",
s:[["Absolute monarchy","In an absolute monarchy, the monarch holds supreme power and is not limited by a constitution or elected parliament. In Europe, King Louis XIV of France, who reigned for 72 years, became a famous example. Today countries such as Saudi Arabia, Brunei, Oman and Eswatini have monarchs with wide powers."],["Constitutional monarchy","In a constitutional monarchy, the monarch’s powers are limited by a constitution, and an elected parliament and prime minister govern. The monarch performs ceremonial duties, represents the nation and may advise. The United Kingdom, Japan, Spain, Sweden, Norway, the Netherlands, Thailand and Malaysia are examples."],["How monarchs are chosen","Most monarchies are hereditary: the crown passes to a child or relative. Traditionally, sons came before daughters, but countries such as Sweden, in 1980, and others changed their laws so the eldest child inherits regardless of gender. Some monarchies are elective: in Malaysia, the king is chosen from among nine state rulers for a five-year term, and the pope, head of Vatican City, is elected by cardinals."],["The end of many monarchies","Many monarchies ended through revolutions or reforms. France abolished its monarchy in 1792, China’s last emperor gave up the throne in 1912, Russia’s monarchy fell in 1917 and Nepal became a republic in 2008. Some countries, such as Spain in 1975, restored their monarchies."]],
k:[["Countries with monarchs","About 40"],["Oldest continuing hereditary monarchy","Japan"],["Longest reign (European sovereign state)","Louis XIV of France, 72 years"],["Elective monarchy examples","Malaysia, Vatican City"],["Recent abolition","Nepal, 2008"]],
d:[["c. 3100 BCE","Egypt is united under pharaohs"],["1215","Magna Carta limits the English king’s power"],["1792","France abolishes its monarchy"],["1912","China’s last emperor abdicates"],["1975","Spain restores its monarchy"],["2008","Nepal abolishes its monarchy; Bhutan adopts a constitutional monarchy"]],
s2:[["Japan’s imperial family","Japan’s imperial family claims an unbroken line stretching back, according to tradition, to 660 BCE, making it the oldest continuing hereditary monarchy in the world, though the earliest emperors are legendary. Since 1947, the emperor has been the symbol of the state with no political power. In 2019 Emperor Akihito became the first Japanese emperor to abdicate in about two centuries."],["Sultanates of the Philippines","Before and during Spanish colonization, parts of the southern Philippines were ruled by Muslim sultanates, such as the Sultanate of Sulu, founded in the 1400s, and the Sultanate of Maguindanao. Their sultans governed, traded with China and Southeast Asia and resisted Spanish conquest for centuries. Royal families of these sultanates still hold cultural importance today."],["Bhutan’s choice","In Bhutan, the king himself pushed for democracy. King Jigme Singye Wangchuck began reforms and abdicated in 2006 in favor of his son, and in 2008 Bhutan adopted a constitution and held its first parliamentary elections, becoming a constitutional monarchy. Bhutan is also known for measuring Gross National Happiness."],["Thailand’s monarchy","Thailand has been a constitutional monarchy since 1932. King Bhumibol Adulyadej reigned from 1946 to 2016, more than 70 years, one of the longest reigns in history. The Thai monarchy is highly revered, and strict laws protect it from criticism."],["Monarchies and tourism","Royal palaces, ceremonies and weddings attract visitors and media attention around the world. The Changing of the Guard at Buckingham Palace in London and royal palaces in Bangkok, Kyoto and Stockholm are major tourist attractions. Supporters say monarchies provide continuity and national unity; critics say they are undemocratic and costly."],["The Commonwealth realms","King Charles III is head of state not only of the United Kingdom but also of 14 other countries, called Commonwealth realms, including Canada, Australia, New Zealand, Jamaica and Papua New Guinea. In each, he is represented by a governor-general. Some of these countries are debating whether to become republics; Barbados did so in 2021."]],
sp:["Queen Elizabeth II","Queen Elizabeth II became queen of the United Kingdom and other realms in 1952, at age 25, and reigned until her death in September 2022, a total of 70 years and 214 days. She was the longest-reigning British monarch and the second-longest-reigning monarch of a sovereign country in recorded history, after Louis XIV. During her reign, the British Empire gave way to the Commonwealth, a voluntary association of 56 countries."],
g:[["Monarch","A king, queen, emperor or other hereditary ruler"],["Absolute monarchy","A monarchy where the ruler holds supreme power"],["Constitutional monarchy","A monarchy limited by a constitution"],["Abdicate","To give up the throne"]],
f:["About 40 countries have monarchs.","Malaysia’s king is elected every five years.","Nepal became a republic in 2008."],
fun:"Malaysia’s king is chosen from among nine state rulers and serves for five years.",
tl:"Ang monarkiya ay uri ng pamahalaan na may hari, reyna o emperador bilang pinuno ng estado. Karaniwang namamana ang korona. Sa ilang bansa, malawak ang kapangyarihan ng monarka; sa iba, seremonyal lamang ang tungkulin nito."});

ADDART({id:"journalism",t:"News and Journalism",c:"Economics and Society",i:"📰",w:"Journalism",r:["print","tv","radio","socialmedia","hrights"],
p:[["Telling people what happens","Journalism is the work of gathering, checking and reporting news and information."],["From newspapers to phones","News has traveled by handwritten bulletins, printed papers, radio, television and the internet."],["Watchdogs of power","A free press helps citizens hold governments and powerful people to account."]],
l:"Journalism is the activity of gathering, verifying, writing and presenting news and information to the public. Journalists report on events in their communities and around the world, investigate wrongdoing, explain complex issues and give voice to different people. News has been shared for thousands of years, but modern journalism grew with the printing press, newspapers, radio, television and now the internet. A free and independent press is considered essential to democracy, and journalists in many countries face danger for their work.",
s:[["Early news","In ancient Rome, from 59 BCE, the government posted daily news bulletins called the Acta Diurna in public places. In China, court gazettes reported government news for centuries. The first printed newspaper is often considered to be Relation, published in Strasbourg from 1605. Newspapers soon spread across Europe."],["Newspapers for everyone","In the 1830s, cheap penny newspapers in cities such as New York brought news to ordinary workers. The telegraph sent news across continents within hours, and news agencies such as Reuters, founded in 1851, supplied stories to papers worldwide. Photography and later radio and television brought news to life."],["Principles of journalism","Good journalism aims to be accurate, fair and independent. Journalists check facts, use reliable sources, give people a chance to respond to criticism and correct mistakes. They separate news reports from opinion and avoid conflicts of interest. Protecting confidential sources can be essential to uncovering wrongdoing."],["News in the digital age","The internet and smartphones transformed journalism. News is now available instantly, often for free, and anyone can share information on social media. This has opened new voices but also made it easier for false information to spread. Many newspapers have lost advertising income, while new online outlets and fact-checking organizations have emerged."]],
k:[["Early news bulletin","Acta Diurna, Rome, 59 BCE"],["First printed newspaper","Relation, Strasbourg, 1605"],["World Press Freedom Day","3 May"],["Famous journalism prize","Pulitzer Prize, since 1917"],["Filipino Nobel laureate","Maria Ressa, 2021"]],
d:[["59 BCE","Rome posts the Acta Diurna"],["1605","Relation is published in Strasbourg"],["1811","Del Superior Gobierno, the first newspaper in the Philippines, appears"],["1889","La Solidaridad is founded by Filipino reformists in Spain"],["1993","The UN proclaims World Press Freedom Day"],["2021","Maria Ressa and Dmitry Muratov win the Nobel Peace Prize"]],
s2:[["Journalism in the Philippines","The first newspaper in the Philippines, Del Superior Gobierno, appeared in 1811. In the late 1800s, Filipino reformists including José Rizal, Graciano López Jaena and Marcelo H. del Pilar wrote for La Solidaridad in Spain, calling for reforms. Under martial law in the 1970s, many news outlets were closed, and alternative newspapers helped keep information flowing."],["Investigative journalism","Investigative journalists spend months or years uncovering hidden wrongdoing. In the 1970s, reporters Bob Woodward and Carl Bernstein of The Washington Post helped expose the Watergate scandal, leading to the resignation of US President Richard Nixon in 1974. Teams of journalists from many countries have since worked together to expose corruption and hidden wealth."],["Press freedom","Press freedom means journalists can report without censorship or fear. In some countries, governments control the media or jail critics. Organizations such as Reporters Without Borders and the Committee to Protect Journalists track threats. Journalists are sometimes killed for their work; in 2009, 32 journalists were among the 58 people killed in the Maguindanao massacre in the Philippines."],["Maria Ressa","Maria Ressa, a Filipino-American journalist, co-founded the online news site Rappler in 2012. In 2021 she and the Russian journalist Dmitry Muratov were awarded the Nobel Peace Prize for their efforts to safeguard freedom of expression. She was the first Filipino to win a Nobel Prize."],["Spotting false news","False and misleading information can spread quickly online. Readers can protect themselves by checking who published a story, looking for the same news from reliable sources, checking dates, being wary of headlines designed to shock and asking whether a photo or video might be altered or taken out of context. Fact-checking groups help verify viral claims."],["Kinds of journalism","Journalism comes in many forms: breaking news, sports, science, business, culture, photojournalism, documentaries, podcasts and data journalism, which uses statistics and graphics to tell stories. Student newspapers give young people practice in reporting and writing."]],
sp:["The Acta Diurna","Around 59 BCE, Julius Caesar ordered that a record of public events be posted daily in the Roman Forum. Written on boards, the Acta Diurna, or daily acts, reported on government decisions, trials, births, deaths, marriages and even gladiator contests. Scribes copied the news and sent it to readers across the empire. It was one of the earliest known forms of a government news bulletin."],
g:[["Journalist","A person who gathers and reports news"],["Source","A person or document that provides information"],["Press freedom","The right to report without censorship"],["Fact-checking","Checking whether claims are true"]],
f:["Ancient Rome posted daily news bulletins.","The first printed newspaper appeared in 1605.","Maria Ressa won the Nobel Peace Prize in 2021."],
fun:"Ancient Rome had a daily news bulletin called the Acta Diurna.",
tl:"Ang pamamahayag o journalism ay pangangalap, pagsusuri at pag-uulat ng balita. Mahalaga ang malayang pamamahayag sa demokrasya. Si Maria Ressa ang unang Pilipinong nanalo ng Nobel Prize, noong 2021."});

ADDART({id:"tourism",t:"Tourism and Travel",c:"Economics and Society",i:"🧳",w:"Tourism",r:["trade","flight","rail","boracay","urban"],
p:[["Seeing the world","Tourism means traveling for pleasure, to visit places, meet people and experience other cultures."],["A giant industry","Tourism employs millions of people and is a major part of many countries’ economies."],["Travel with care","Responsible tourism protects places and respects local communities."]],
l:"Tourism is travel for leisure, recreation, culture, family visits or business, along with the industry that serves travelers: transport, hotels, restaurants, guides and attractions. People have traveled to holy sites and famous places for thousands of years, but mass tourism took off in the 1800s with railways and in the 1900s with cars and jet planes. In 2024 there were about 1.4 billion international tourist arrivals. Tourism brings income and jobs but can also strain environments and communities.",
s:[["Early travelers","Pilgrims have long traveled to sacred places, such as Mecca, Jerusalem, Varanasi and Santiago de Compostela. Ancient Romans visited Greece and Egypt. From the 1600s, young wealthy Europeans took the Grand Tour through France and Italy to study art and culture. Travel writers such as Ibn Battuta and Marco Polo inspired others."],["The birth of mass tourism","In 1841 the Englishman Thomas Cook organized a railway trip for about 500 people from Leicester to Loughborough, often called the first package tour. Railways, steamships and later cars made travel cheaper. After the 1950s, jet airplanes and paid holidays allowed millions of ordinary people to travel abroad."],["Kinds of tourism","People travel for many reasons: beach holidays, city sightseeing, adventure sports, food, history, religion, nature and visiting family. Ecotourism focuses on nature while protecting it. Medical tourism involves traveling for treatment, and business travel includes conferences and meetings."],["Tourism and the economy","Tourism provides jobs for tens of millions of people, from hotel workers and guides to farmers and craftspeople who sell to visitors. For some small island countries, it is the largest industry. France, Spain, the United States, Italy and Türkiye are among the most visited countries."]],
k:[["International arrivals (2024)","About 1.4 billion"],["First package tour","Thomas Cook, 1841"],["Most visited country","France"],["World Tourism Day","27 September"],["UN agency","UN Tourism"]],
d:[["1600s–1700s","Young Europeans take the Grand Tour"],["1841","Thomas Cook organizes his first group excursion"],["1872","Yellowstone becomes the first national park"],["1950s","Jet airliners begin to make long-distance travel faster"],["2018","Boracay closes for six months for environmental rehabilitation"],["2020","The COVID-19 pandemic causes international tourism to collapse"]],
s2:[["Tourism in the Philippines","The Philippines attracts visitors with its beaches, dive sites, rice terraces, historic towns and festivals. Popular destinations include Boracay, Palawan, Bohol, Siargao, Cebu, Vigan and Banaue. The 2012 campaign It’s More Fun in the Philippines became widely known. Tourism provides many jobs, and Filipino hospitality is famous."],["Overtourism","Some places receive more visitors than they can handle, a problem called overtourism. Crowds can damage monuments, raise housing costs and disrupt local life. Venice began charging day-trippers an entry fee in 2024, and Boracay was closed for six months in 2018 to clean up pollution. Other places limit visitor numbers, as at Machu Picchu."],["Ecotourism","Ecotourism aims to let visitors enjoy nature while protecting it and benefiting local people. In Costa Rica, Rwanda and the Philippines, entry fees and guided tours help fund conservation of rainforests, mountain gorillas and marine sanctuaries such as Tubbataha Reefs. Travelers are encouraged to leave no trace and respect wildlife."],["The pandemic","In 2020 the COVID-19 pandemic brought international travel almost to a stop, as borders closed and flights were cancelled. International arrivals fell by about 70 percent, causing millions of job losses. Tourism gradually recovered, reaching close to pre-pandemic levels by 2024."],["Being a responsible traveler","Responsible travelers learn about local customs, dress respectfully at religious sites, support local businesses, avoid buying products made from endangered animals, reduce waste and respect people’s privacy when taking photos. Learning a few words of the local language is always appreciated."],["Travel and climate","Air travel produces a significant share of transport emissions. Some travelers choose trains for shorter trips, travel less often but stay longer, or support airlines investing in cleaner fuels. Tourism destinations themselves, especially islands and ski resorts, are affected by climate change."]],
sp:["Thomas Cook’s first trip","On 5 July 1841, Thomas Cook, a cabinetmaker and temperance campaigner, arranged for about 500 people to travel by train from Leicester to a meeting in Loughborough, about 18 kilometers away, for a shilling each, including food. The trip was a success, and Cook began organizing tours across Britain, then Europe, Egypt and around the world. His company became one of the most famous names in travel for more than 175 years."],
g:[["Tourism","Travel for pleasure, culture or business"],["Package tour","A trip with transport, lodging and activities sold together"],["Ecotourism","Nature travel that protects the environment"],["Overtourism","Too many tourists in one place"]],
f:["About 1.4 billion international tourist trips were made in 2024.","Thomas Cook organized an early package tour in 1841.","World Tourism Day is on 27 September."],
fun:"The first package tour in 1841 cost a shilling per person and included food.",
tl:"Ang turismo ay paglalakbay para sa kasiyahan, kultura o negosyo. Nagbibigay ito ng hanapbuhay sa milyon-milyong tao. Sa Pilipinas, kilala ang Boracay, Palawan, Bohol at Banaue."});

ADDART({id:"noodles",t:"Noodles",c:"Food and Agriculture",i:"🍜",w:"Noodle",r:["wheat","rice","phfood","china","japan"],
p:[["A world favorite","Noodles are long strips of dough, eaten in soups, stir-fries and sauces around the world."],["Very old food","The oldest known noodles, found in China, are about 4,000 years old."],["Pancit and more","In the Philippines, pancit is a favorite dish at celebrations, symbolizing long life."]],
l:"Noodles are strips or strings of unleavened dough, usually made from wheat, rice, buckwheat or mung bean starch, cooked by boiling, frying or steaming. They are a staple food in much of Asia and are loved worldwide, from Chinese lamian and Japanese ramen to Italian pasta, Vietnamese phở and Filipino pancit. The oldest known noodles, made of millet, were found in China and are about 4,000 years old. Instant noodles, invented in Japan in 1958, are now eaten in more than 100 billion servings a year.",
s:[["Ancient noodles","In 2005 archaeologists at Lajia in northwestern China reported finding an overturned bowl containing thin yellow noodles made from millet, preserved for about 4,000 years. Noodles made from wheat became common in China during the Han dynasty, about 2,000 years ago, and spread across Asia along trade routes."],["Kinds of noodles","Wheat noodles include Chinese lamian, hand-pulled into long strands, Japanese udon and ramen, and Italian spaghetti. Rice noodles are popular in southern China and Southeast Asia, in dishes such as phở and pad thai. Japanese soba is made from buckwheat, and glass noodles from mung bean starch."],["Pancit","In the Philippines, noodles were introduced by Chinese traders and settlers, and the word pancit is thought to come from a Hokkien phrase meaning something conveniently cooked. Varieties include pancit canton, bihon, palabok, Malabon, lomi and Lucban’s pancit habhab, eaten off a banana leaf. Pancit is served at birthdays because long noodles symbolize long life."],["Pasta","Italy is famous for pasta, made from durum wheat, in hundreds of shapes, from spaghetti and penne to ravioli and lasagna. A popular legend says Marco Polo brought noodles from China to Italy, but historians have shown that pasta was known in Italy before his return in 1295; Arab traders may have helped bring dried pasta to Sicily."]],
k:[["Oldest known","About 4,000 years old, Lajia, China"],["Instant noodles invented","1958, Japan"],["Instant noodle servings","More than 100 billion a year"],["Filipino noodles","Pancit"],["Main ingredients","Wheat, rice, buckwheat, starch"]],
d:[["c. 2000 BCE","Millet noodles are left in a bowl at Lajia, China"],["c. 200 BCE–200 CE","Wheat noodles spread in Han dynasty China"],["1100s","Dried pasta is made in Sicily"],["1958","Momofuku Ando invents instant noodles"],["1971","Cup noodles are introduced"],["2005","The ancient Lajia noodles are reported by scientists"]],
s2:[["Instant noodles","In 1958 Momofuku Ando, working in a shed in Osaka, Japan, invented instant noodles by flash-frying cooked noodles so they could be prepared quickly with hot water. His Chicken Ramen was a hit, and in 1971 he introduced Cup Noodles. Today instant noodles are eaten around the world, especially in China, Indonesia, India, Vietnam, Japan and the Philippines."],["Ramen","Ramen, wheat noodles in a rich broth with toppings such as pork, egg and seaweed, came to Japan from China and became hugely popular in the 1900s. Each region of Japan has its own style, such as tonkotsu pork bone broth in Fukuoka and miso ramen in Sapporo. Ramen shops are now found around the world."],["Noodles in Southeast Asia","Vietnamese phở is a fragrant soup of rice noodles and beef or chicken. Thai pad thai is stir-fried rice noodles with tamarind, egg and peanuts. Malaysia and Singapore have laksa, noodles in spicy coconut curry broth, and char kway teow. Indonesia’s mie goreng is fried noodles with sweet soy sauce."],["Hand-pulled noodles","Chinese lamian is made by stretching and folding dough repeatedly until it forms dozens or even thousands of thin strands. Skilled cooks in Lanzhou, famous for its beef noodle soup, can pull noodles of different thicknesses in seconds. Watching a noodle puller at work is a popular show."],["Noodles and long life","In China and other parts of Asia, long noodles symbolize long life, so they are eaten at birthdays and New Year. Cutting them is considered bad luck. In the Philippines, the same tradition makes pancit a must at birthday parties."],["Healthy eating","Noodles provide energy from carbohydrates. Adding vegetables, eggs, tofu, fish or meat makes a balanced meal. Instant noodles are convenient but often high in salt and fat, so nutritionists recommend eating them in moderation and adding fresh ingredients."]],
sp:["Momofuku Ando","Momofuku Ando, born in 1910 in Taiwan when it was under Japanese rule, was troubled by the long lines of hungry people waiting for noodle soup in postwar Japan. After a year of experiments in a backyard shed, he found that frying noodles in hot oil dried them and left tiny holes that let them soften quickly in hot water. He launched Chicken Ramen in 1958. A museum about instant noodles in Osaka and Yokohama honors his invention."],
g:[["Noodle","A long strip of dough cooked in water or broth"],["Pancit","Filipino noodle dishes"],["Durum wheat","A hard wheat used for pasta"],["Lamian","Chinese hand-pulled noodles"]],
f:["The oldest noodles are about 4,000 years old.","Instant noodles were invented in 1958.","Long noodles symbolize long life."],
fun:"The world eats more than 100 billion servings of instant noodles every year.",
tl:"Ang noodles ay mahahabang piraso ng masa na kinakain sa sabaw, gisado at iba pa. Ang pinakamatandang noodles ay natagpuan sa Tsina, mga 4,000 taon na ang edad. Sa Pilipinas, ang pancit ay simbolo ng mahabang buhay."});

ADDART({id:"badminton",t:"Badminton",c:"Sports",i:"🏸",w:"Badminton",r:["olymp","tennis","volley","phsport","indonesiacountry"],
p:[["Racquets and a shuttlecock","Badminton is a sport in which players hit a feathered shuttlecock over a high net with light racquets."],["Fastest racquet sport","A smash can send the shuttlecock flying faster than in any other racquet sport."],["Loved in Asia","Badminton is hugely popular in China, Indonesia, Malaysia, India, Japan, Korea and the Philippines."]],
l:"Badminton is a racquet sport played by two players, singles, or two pairs, doubles, on a court divided by a net 1.55 meters high. Players use light racquets to hit a shuttlecock, a cone of feathers or plastic attached to a rounded cork base, back and forth without letting it touch the ground. The modern game developed in England and India in the 1800s and became an Olympic sport in 1992. It is one of the world’s most widely played sports, especially in Asia, and is enjoyed both casually and at the highest professional level.",
s:[["Origins","Games of hitting a shuttlecock with a paddle, such as battledore and shuttlecock, were played in China, Japan, India, Greece and Europe for centuries. British army officers in India played a version with a net in the 1860s, sometimes called Poona. In 1873 the game was played at Badminton House, the estate of the Duke of Beaufort in England, giving the sport its name."],["How it is played","Players score a point when the shuttlecock lands in the opponent’s court or the opponent hits it out or into the net. Since 2006, matches have been played as the best of three games, each to 21 points, with a point scored on every rally. Players use clears, drops, drives, net shots and powerful smashes."],["The shuttlecock","A top-level shuttlecock has 16 goose or duck feathers fixed in a cork base and weighs about 5 grams. Its shape makes it slow down quickly in the air, so it travels fast off the racquet but then drops steeply. This makes badminton a game of speed, reflexes and deception."],["A global sport","The Badminton World Federation, founded in 1934, governs the sport. Major team competitions include the Thomas Cup for men, first held in 1949, the Uber Cup for women and the mixed Sudirman Cup. China, Indonesia, Denmark, Japan, South Korea, Malaysia and India have produced many champions."]],
k:[["Net height","1.55 m at the center"],["Olympic sport since","1992"],["Governing body","Badminton World Federation, 1934"],["Fastest recorded smash","565 km/h (2023)"],["Game length","Best of three games to 21 points"]],
d:[["1860s","British officers play a form of badminton in India"],["1873","The game is played at Badminton House, England"],["1934","The International Badminton Federation is founded"],["1949","The first Thomas Cup is held"],["1992","Badminton becomes an Olympic sport in Barcelona"],["2006","The 21-point rally scoring system is adopted"]],
s2:[["Speed of the smash","The badminton smash is the fastest shot in any racquet sport. In 2023 the Indian doubles player Satwiksairaj Rankireddy set a Guinness World Record with a smash measured at 565 km/h in a speed test. In matches, top players regularly hit smashes over 400 km/h, although the shuttlecock slows dramatically before reaching the other side."],["Indonesia’s first Olympic gold","At the 1992 Barcelona Olympics, the first to include badminton, Susi Susanti won the women’s singles and Alan Budikusuma the men’s singles, giving Indonesia its first Olympic gold medals. The two champions later married. Badminton remains Indonesia’s most successful Olympic sport."],["Legends of the game","China’s Lin Dan won Olympic gold in men’s singles in 2008 and 2012 and five world titles. Malaysia’s Lee Chong Wei won three Olympic silver medals and was ranked world number one for many years. Their rivalry thrilled fans across Asia. Spain’s Carolina Marín, India’s P. V. Sindhu and Japan’s Akane Yamaguchi have been among the stars of women’s badminton."],["Badminton in the Philippines","Badminton is one of the most popular recreational sports in the Philippines, played in schools, gyms and barangay courts. The Philippines competes in Southeast Asian Games badminton, and young players train with hopes of reaching international competitions."],["Fitness and skills","Badminton is an excellent workout, building speed, agility, endurance and hand-eye coordination. Players may run several kilometers during a long match, constantly lunging, jumping and changing direction. It can be enjoyed at any age, from children in the backyard to older adults in clubs."],["Para-badminton","Para-badminton, for players with physical impairments, made its debut at the Tokyo 2020 Paralympic Games, held in 2021. Players compete in wheelchair and standing classes. The sport has grown quickly, with players from many countries."]],
sp:["Why is it called badminton?","The game takes its name from Badminton House in Gloucestershire, England, the country estate of the Duke of Beaufort. In 1873 guests there played a version of the game brought back by officers from India, and it became known as the Badminton game. The first official rules were drawn up in 1877, and the Badminton Association of England was founded in 1893."],
g:[["Shuttlecock","The feathered projectile hit in badminton"],["Smash","A powerful downward shot"],["Rally","An exchange of shots until a point is won"],["Doubles","A match between two pairs of players"]],
f:["Badminton became an Olympic sport in 1992.","A shuttlecock has 16 feathers.","The badminton smash is the fastest shot in racquet sports."],
fun:"A badminton smash has been measured at 565 km/h.",
tl:"Ang badminton ay isport kung saan pinapalo ng mga manlalaro ang shuttlecock na may balahibo sa ibabaw ng mataas na net. Ito ang pinakamabilis na racquet sport. Napakapopular ito sa Asya, kasama ang Pilipinas."});

ADDART({id:"festivals",t:"Festivals Around the World",c:"Religion and Philosophy",i:"🎆",w:"Festival",r:["phfest","relig","hindu","islam","christ"],
p:[["Times to celebrate","Festivals are special days or seasons when communities celebrate together."],["Many reasons","People celebrate harvests, new years, religious events, seasons and history."],["Lights, food and music","Festivals bring lights, feasts, music, dancing, costumes and gift-giving."]],
l:"Festivals are occasions when communities come together to celebrate, remember or give thanks. Many are religious, such as Christmas, Eid al-Fitr, Diwali, Hanukkah and Vesak; others mark the new year, the seasons, harvests or historical events. Festivals often involve special foods, music, dance, decorations, prayers, parades and time with family. They help people pass on traditions and share their culture. Some, such as the Day of the Dead in Mexico and the Lunar New Year, are recognized by UNESCO as important cultural heritage.",
s:[["New year festivals","Different cultures celebrate the new year at different times. Lunar New Year, or Spring Festival, is celebrated in China, Korea, Vietnam and many communities worldwide with family reunions, red decorations, lanterns, dragon and lion dances and gifts of money in red envelopes. Nowruz, the Persian New Year, marks the spring equinox. Thailand’s Songkran is celebrated with water splashing."],["Festivals of light","Diwali, the Hindu, Sikh and Jain festival of lights, celebrates the victory of light over darkness with oil lamps, fireworks, sweets and prayers. Hanukkah, the Jewish festival of lights, lasts eight nights, with a candle lit each night. Christmas, celebrating the birth of Jesus, is marked with lights, carols and gifts; in the Philippines, the season begins in September."],["Religious festivals","Muslims celebrate Eid al-Fitr at the end of Ramadan, the month of fasting, with prayers, feasts and charity, and Eid al-Adha at the time of the Hajj pilgrimage. Buddhists celebrate Vesak, marking the birth, enlightenment and death of the Buddha. Easter is the most important Christian festival, celebrating the resurrection of Jesus."],["Harvest festivals","Many festivals give thanks for the harvest. Korea’s Chuseok and China’s Mid-Autumn Festival, with mooncakes and lanterns, take place under the full moon of autumn. The United States and Canada celebrate Thanksgiving. Pongal in southern India honors the sun and the rice harvest. In the Philippines, the Pahiyas festival in Lucban decorates houses with colorful rice wafers."]],
k:[["Lunar New Year","Late January to mid-February"],["Diwali","October or November"],["Eid al-Fitr","End of Ramadan"],["Day of the Dead","1–2 November"],["UNESCO-listed examples","Day of the Dead, Spring Festival, Songkran, Nowruz"]],
d:[["c. 2000 BCE","Babylonians celebrate a spring new year festival, Akitu"],["1810","The first Oktoberfest is held in Munich"],["2003","UNESCO adopts its convention on intangible cultural heritage"],["2008","Mexico’s Day of the Dead is recognized by UNESCO"],["2023","Thailand’s Songkran is recognized by UNESCO"],["2024","China’s Spring Festival is recognized by UNESCO"]],
s2:[["Day of the Dead","In Mexico, the Día de los Muertos, on 1 and 2 November, honors family members who have died. Families build altars called ofrendas with photographs, marigold flowers, candles, sugar skulls and the favorite foods of their loved ones, and visit cemeteries. It is a joyful celebration of memory rather than a sad occasion. In the Philippines, families similarly visit graves on Undas, All Saints’ Day."],["Holi","Holi, the Hindu festival of colors, celebrates the arrival of spring and the victory of good over evil. People throw colored powders and water at each other, sing, dance and share sweets. Celebrated mainly in India and Nepal, Holi is now enjoyed in many countries."],["Carnival","Carnival is a festive season before Lent, the Christian period of fasting before Easter. Rio de Janeiro’s Carnival in Brazil is world-famous for its samba schools, which parade with dancers, drummers and spectacular floats. Carnivals are also celebrated in Venice with masks, in Trinidad and Tobago with steel bands and in New Orleans as Mardi Gras."],["Festivals of the Philippines","The Philippines has a festival for almost every town, often honoring a patron saint. The Sinulog in Cebu, Ati-Atihan in Kalibo and Dinagyang in Iloilo honor the Santo Niño with street dancing. The Panagbenga in Baguio celebrates flowers, and the MassKara in Bacolod features smiling masks. Filipino Christmas, with parols and Simbang Gabi, is one of the longest in the world."],["Cherry blossoms and spring","In Japan, hanami, flower viewing, is the custom of gathering under blooming cherry trees in spring for picnics and parties. Weather forecasters track the cherry blossom front as it moves north. Similar spring festivals celebrate flowers in Korea, China, the Netherlands and the United States."],["Why festivals matter","Festivals give rhythm to the year, strengthen family and community ties and pass traditions from one generation to the next. They also attract visitors and support local economies. Respecting the meaning of other people’s festivals, and sharing in them when invited, is a way to learn about different cultures."]],
sp:["Lunar New Year","Lunar New Year falls on the second new moon after the winter solstice, between late January and mid-February. In China, hundreds of millions of people travel home for family reunion dinners, the largest annual human migration on Earth. Homes are cleaned to sweep away bad luck, doors are decorated with red paper couplets and children receive red envelopes with money. Each year is associated with one of 12 animals of the zodiac. Chinese Filipinos celebrate it too, and it is a public holiday in the Philippines."],
g:[["Festival","A day or period of celebration"],["Lunar calendar","A calendar based on the cycles of the Moon"],["Ofrenda","An altar for the dead in the Day of the Dead"],["Patron saint","A saint believed to protect a place or group"]],
f:["Diwali is a festival of lights.","The Day of the Dead is celebrated on 1 and 2 November.","Lunar New Year sees the largest annual human migration."],
fun:"Christmas in the Philippines starts in September, making it one of the longest holiday seasons in the world.",
tl:"Ang pista o festival ay mga espesyal na araw ng pagdiriwang ng komunidad. Ipinagdiriwang ang ani, bagong taon, relihiyosong okasyon at kasaysayan. Kabilang dito ang Lunar New Year, Diwali, Eid, Pasko at ang Sinulog."});

