// Animal Kingdom articles, part 8: bird migration, hornbills, salamanders, the tuatara, classification and extinction.
ADDART({id:"birdmigr",t:"Bird Migration",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🕊️",w:"Bird migration",r:["birds","eagle","wetlands","penguin","phbio"],
p:[["Great journeys","Many birds fly long distances each year between breeding grounds and wintering grounds."],["Natural compasses","Birds navigate using the Sun, stars, Earth’s magnetic field, landmarks and even smell."],["The Philippines on the route","The Philippines lies on a major flyway used by millions of migrating shorebirds and waterbirds."]],
l:"Bird migration is the regular seasonal movement of birds, often between northern breeding grounds in summer and warmer wintering areas. Nearly 2,000 bird species migrate, some traveling thousands of kilometers each way. Migration lets birds take advantage of seasonal food and safe nesting places. Birds prepare by eating heavily and storing fat, then fly by day or night, finding their way with remarkable navigation skills. Many migratory birds are declining because of habitat loss, so countries along their routes cooperate to protect them.",
s:[["Why birds migrate","In spring, the far north offers long days and an explosion of insects and plants, ideal for raising chicks. In autumn, as food disappears and cold sets in, birds fly to warmer places. Migration takes great energy and risk, but the rewards of plentiful food and fewer predators make it worthwhile for many species."],["Champions of migration","The Arctic tern makes the longest known migration, traveling from the Arctic to the Antarctic and back each year, a round trip of 70,000 kilometers or more. In 2022 a young bar-tailed godwit flew nonstop from Alaska to Tasmania, about 13,500 kilometers, in 11 days without landing, eating or drinking."],["Finding the way","Birds use several compasses. They follow the position of the Sun by day and the stars by night, and they can sense Earth’s magnetic field, possibly through special molecules in their eyes. They also remember landmarks such as coastlines, rivers and mountains, and some seabirds use smell. Young birds of some species migrate alone, guided by instinct."],["Flyways","Migrating birds follow broad routes called flyways. The East Asian–Australasian Flyway runs from Siberia and Alaska through China, Korea, Japan and Southeast Asia, including the Philippines, to Australia and New Zealand. Tens of millions of shorebirds use it, resting and feeding at wetlands and mudflats along the way."]],
k:[["Migratory species","Nearly 2,000"],["Longest migration","Arctic tern, 70,000 km or more a year"],["Longest nonstop flight","Bar-tailed godwit, about 13,500 km"],["Navigation","Sun, stars, magnetic field, landmarks"],["World Migratory Bird Day","Second Saturday of May and October"]],
d:[["c. 350 BCE","Aristotle writes about birds disappearing in winter"],["1822","A stork with an African arrow in its neck is found in Germany"],["1899","Hans Christian Mortensen begins ringing birds to track them"],["1979","The Bonn Convention on migratory species is signed"],["1994","Olango Island becomes the Philippines’ first Ramsar wetland site"],["2022","A bar-tailed godwit sets a nonstop flight record"]],
s2:[["The arrow stork","People once had strange ideas about where birds went in winter; some believed swallows slept underwater. In 1822 a white stork was shot in Germany with an 80-centimeter African spear through its neck. It had survived the wound and flown back from Africa, proving that storks travel there. Germans call such birds Pfeilstörche, arrow storks."],["Tracking birds","In 1899 the Danish teacher Hans Christian Mortensen began fitting birds with numbered metal rings to learn where they traveled. Today scientists use tiny satellite tags, GPS trackers and geolocators weighing less than a gram, as well as radar and microphones that record birds calling as they fly over at night."],["Migrants in the Philippines","Each year, migratory birds such as plovers, sandpipers, egrets, herons and ducks arrive in the Philippines from northern Asia, mostly from about September to April. Olango Island in Cebu, Candaba Swamp in Pampanga and the coasts of Manila Bay are important resting and feeding sites. The Philippines has protected several wetlands under the international Ramsar Convention."],["Dangers on the journey","Migrating birds face storms, predators, hunting and exhaustion. Many die colliding with lit-up buildings and windows at night, so some cities dim their lights during migration seasons. The loss of coastal wetlands, such as the mudflats of the Yellow Sea, where shorebirds refuel, has caused steep declines in some species."],["Getting ready","Before migrating, many birds enter a state of restlessness and eat huge amounts, sometimes doubling their body weight with fat for fuel. Some shrink their digestive organs during flight to save weight. Bar-headed geese fly over the Himalayas at great altitudes where oxygen is scarce, helped by special hemoglobin in their blood."],["Not only birds","Many other animals migrate, including whales, sea turtles, salmon, wildebeest, monarch butterflies and dragonflies. But birds make some of the most spectacular and widespread journeys, connecting countries and continents."]],
sp:["The godwit’s record","On 13 October 2022, a five-month-old bar-tailed godwit fitted with a satellite tag took off from Alaska and flew without stopping for 11 days, landing in Tasmania after about 13,560 kilometers. It never landed to rest, feed or drink, flapping its wings the whole way and using winds to help. Before leaving, godwits fatten up so much that fat makes up more than half their body weight."],
g:[["Migration","Regular seasonal movement from one place to another"],["Flyway","A broad route used by migrating birds"],["Ringing","Putting a numbered band on a bird’s leg to track it"],["Ramsar site","A wetland protected under an international treaty"]],
f:["The Arctic tern flies from the Arctic to the Antarctic each year.","Birds can sense Earth’s magnetic field.","The Philippines lies on the East Asian–Australasian Flyway."],
fun:"A young bar-tailed godwit flew nonstop from Alaska to Tasmania in 11 days.",
tl:"Ang migrasyon ng ibon ay taunang paglalakbay nila sa pagitan ng pugaran at taglamigang lugar. Ginagamit nila ang araw, bituin at magnetic field ng Earth para hanapin ang daan. Dumadaan sa Pilipinas ang milyon-milyong migratoryong ibon."});

ADDART({id:"hornbill",t:"Hornbills",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦜",w:"Hornbill",r:["birds","trees","phbio","borneo","eagle"],
p:[["Big beaks","Hornbills are birds with huge curved bills, often topped by a hollow helmet called a casque."],["Walled-in nests","Many female hornbills seal themselves inside tree holes while nesting, fed by their mates."],["Forest gardeners","By eating fruit and spreading seeds, hornbills help forests grow."]],
l:"Hornbills are a family of about 60 bird species found in the tropical forests and savannas of Africa and Asia, including the Philippines. They are known for their long, downcurved bills, often brightly colored and topped with a casque. Hornbills eat fruit, insects and small animals, and they play a vital role in spreading the seeds of forest trees. Many species have unusual nesting habits, with the female walling herself into a tree hole. Because they need large old trees, many hornbills are threatened by deforestation and hunting.",
s:[["The bill and casque","Despite its size, a hornbill’s bill is light, made of keratin over a honeycomb of bone. The casque on top is usually hollow and may help amplify calls or display maturity. The helmeted hornbill is unusual: its casque is solid, like ivory. Hornbills are also the only birds whose first two neck vertebrae are fused, perhaps to support the heavy bill."],["Sealed in the nest","In most hornbill species, the female enters a hole in a tree and seals the entrance with mud, droppings and food, leaving only a narrow slit. The male feeds her and later the chicks through the slit, sometimes for months. Inside, the female molts her flight feathers. This protects the nest from snakes, monkeys and other predators."],["Farmers of the forest","Hornbills eat large amounts of fruit, especially figs, and fly long distances, dropping seeds far from the parent tree. Some trees depend on hornbills to spread their seeds. Because of this, hornbills are sometimes called farmers of the forest, and their disappearance can change which trees grow."],["Hornbills of the Philippines","The Philippines has about ten hornbill species, most found nowhere else. They include the large rufous hornbill, called kalaw for its loud call, the Visayan hornbill, the Mindanao wrinkled hornbill and the Sulu hornbill, one of the world’s rarest birds, surviving only in tiny numbers on Tawi-Tawi. Most Philippine hornbills are threatened by forest loss and hunting."]],
k:[["Species","About 60"],["Found in","Africa and Asia"],["Special feature","Large bill with casque"],["Philippine species","About 10, mostly endemic"],["Critically endangered example","Helmeted hornbill, Sulu hornbill"]],
d:[["1758","Linnaeus names the first hornbill species"],["1978","Thai scientists begin a long-term hornbill research and protection project"],["2001","The Philippines passes its Wildlife Resources Conservation and Protection Act"],["2015","The helmeted hornbill is listed as critically endangered"],["2016","International trade in helmeted hornbill casques gets stronger protection"],["2020s","Conservationists count fewer than about 40 Sulu hornbills"]],
s2:[["The helmeted hornbill","The helmeted hornbill of Borneo, Sumatra and the Malay Peninsula has a solid casque, sometimes called red ivory, which is carved into ornaments. Poaching for this trade caused a sharp decline, and in 2015 the species was listed as critically endangered. Its call, a series of hoots ending in a maniacal laugh, can be heard far through the forest."],["The great hornbill","The great hornbill, found from India to Southeast Asia, is one of the largest hornbills, with a wingspan of about 1.5 meters and a bright yellow casque. Its wingbeats make a loud whooshing sound. It is the state bird of Kerala and Arunachal Pradesh in India, and many Indigenous peoples consider hornbills sacred."],["Ground hornbills","Africa’s ground hornbills are large black birds with red throat skin that walk on the savanna hunting insects, snakes and small mammals. Southern ground hornbills live in groups that help raise one chick at a time and can live for decades. They are considered sacred or bringers of rain by some African peoples."],["Hornbills in culture","In Borneo, the rhinoceros hornbill is a symbol of the Dayak peoples, and the Malaysian state of Sarawak is called the Land of the Hornbills. Hornbill feathers and carvings are used in ceremonies. In the film The Lion King, the character Zazu is a red-billed hornbill."],["Protecting hornbills","Conservationists protect nesting trees, install artificial nest boxes, patrol forests against poachers and work with local communities. In Thailand and India, villagers are paid to guard hornbill nests. In the Philippines, breeding centers and forest protection programs help species such as the Visayan hornbill."],["A loving family","Many hornbill pairs stay together for years. During nesting, the male may bring hundreds of fruits a day to his sealed-in mate and chicks, regurgitating them one by one through the slit. If the male dies, the female and chicks may starve, which is one reason hunting adult hornbills is so harmful."]],
sp:["The Sulu hornbill","The Sulu hornbill is one of the rarest birds on Earth. Once found on several islands of the Sulu Archipelago, it now survives only in the remaining forests of Tawi-Tawi, where surveys have counted fewer than about 40 birds. It is black with a white tail, and males have pale bills. Logging and hunting have pushed it to the edge of extinction, and local conservationists are working to protect its last forests."],
g:[["Casque","A helmet-like growth on top of a hornbill’s bill"],["Seed disperser","An animal that spreads plant seeds"],["Endemic","Found naturally in only one place"],["Critically endangered","At extremely high risk of extinction"]],
f:["Female hornbills seal themselves into tree holes.","The Philippines has about ten hornbill species.","Hornbills spread the seeds of forest trees."],
fun:"Zazu in The Lion King is a red-billed hornbill.",
tl:"Ang hornbill o kalaw ay ibong may malaki at kurbadong tuka, na kadalasang may casque sa ibabaw. Ikinukulong ng babaeng hornbill ang sarili sa butas ng puno habang nangingitlog. Mahalaga sila sa pagkalat ng buto ng mga puno sa gubat."});

ADDART({id:"salamander",t:"Salamanders and Newts",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦎",w:"Salamander",r:["frog","lizard","evo","cell","wetlands"],
p:[["Amphibians with tails","Salamanders are amphibians with long bodies, tails and usually four short legs."],["Masters of regrowth","Many salamanders can regrow lost legs, tails and even parts of their hearts and eyes."],["Moist places","Most live in damp forests, streams and ponds, and many breathe through their skin."]],
l:"Salamanders are a group of about 800 species of amphibians with slender bodies, long tails and moist skin. Newts are a kind of salamander. Although they look somewhat like lizards, salamanders are amphibians, related to frogs, and have no scales. They live mainly in the Northern Hemisphere, especially in the Americas, in damp forests, streams, caves and ponds. Salamanders are famous for their ability to regenerate lost body parts, and they range from tiny species a few centimeters long to giant salamanders nearly 2 meters long.",
s:[["Salamander life","Most salamanders lay eggs in water or damp places. Many have aquatic larvae with feathery external gills, which later change into adults that live on land. Salamanders eat insects, worms, snails and other small animals, catching them with sticky tongues. They are mostly active at night or in wet weather."],["Breathing through skin","The largest family of salamanders, the lungless salamanders, has no lungs at all. They breathe entirely through their thin, moist skin and the lining of their mouths. This is why they must stay damp. Most salamanders also absorb some oxygen through their skin, making them sensitive to pollution."],["Regeneration","Salamanders can regrow lost limbs, tails, parts of the spinal cord, heart, eyes and jaws. When a limb is lost, cells at the wound form a bud that grows into a perfect new limb, with bones, muscles and nerves. Scientists study salamanders, especially axolotls, hoping to learn how to help humans heal injuries."],["Where they live","Salamanders are most diverse in North and Central America; the Appalachian Mountains of the United States are a world hotspot. They also live in Europe, northern Africa and Asia, including China, Japan and Vietnam. None are native to Australia, and they are absent from most of Africa and from tropical islands such as the Philippines."]],
k:[["Species","About 800"],["Group","Amphibians"],["Largest","Chinese giant salamanders, up to about 1.8 m"],["Largest family","Lungless salamanders"],["Special ability","Regrowing body parts"]],
d:[["c. 160 million years ago","Salamander fossils show the group was already present"],["1689","The olm, a cave salamander, is first written about in Slovenia"],["1864","Axolotls are brought from Mexico to Paris and studied"],["2013","A new fungus deadly to salamanders is identified in Europe"],["2018","The axolotl’s genome, one of the largest known, is sequenced"],["2019","Scientists suggest Chinese giant salamanders are several species"]],
s2:[["The axolotl","The axolotl, from lakes near Mexico City, keeps its gills and lives in water its whole life, a trait called neoteny, as if staying a larva forever. It can regenerate almost any body part. Wild axolotls are critically endangered, surviving mainly in the canals of Xochimilco, though millions are kept in laboratories and as pets. It is a cultural symbol of Mexico City."],["Giant salamanders","Chinese giant salamanders are the world’s largest amphibians, growing up to about 1.8 meters long. They live in cold mountain streams, breathing through wrinkly skin. Overhunting for food has made them critically endangered. Japan has its own giant salamander, and North America has the hellbender, up to about 70 centimeters long."],["The olm","The olm lives in flooded caves in Slovenia, Croatia and nearby countries. It is blind, with pale pink skin, and can sense light, smells and electric fields. Olms are thought to live for about 100 years and may go years without eating. Local people once believed they were baby dragons washed out of caves by floods."],["Newts","Newts are salamanders that spend much of their adult life in water, especially during breeding. Male newts of some species grow wavy crests and bright colors in spring to attract females. Some newts, like the rough-skinned newt of North America, contain a powerful poison in their skin."],["Fire salamanders","The fire salamander of Europe is black with bright yellow spots, a warning that its skin produces toxins. People once believed salamanders could live in fire, perhaps because they crawled out of logs thrown on fires. This myth gave the salamander a place in legends and heraldry."],["Threats","Salamanders are threatened by habitat loss, pollution, climate change and disease. A fungus called Batrachochytrium salamandrivorans, identified in 2013, has wiped out fire salamander populations in parts of Europe. Scientists are working to stop it from spreading to North America, the world’s salamander hotspot."]],
sp:["The giant genome","The axolotl has one of the largest genomes of any animal, about ten times larger than the human genome. In 2018 scientists finished sequencing it, a major challenge because of its size and repetitive DNA. They hope to find the genes that allow axolotls to regrow limbs perfectly, which might one day help medicine heal wounds and injuries in people."],
g:[["Amphibian","A cold-blooded animal that usually begins life in water"],["Regeneration","Regrowing lost body parts"],["Neoteny","Keeping juvenile features as an adult"],["Larva","An early, immature stage of an animal"]],
f:["Salamanders are amphibians, not lizards.","Some salamanders have no lungs.","Axolotls can regrow lost limbs."],
fun:"The olm, a blind cave salamander, may live for about 100 years.",
tl:"Ang salamander ay amphibian na may mahabang katawan at buntot. Kaya ng marami sa kanila na muling patubuin ang nawalang paa at buntot. Walang katutubong salamander sa Pilipinas."});

ADDART({id:"tuatara",t:"The Tuatara",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦖",w:"Tuatara",r:["lizard","nzcountry","dino","evo","chameleon"],
p:[["A living relic","The tuatara of New Zealand is the last survivor of a group of reptiles that lived alongside the early dinosaurs."],["Not a lizard","Although it looks like a lizard, it belongs to its own ancient order of reptiles."],["Slow and long-lived","Tuatara grow slowly and can live for more than 100 years."]],
l:"The tuatara is a reptile found only in New Zealand. It looks like a lizard but is the only living member of an ancient order called Rhynchocephalia, whose relatives flourished more than 200 million years ago. Tuatara grow up to about 80 centimeters long, are active at cool temperatures and can live for over a century. They nearly disappeared from mainland New Zealand after people brought rats, but survived on offshore islands and are now being returned to protected sanctuaries.",
s:[["An ancient lineage","The tuatara’s ancestors appeared about 240 million years ago, in the Triassic period, and were widespread around the world. All died out except the tuatara, which survived in New Zealand. Its body shows features unlike those of lizards, such as the way its teeth are fused to its jawbone and the structure of its skull."],["The third eye","Young tuatara have a parietal eye, sometimes called a third eye, on top of the head, with a lens and retina. It becomes covered by scales as the animal grows. Scientists think it may help regulate daily and seasonal rhythms by sensing light. Some lizards and frogs also have a parietal eye."],["Life in the cold","Most reptiles need warm temperatures, but tuatara are active at temperatures as low as about 5°C. They come out at night to hunt beetles, wētā insects, spiders, and sometimes small lizards and seabird chicks. They often share burrows with nesting seabirds such as petrels."],["Slow life","Tuatara grow very slowly, not reaching maturity until 10 to 20 years of age. Females lay eggs only every few years, and the eggs take 11 to 16 months to hatch, among the longest of any reptile. The temperature of the nest decides whether hatchlings are male or female: warmer nests produce more males."]],
k:[["Found in","New Zealand only"],["Order","Rhynchocephalia, the only living member"],["Length","Up to about 80 cm"],["Lifespan","Can exceed 100 years"],["Egg incubation","11–16 months"]],
d:[["c. 240 million years ago","The tuatara’s ancestors appear"],["c. 1250–1300","Polynesian settlers, with Pacific rats, arrive in New Zealand"],["1831","The tuatara is first described by scientists"],["1895","Tuatara receive legal protection"],["2005","Tuatara are released into the Zealandia sanctuary in Wellington"],["2008","The first wild-born tuatara on the mainland in about 200 years is found"]],
s2:[["Henry the tuatara","Henry, a tuatara at the Southland Museum in Invercargill, New Zealand, became famous for becoming a father at about the age of 111 in 2009, after surgery to remove a tumor seemed to improve his mood. He had lived at the museum since the 1970s and showed little interest in mating for decades."],["Saved on islands","When Polynesian settlers and later Europeans arrived in New Zealand, they brought rats, cats and other predators that ate tuatara eggs and young. By the 1900s, tuatara survived only on about 30 offshore islands. Conservationists removed rats from many islands and bred tuatara in captivity, helping populations recover."],["Back to the mainland","In 2005, 70 tuatara were released into Zealandia, a fenced, predator-free sanctuary in Wellington. In 2008 rangers found a wild-born hatchling there, the first on the mainland in about 200 years. More sanctuaries have since received tuatara."],["A treasure of the Māori","For Māori, the Indigenous people of New Zealand, the tuatara is a taonga, a treasure, and is linked to Tangaroa and other ancestral figures. Its name means peaks on the back in the Māori language, describing its spiny crest. Iwi, tribes, are kaitiaki, guardians, of tuatara, and work with scientists on their protection."],["Climate risk","Because nest temperature decides the sex of tuatara, climate change is a concern: warmer temperatures could produce too many males. On North Brother Island, males already outnumber females. Scientists are studying ways to help, such as shading nests or moving populations to cooler places."],["Tuatara and evolution","Studies of the tuatara’s genome, published in 2020, revealed that it has an unusual mix of reptile and even mammal-like features. Scientists study tuatara to learn how reptiles evolved and how such an ancient lineage survived for so long."]],
sp:["Peaks on the back","The word tuatara comes from the Māori language: tua means back, and tara means spines or peaks, describing the crest of spines along the animal’s back, especially large in males. When males fight or court females, they raise their crests and puff up their bodies to look bigger. The crest has made the tuatara one of New Zealand’s most recognizable animals, featured on coins and stamps."],
g:[["Rhynchocephalia","An ancient order of reptiles with one living member"],["Parietal eye","A light-sensitive spot on top of the head"],["Predator-free","Without introduced animals that eat native wildlife"],["Taonga","A Māori word for a treasure"]],
f:["The tuatara is not a lizard.","Tuatara can live for over 100 years.","Nest temperature decides whether tuatara hatchlings are male or female."],
fun:"A tuatara named Henry became a father at about 111 years old.",
tl:"Ang tuatara ay reptilyang matatagpuan lamang sa New Zealand. Mukha itong butiki ngunit ito na lamang ang natitirang miyembro ng sinaunang grupo ng mga reptilya. Maaari itong mabuhay nang higit 100 taon."});

ADDART({id:"classification",t:"Classifying Living Things",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🏷️",w:"Taxonomy (biology)",r:["evo","cell","dna","microbe","mendel"],
p:[["Sorting life","Scientists sort living things into groups based on how they are related."],["Two-part names","Every species has a two-part scientific name, such as Homo sapiens for humans."],["Millions of species","About 2 million species have been named, but millions more await discovery."]],
l:"Classification, or taxonomy, is the science of naming, describing and grouping living things. It helps scientists organize the enormous diversity of life and understand how organisms are related. The modern system was founded by the Swedish naturalist Carl Linnaeus in the 1700s, who gave each species a two-part Latin name and arranged them into a hierarchy of groups. Today scientists use DNA to work out the family tree of life, grouping organisms by shared ancestry. About 2 million species have been described, but estimates of the total range from about 8 million to many more.",
s:[["Linnaeus","Carl Linnaeus, a Swedish botanist, published Systema Naturae in 1735, sorting plants, animals and minerals into groups. In later editions, especially the 10th edition of 1758, he used binomial nomenclature, giving every species a two-part name: a genus name and a species name. Humans became Homo sapiens, meaning wise human."],["The ranks of life","Living things are classified in nested ranks: domain, kingdom, phylum, class, order, family, genus and species. For example, the Philippine eagle belongs to the domain Eukarya, kingdom Animalia, phylum Chordata, class Aves, order Accipitriformes, family Accipitridae, genus Pithecophaga and species Pithecophaga jefferyi."],["Three domains","In the 1970s, the American microbiologist Carl Woese studied genetic material and found that microbes once lumped together as bacteria were really two very different groups. Life is now divided into three domains: Bacteria; Archaea, microbes often found in extreme environments; and Eukarya, organisms with complex cells, including plants, animals, fungi and protists."],["Classifying with DNA","Scientists once grouped organisms mainly by appearance and anatomy. Today they compare DNA to build family trees, called phylogenies. DNA has revealed surprises: whales are close relatives of hippos, birds are living dinosaurs and fungi are more closely related to animals than to plants."]],
k:[["Founder of modern system","Carl Linnaeus"],["Key book","Systema Naturae (1735)"],["Naming system","Binomial nomenclature"],["Named species","About 2 million"],["Three domains","Bacteria, Archaea, Eukarya"]],
d:[["c. 350 BCE","Aristotle groups animals by their features"],["1735","Linnaeus publishes Systema Naturae"],["1758","The 10th edition sets the starting point for animal names"],["1859","Darwin’s On the Origin of Species links classification to evolution"],["1977","Carl Woese identifies the Archaea"],["2011","A study estimates about 8.7 million species of complex life"]],
s2:[["How species get names","When scientists discover a new species, they describe it in a scientific publication, give it a Latin or Latin-style name and deposit a type specimen in a museum as a reference. Names often honor a feature, a place or a person. Rules set by international commissions make sure each species has only one valid name."],["New species every year","Scientists describe thousands of new species every year, mostly insects, plants, fungi and other small organisms, but also frogs, lizards and occasionally mammals. The Philippines, with its many islands, is a hotspot of new discoveries, including new species of frogs, lizards, beetles, plants and even forest mice found on single mountains."],["Fun names","Some species have playful names. A horsefly from Australia was named Scaptia beyonceae after the singer Beyoncé, a beetle Agra katewinsletae after the actress Kate Winslet, and a trapdoor spider Aptostichus barackobamai after a former US president. Such names draw attention to the importance of discovering species."],["The puzzle of the platypus","When the first platypus specimen reached Britain from Australia in 1799, some scientists thought it was a fake, made by sewing a duck’s bill onto a mammal’s body. The platypus, a mammal that lays eggs, challenged the categories scientists used and showed that nature does not always fit neatly into boxes."],["Common names and scientific names","Common names vary from place to place: the bird called a kalaw in the Philippines is the rufous hornbill in English. Scientific names are the same worldwide, so scientists in different countries know exactly which organism they mean. Scientific names are written in italics, with the genus capitalized."],["Why classification matters","Classification helps scientists study diseases, find new medicines, protect endangered species and understand evolution. Knowing that a new plant is related to a known medicinal plant, for example, can suggest where to look for useful chemicals. Biodiversity cannot be protected if species are unknown."]],
sp:["Carl Linnaeus","Carl Linnaeus, born in Sweden in 1707, loved plants from childhood. He traveled through Lapland collecting specimens and became a professor at Uppsala University, where he sent students around the world to collect plants and animals. His system of two-part names was so simple and useful that it is still used today. He was so proud of his work that he reportedly said God created, but Linnaeus organized."],
g:[["Taxonomy","The science of naming and classifying living things"],["Species","A group of organisms that can breed with one another"],["Genus","A group of closely related species"],["Binomial nomenclature","The two-part naming system for species"]],
f:["Humans are called Homo sapiens.","Life is divided into three domains.","About 2 million species have been named."],
fun:"A species of horsefly was named after the singer Beyoncé.",
tl:"Ang klasipikasyon o taxonomy ay agham ng pagpapangalan at pagpapangkat ng mga buhay na bagay. Binigyan ni Carl Linnaeus ang bawat species ng dalawang-bahaging pangalan, gaya ng Homo sapiens para sa tao."});

ADDART({id:"extinction",t:"Extinction",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦤",w:"Extinction",r:["dino","evo","eco","climchange","phbio"],
p:[["Gone forever","Extinction happens when the last individual of a species dies."],["Mass extinctions","Five times in Earth’s history, huge numbers of species died out in a short time."],["Preventing extinction","Today people cause many extinctions, but conservation can save species from disappearing."]],
l:"Extinction is the end of a species, when its last member dies. It is a natural part of evolution: about 99 percent of all species that have ever lived are now extinct. Five times in Earth’s history, mass extinctions wiped out a large share of life, including the event that ended the age of dinosaurs. Today human activities, such as habitat destruction, hunting, pollution, invasive species and climate change, are causing species to disappear far faster than the natural rate. Conservation efforts aim to protect threatened species and the habitats they need.",
s:[["The five mass extinctions","Scientists recognize five mass extinctions: at the end of the Ordovician, about 445 million years ago; in the Late Devonian; at the end of the Permian, about 252 million years ago; at the end of the Triassic; and at the end of the Cretaceous, 66 million years ago. The end-Permian event, the Great Dying, killed around 90 percent of marine species."],["Causes of extinction","Natural extinctions can result from climate change, volcanic eruptions, asteroid impacts, competition and disease. Today the main causes are human: destroying forests, wetlands and reefs; overhunting and overfishing; introducing invasive species such as rats and cats to islands; pollution; and climate change."],["Lost species","Famous extinct species include the dodo of Mauritius, last reliably seen around 1662; the passenger pigeon, once numbering billions in North America, whose last member, Martha, died in 1914; and the thylacine, or Tasmanian tiger, whose last known individual died in a zoo in 1936. Many plants, insects and small animals have vanished without being noticed."],["The Red List","The International Union for Conservation of Nature keeps the Red List of Threatened Species, which assesses the risk of extinction for tens of thousands of species. Categories range from least concern to vulnerable, endangered, critically endangered, extinct in the wild and extinct. More than 45,000 species are listed as threatened."]],
k:[["Share of species ever lived now extinct","About 99 percent"],["Mass extinctions","Five"],["Worst mass extinction","End-Permian, about 252 million years ago"],["Threatened species (IUCN)","More than 45,000"],["Dodo last seen","About 1662"]],
d:[["c. 252 million years ago","The Great Dying wipes out most life"],["66 million years ago","An asteroid impact ends the age of non-bird dinosaurs"],["c. 1662","The dodo is last reliably seen"],["1914","Martha, the last passenger pigeon, dies"],["1936","The last known thylacine dies"],["1964","The IUCN Red List is established"]],
s2:[["The dodo","The dodo was a large, flightless pigeon found only on the island of Mauritius in the Indian Ocean. It had no natural predators and was unafraid of people. After Dutch sailors arrived in 1598, dodos were hunted, and introduced rats, pigs and monkeys ate their eggs. Within about 70 years they were gone. As dead as a dodo became a saying for something gone forever."],["The passenger pigeon","In the early 1800s, passenger pigeons may have been the most numerous birds in North America, flying in flocks so huge they darkened the sky for hours. Commercial hunting and forest clearing destroyed them within decades. Martha, the last one, died at the Cincinnati Zoo on 1 September 1914. Her story helped inspire wildlife protection laws."],["Lazarus species","Sometimes species thought extinct are found again, called Lazarus species. The coelacanth, a fish known only from fossils and thought extinct for 66 million years, was found alive off South Africa in 1938. In the Philippines, the Cebu flowerpecker, feared extinct, was rediscovered in 1992 in a small patch of forest."],["A sixth mass extinction?","Many scientists warn that the current rate of extinction is far higher than the natural rate and that the world may be entering a sixth mass extinction. A major international report in 2019 estimated that about a million species are threatened with extinction, many within decades, unless action is taken."],["Saving species","Conservation has saved many species. Protecting habitats, banning hunting, breeding animals in captivity and removing invasive predators have helped species such as the California condor, the black-footed ferret, the giant panda and humpback whales, and are helping rare species such as the Philippine crocodile. Protected areas on land and sea are vital."],["Island extinctions","Islands are especially vulnerable because their species often live nowhere else and evolved without predators. The Philippines, with thousands of islands and many unique species, is a global conservation priority. Cebu, for example, lost most of its original forest, and several of its unique birds became extinct or nearly so."]],
sp:["The last passenger pigeon","Martha was named after Martha Washington, the wife of the first US president. She lived at the Cincinnati Zoo, where visitors came to see the last of a species that had once numbered in the billions. When she died in 1914, her body was frozen in a block of ice and sent to the Smithsonian Institution in Washington. Her death became one of the most famous examples of how quickly humans can drive even a very common species to extinction."],
g:[["Extinction","The disappearance of a species"],["Mass extinction","The loss of a large share of species in a short time"],["Red List","A list assessing species’ risk of extinction"],["Lazarus species","A species rediscovered after being thought extinct"]],
f:["About 99 percent of all species that ever lived are extinct.","The dodo lived only on Mauritius.","The coelacanth was rediscovered alive in 1938."],
fun:"The coelacanth, a fish thought extinct for 66 million years, was found alive in 1938.",
tl:"Ang ekstinksiyon ay ang tuluyang pagkawala ng isang species. Limang beses nagkaroon ng malawakang ekstinksiyon sa kasaysayan ng Earth. Ngayon, maraming species ang nawawala dahil sa gawain ng tao, ngunit maaari silang iligtas ng konserbasyon."});

;
/* data/history3.js */
// History articles, part 3: the Phoenicians, the Celts, the Mughal Empire, Srivijaya and Majapahit, the Black Death and the Green Revolution.
ADDART({id:"phoenicians",t:"The Phoenicians",c:"History",i:"⛵",w:"Phoenicia",r:["alphabet","rome","greece","egypt","trade"],
p:[["Sailors and traders","The Phoenicians were seafaring traders from city-states on the coast of what is now Lebanon."],["Letters for the world","Their alphabet became the ancestor of the Greek, Latin, Hebrew and Arabic scripts."],["Royal purple","They were famous for a precious purple dye made from sea snails."]],
l:"The Phoenicians were an ancient people who lived in independent city-states along the eastern Mediterranean coast, mainly in present-day Lebanon, from about 1500 to 300 BCE. Cities such as Tyre, Sidon and Byblos grew rich through trade and seafaring, sailing across the Mediterranean and beyond and founding colonies such as Carthage in North Africa. The Phoenicians spread goods, ideas and above all their alphabet, the ancestor of many of the world’s writing systems, including the letters you are reading now.",
s:[["City-states of the coast","Phoenicia was never a single country but a group of cities, each with its own king, sharing a language and culture. Hemmed in by mountains, they turned to the sea. Their famous cedar forests supplied timber to Egypt and Mesopotamia for ships, temples and palaces."],["Masters of the sea","Phoenician sailors built strong ships and navigated by the stars, including the North Star, which the Greeks called the Phoenician star. They traded across the Mediterranean, reached Spain and the Atlantic coast and may have sailed to Britain for tin. Greek historian Herodotus reported that Phoenicians sailed around Africa for an Egyptian pharaoh around 600 BCE, though some doubt the story."],["The alphabet","Around 1050 BCE, the Phoenicians used an alphabet of 22 letters, each standing for a consonant sound. It was far simpler than Egyptian hieroglyphs or Mesopotamian cuneiform. The Greeks adopted it and added vowels, and it later developed into the Latin alphabet. Aramaic, Hebrew and Arabic scripts also descend from it."],["Purple and glass","The Phoenicians made a rich purple dye from murex sea snails, which took thousands of snails to produce a small amount. Tyrian purple became a symbol of royalty and power in Rome and elsewhere. They were also skilled glassmakers, metalworkers and ivory carvers."]],
k:[["Homeland","Coast of present-day Lebanon"],["Main cities","Tyre, Sidon, Byblos"],["Most famous colony","Carthage"],["Alphabet","22 consonant letters"],["Famous product","Tyrian purple dye"]],
d:[["c. 1500 BCE","Phoenician cities flourish on the coast"],["c. 1050 BCE","The Phoenician alphabet is in use"],["814 BCE","Carthage is founded, according to tradition"],["c. 600 BCE","Phoenicians reportedly sail around Africa"],["332 BCE","Alexander the Great captures Tyre after a long siege"],["146 BCE","Rome destroys Carthage"]],
s2:[["Carthage","According to tradition, Carthage was founded in 814 BCE by settlers from Tyre led by Queen Dido, in present-day Tunisia. It grew into a great trading empire controlling much of the western Mediterranean. Its rivalry with Rome led to the three Punic Wars, ending with Carthage’s destruction in 146 BCE."],["Hannibal","The Carthaginian general Hannibal led an army with war elephants from Spain over the Alps into Italy in 218 BCE, during the Second Punic War. He defeated Roman armies in several battles, including Cannae in 216 BCE, but could not capture Rome. He is remembered as one of history’s greatest military commanders."],["Byblos and the Bible","The city of Byblos exported papyrus from Egypt, and the Greeks named papyrus after it: byblos. From this came biblion, book, and eventually the word Bible. Byblos is one of the oldest continuously inhabited cities in the world."],["The siege of Tyre","Tyre stood on an island just off the coast, making it hard to attack. In 332 BCE Alexander the Great besieged it for seven months, building a causeway out to the island. After capturing it, he destroyed much of the city. Silt built up along the causeway over centuries, turning Tyre into a peninsula."],["Religion","The Phoenicians worshipped gods such as Baal, Astarte and Melqart, the patron god of Tyre. Temples stood in every city. Carthage’s religion was influenced by its Phoenician roots, and ancient writers made claims about its practices that historians still debate."],["Legacy","The Phoenicians linked the peoples of the Mediterranean through trade, spreading goods, technologies and ideas. Their greatest legacy is the alphabet. Modern Lebanon proudly remembers its Phoenician heritage, and archaeological sites such as Byblos and Tyre are World Heritage Sites."]],
sp:["From Phoenician letters to ours","The first letter of the Phoenician alphabet was ʾaleph, meaning ox, drawn like an ox’s head; the second was bet, meaning house. The Greeks turned these into alpha and beta, the source of the word alphabet. Over centuries, the ox-head letter rotated and changed into our letter A. Many of the letters we use today can be traced back through Greek and Latin to Phoenician signs."],
g:[["City-state","An independent city ruling its own territory"],["Alphabet","A writing system with letters for sounds"],["Colony","A settlement founded in a new land"],["Murex","A sea snail used to make purple dye"]],
f:["The word alphabet comes from the first two Greek letters, based on Phoenician ones.","Carthage was a Phoenician colony.","Tyrian purple dye was made from sea snails."],
fun:"The word Bible comes from Byblos, a Phoenician city that traded papyrus.",
tl:"Ang mga Phoenician ay sinaunang mangangalakal at mandaragat mula sa baybayin ng Lebanon ngayon. Ang kanilang alpabeto ang pinagmulan ng alpabetong Griyego, Latin, Hebreo at Arabe. Kilala rin sila sa mamahaling pulang-ube na tina."});

ADDART({id:"celts",t:"The Celts",c:"History",i:"🍀",w:"Celts",r:["rome","greece","viking","langevo","fairy"],
p:[["Peoples of ancient Europe","The Celts were groups of Iron Age peoples across much of Europe who shared related languages and cultures."],["Warriors and artists","They were known as fierce warriors and skilled metalworkers who made beautiful jewelry and swirling designs."],["Living languages","Celtic languages such as Irish, Scottish Gaelic, Welsh and Breton are still spoken today."]],
l:"The Celts were a collection of peoples in ancient Europe who spoke related Celtic languages and shared aspects of culture, religion and art. From about 800 BCE, their culture spread across central Europe to Britain and Ireland, Spain, France, northern Italy and even Anatolia. They were never a single nation but many tribes. Roman expansion conquered most Celtic lands, but Celtic languages and traditions survived, especially in Ireland, Scotland, Wales, Brittany, Cornwall and the Isle of Man.",
s:[["Hallstatt and La Tène","Archaeologists name two main phases of early Celtic culture after sites where many objects were found. The Hallstatt culture, named after a salt-mining village in Austria, flourished from about 800 to 450 BCE. The La Tène culture, named after a site in Switzerland, followed from about 450 BCE, famous for its flowing, curving art."],["Life and society","Celtic peoples lived mainly as farmers and herders in villages and hillforts, and some built large fortified towns the Romans called oppida. Society included warriors, craftsmen, farmers and druids, who served as priests, teachers and judges. Bards preserved history and stories in poetry and song."],["Warriors","The Celts were famed and feared as warriors. In about 390 BCE, a Celtic army led by Brennus sacked Rome. Celtic groups also invaded Greece and settled in central Anatolia, where they were called Galatians. Roman writers described them as brave, proud and fond of feasting and bright clothing."],["Conquest by Rome","Julius Caesar conquered Gaul, roughly modern France, between 58 and 50 BCE, defeating the Gallic leader Vercingetorix at Alesia in 52 BCE. Rome later conquered much of Britain. In 60 or 61 CE, Queen Boudica of the Iceni led a major revolt against Roman rule in Britain, burning London before being defeated."]],
k:[["Period","From about 800 BCE"],["Early cultures","Hallstatt and La Tène"],["Religious leaders","Druids"],["Living Celtic languages","Irish, Scottish Gaelic, Welsh, Breton, Cornish, Manx"],["Famous revolt leader","Boudica"]],
d:[["c. 800 BCE","The Hallstatt culture develops"],["c. 450 BCE","The La Tène culture emerges"],["c. 390 BCE","Celts led by Brennus sack Rome"],["52 BCE","Caesar defeats Vercingetorix at Alesia"],["60–61 CE","Boudica leads a revolt in Britain"],["c. 800","Monks create the Book of Kells"]],
s2:[["Druids","Druids were the religious and learned class of Celtic society. They led ceremonies, studied the stars and nature, settled disputes and taught young people, memorizing knowledge rather than writing it down. Much of what we know about them comes from Roman writers, who may have exaggerated. Rome suppressed the druids."],["Celtic art","Celtic artists created beautiful metalwork, such as golden neck rings called torcs, decorated shields and mirrors, with swirling spirals, knots and animal shapes. Later, Christian monks in Ireland and Britain combined these designs with Christian themes in illuminated manuscripts such as the Book of Kells."],["Samhain and Halloween","The Celts marked the year with festivals. Samhain, at the beginning of November, marked the end of the harvest and the start of winter, when the boundary between the world of the living and the dead was thought to be thin. Many traditions of Halloween, such as costumes and bonfires, may have roots in Samhain."],["Celtic languages today","Celtic languages once spoken across Europe survive today in two groups. Irish, Scottish Gaelic and Manx form one; Welsh, Breton and Cornish form the other. Welsh is spoken by hundreds of thousands of people, and Irish is an official language of Ireland and the European Union. Efforts to revive these languages include schools and media."],["Myths and legends","Celtic myths are full of heroes, gods and magical beings. Irish legends tell of the hero Cú Chulainn and of the Tuatha Dé Danann, a godlike people. Welsh tales collected in the Mabinogion and Arthurian legends, possibly rooted in Celtic Britain, have inspired many later stories."],["Who were the Celts?","Historians debate the term Celts. Ancient Greeks and Romans used names such as Keltoi and Galli for various peoples, and the idea of a single Celtic people became popular much later. Today scholars often speak of Celtic-speaking peoples with many different identities and ways of life."]],
sp:["Boudica","Boudica was queen of the Iceni people in eastern Britain. After her husband died, the Romans seized her kingdom, flogged her and mistreated her daughters. In 60 or 61 CE, she led a huge revolt, destroying the Roman towns of Camulodunum (Colchester), Londinium (London) and Verulamium (St Albans). The Roman governor eventually defeated her army. She has since become a symbol of resistance, and a bronze statue of her in a chariot stands in London."],
g:[["Druid","A priest and scholar of the ancient Celts"],["Torc","A rigid metal neck ring worn by Celts"],["Hillfort","A fortified settlement on a hill"],["Gaul","The Roman name for the land of the Celts, roughly France"]],
f:["Celts sacked Rome around 390 BCE.","Welsh and Irish are Celtic languages.","Halloween may have roots in the Celtic festival of Samhain."],
fun:"Some Halloween traditions may come from the ancient Celtic festival of Samhain.",
tl:"Ang mga Celt ay mga sinaunang mamamayan sa Europa na may magkakaugnay na wika at kultura. Kilala sila bilang mandirigma at mahuhusay na panday. Sinasalita pa rin ang mga wikang Celtic gaya ng Irish at Welsh."});

ADDART({id:"mughal",t:"The Mughal Empire",c:"History",i:"🕌",w:"Mughal Empire",r:["tajmahal","islam","hindu","indiacountry","partition"],
p:[["Rulers of India","The Mughal Empire ruled much of the Indian subcontinent from 1526 to the 1700s."],["Wealth and splendor","It was one of the richest empires in the world, famous for its art, gardens and buildings."],["The Taj Mahal","The Mughal emperor Shah Jahan built the Taj Mahal as a tomb for his wife."]],
l:"The Mughal Empire was a powerful empire that ruled most of the Indian subcontinent from the early 1500s until the 1700s, with emperors continuing in name until 1857. Founded by Babur, a descendant of Timur and Genghis Khan from Central Asia, it reached its height under Akbar, Jahangir, Shah Jahan and Aurangzeb. The Mughals ruled a vast, diverse population of Hindus, Muslims and others, created magnificent architecture such as the Taj Mahal and the Red Fort, and blended Persian, Indian and Central Asian cultures.",
s:[["Babur and the founding","Babur, a prince from Fergana in present-day Uzbekistan, invaded northern India and defeated the Sultan of Delhi at the First Battle of Panipat in 1526, using cannons and cavalry tactics. He founded the Mughal dynasty. His memoir, the Baburnama, describes his life, battles and love of gardens with remarkable honesty."],["Akbar the Great","Akbar, who ruled from 1556 to 1605, expanded the empire and built a strong administration. He sought to unite his diverse subjects through a policy of tolerance called sulh-i kul, peace with all, abolished a tax on non-Muslims and held discussions with scholars of many religions. He married Rajput princesses and appointed Hindus to high positions."],["Shah Jahan and the Taj Mahal","Shah Jahan, who ruled from 1628 to 1658, was a great builder. After his beloved wife Mumtaz Mahal died in childbirth in 1631, he built the Taj Mahal in Agra as her tomb. He also built the Red Fort and the Jama Masjid in Delhi. His son Aurangzeb imprisoned him in Agra Fort, where he could see the Taj Mahal from his window."],["Aurangzeb and decline","Aurangzeb, who ruled from 1658 to 1707, expanded the empire to its greatest size but faced revolts and costly wars, especially against the Marathas. His stricter religious policies are debated by historians. After his death, the empire weakened, regional powers rose and in 1739 the Persian ruler Nader Shah sacked Delhi."]],
k:[["Founded","1526"],["Founder","Babur"],["Greatest emperors","Akbar, Jahangir, Shah Jahan, Aurangzeb"],["Famous monument","Taj Mahal"],["Ended","1857"]],
d:[["1526","Babur wins the First Battle of Panipat"],["1556","Akbar becomes emperor"],["1632","Construction of the Taj Mahal begins"],["1707","Aurangzeb dies; the empire begins to decline"],["1739","Nader Shah sacks Delhi"],["1857","The last Mughal emperor is exiled after the Indian Rebellion"]],
s2:[["A rich empire","In about 1700 the Mughal Empire was one of the largest economies in the world, by some estimates producing about a quarter of the world’s goods. It exported fine cotton textiles, silk, spices, indigo and gems. European trading companies, including the British East India Company, came to trade and gradually gained power."],["Art and miniature painting","Mughal emperors supported artists who created detailed miniature paintings, blending Persian and Indian styles, showing court life, battles, animals, plants and portraits. Jahangir, who ruled from 1605 to 1627, was a keen naturalist and commissioned paintings of birds and flowers. Mughal jewelry, carpets and metalwork were also renowned."],["Gardens and buildings","The Mughals loved gardens laid out in four parts with water channels, called charbagh, symbolizing paradise. Mughal architecture combined domes, arches, minarets and fine inlay of colored stones in marble. Examples include Humayun’s Tomb in Delhi, Fatehpur Sikri built by Akbar, the Red Fort and the Shalimar Gardens in Lahore."],["Languages and culture","Persian was the language of the Mughal court and administration. Urdu developed from a mix of Hindustani, Persian, Arabic and Turkic words. Mughal cuisine, with dishes such as biryani and kebabs, influenced the food of South Asia. Music, poetry and festivals flourished under Mughal patronage."],["The British take over","As Mughal power declined, the British East India Company expanded its control after victories such as the Battle of Plassey in 1757. The Mughal emperor became a figurehead. After the Indian Rebellion of 1857, in which rebels proclaimed the elderly emperor Bahadur Shah II their leader, the British exiled him to Rangoon in Burma, ending the dynasty."],["The Peacock Throne","Shah Jahan commissioned the Peacock Throne, covered in gold and set with diamonds, rubies, emeralds and pearls, said to have taken seven years to make. When Nader Shah sacked Delhi in 1739, he carried it off to Persia, along with the famous Koh-i-Noor diamond. The original throne was later lost."]],
sp:["The Taj Mahal","The Taj Mahal in Agra was built between about 1632 and 1653 by some 20,000 workers, including craftsmen from across the empire and beyond. Its white marble dome, minarets and inlaid decoration of semi-precious stones make it one of the most beautiful buildings in the world. It changes color with the light, appearing pinkish at dawn and golden at sunset. It was declared a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1983 and is one of the New Seven Wonders of the World."],
g:[["Dynasty","A series of rulers from the same family"],["Miniature painting","A small, detailed painting"],["Charbagh","A four-part Persian-style garden"],["Figurehead","A leader in name with little real power"]],
f:["The Mughal Empire was founded in 1526.","Shah Jahan built the Taj Mahal for Mumtaz Mahal.","Akbar promoted tolerance among religions."],
fun:"Shah Jahan spent his last years imprisoned in Agra Fort, looking out at the Taj Mahal.",
tl:"Ang Imperyong Mughal ay namuno sa malaking bahagi ng subkontinenteng Indian mula 1526. Isa ito sa pinakamayamang imperyo, kilala sa sining at arkitektura. Ipinatayo ni Shah Jahan ang Taj Mahal para sa kanyang asawa."});

ADDART({id:"seaempires",t:"Srivijaya and Majapahit",c:"History",i:"🛕",w:"Majapahit",r:["sea","indonesiacountry","precol","buddh","silk"],
p:[["Kingdoms of the sea","Srivijaya and Majapahit were powerful maritime empires based in what is now Indonesia."],["Controlling trade","They grew rich by controlling sea routes between China, India and the islands of Southeast Asia."],["Links to the Philippines","Their trade and culture reached the islands of the Philippines, as shown by ancient artifacts."]],
l:"Srivijaya and Majapahit were two of the greatest empires of early Southeast Asia. Srivijaya, centered on Palembang in Sumatra, controlled the Strait of Malacca from about the 600s to the 1200s and was a major center of Buddhist learning. Majapahit, based in eastern Java, flourished from 1293 to the early 1500s and claimed influence over much of the Indonesian archipelago and beyond. Both grew rich from sea trade between China, India and the islands, and their cultures influenced the wider region, including the Philippines.",
s:[["Srivijaya","Srivijaya rose in the 600s around Palembang on the island of Sumatra. By controlling the Strait of Malacca and the Sunda Strait, it taxed and protected ships sailing between China and India. Its kings were Buddhist, and its inscriptions, among the oldest in the Malay language, date from the 680s."],["A center of learning","The Chinese Buddhist monk Yijing stopped in Srivijaya in 671 on his way to India and later returned to study and translate Buddhist texts there, advising other pilgrims to learn in Srivijaya first. Thousands of monks lived and studied in the capital. Buddhist art and temples spread across the region."],["Majapahit","Majapahit was founded in 1293 by Raden Wijaya in eastern Java, after he used a Mongol invasion force sent by Kublai Khan to defeat his rivals and then drove the Mongols out. Under King Hayam Wuruk and his chief minister Gajah Mada in the 1300s, Majapahit reached its height, with influence across much of the archipelago."],["Trade and wealth","Majapahit traded rice, spices and other goods with China, India, the Middle East and other Southeast Asian kingdoms. Its ports bustled with merchants. The court poem Nagarakretagama, written in 1365, describes the capital and lists many places said to pay tribute, though historians debate how much real control Majapahit had."]],
k:[["Srivijaya capital","Palembang, Sumatra"],["Srivijaya period","c. 600s–1200s"],["Majapahit capital","Trowulan, East Java"],["Majapahit period","1293–early 1500s"],["Famous minister","Gajah Mada"]],
d:[["671","The monk Yijing visits Srivijaya"],["683","The Kedukan Bukit inscription records Srivijaya’s power"],["900","The Laguna Copperplate Inscription is written in the Philippines"],["1025","The Chola Empire of southern India raids Srivijaya"],["1293","Majapahit is founded after a Mongol invasion of Java"],["1365","The poem Nagarakretagama is written"]],
s2:[["The Laguna Copperplate Inscription","In 1989 a worker near Lumban, Laguna, found a thin copper plate inscribed in the Kawi script, an early script of Java. Dated to the year 900, it records the cancellation of a debt and mentions places in the Philippines and possibly Java. It is the oldest known written document from the Philippines and shows that the islands were connected with the cultures and trade networks of Java and the Malay world."],["Gajah Mada’s oath","Gajah Mada, Majapahit’s powerful chief minister, is said to have sworn the Palapa Oath, vowing not to eat a certain spice until he had united the archipelago under Majapahit. He led military campaigns that expanded its influence. In modern Indonesia, he is a national hero, and the country’s first satellite was named Palapa."],["The Chola raid","In 1025 the Chola Empire of southern India, a rival trading power, launched a naval raid on Srivijaya, attacking its ports and capturing its king. Srivijaya never fully recovered its dominance, and other kingdoms rose in the region over the following centuries."],["Art and religion","Both empires blended local traditions with Hinduism and Buddhism from India. Java’s nearby temples, such as Buddhist Borobudur and Hindu Prambanan, built around the 800s by other Javanese dynasties, show the richness of this culture. Majapahit art included terracotta figures, temples and red-brick architecture at Trowulan."],["The coming of Islam","From the 1300s, Muslim merchants and teachers spread Islam through the ports of Southeast Asia. As Majapahit weakened in the 1400s, Muslim states such as the Sultanate of Malacca and Demak in Java rose. Islam also spread to the southern Philippines, where the Sultanate of Sulu was founded in the 1400s."],["Legacy","Indonesia sees Majapahit as a symbol of national unity, and its red and white colors are thought to have inspired the Indonesian flag. Srivijaya and Majapahit show that Southeast Asia had powerful, sophisticated states long before European arrival, linked by the sea to the wider world."]],
sp:["Driving out the Mongols","In 1292 Kublai Khan sent a large fleet to punish a Javanese king who had insulted his envoy. When the Mongols arrived in 1293, that king had already been killed by a rival. The clever prince Raden Wijaya allied with the Mongols to defeat the rival, then turned on the Mongols themselves and drove them back to their ships. He founded Majapahit, named, according to legend, after a bitter maja fruit found at the site."],
g:[["Maritime empire","An empire whose power is based on the sea"],["Strait","A narrow channel of water linking two seas"],["Tribute","Payment or gifts showing respect to a stronger state"],["Kawi","An old script used in Java and the Philippines"]],
f:["Srivijaya controlled the Strait of Malacca.","Majapahit was founded in 1293.","The Laguna Copperplate Inscription dates from 900."],
fun:"Indonesia’s first communications satellite was named Palapa after Gajah Mada’s oath.",
tl:"Ang Srivijaya at Majapahit ay makapangyarihang imperyong pandagat sa Indonesia ngayon. Yumaman sila sa pagkontrol ng kalakalan sa dagat. Ipinapakita ng Laguna Copperplate Inscription (900) ang ugnayan ng Pilipinas sa kulturang ito."});

ADDART({id:"blackdeath",t:"The Black Death",c:"History",i:"💀",w:"Black Death",r:["pandemic","microbe","silk","mongol","antibiotics"],
p:[["A deadly plague","The Black Death was a devastating plague pandemic that swept across Asia, Europe and Africa in the 1340s and 1350s."],["Spread by fleas","It was caused by bacteria carried by fleas living on rats and other animals."],["A changed world","It killed a large share of Europe’s people and transformed society."]],
l:"The Black Death was a pandemic of bubonic plague that struck western Asia, the Middle East, North Africa and Europe between 1346 and 1353. It was caused by the bacterium Yersinia pestis, spread mainly by fleas carried by rats and by people. It killed an estimated 30 to 60 percent of Europe’s population, tens of millions of people, along with huge numbers elsewhere. The Black Death caused terrible suffering but also brought lasting changes to economies, religion, medicine and society.",
s:[["Origins","Scientists studying DNA from burials have traced the strain behind the Black Death to Central Asia, near Lake Issyk-Kul in present-day Kyrgyzstan, where graves from 1338 and 1339 record deaths from plague. Plague spread along trade routes of the Mongol Empire, including the Silk Roads. In 1346 it struck the Mongol army besieging the trading city of Caffa in Crimea."],["Spread to Europe","Merchant ships from Caffa carried the disease across the Black Sea and Mediterranean. In October 1347, ships arrived at Messina in Sicily with sick and dying sailors. Within months, plague spread through Italy, France, Spain, England and beyond, reaching Scandinavia and Russia by 1351. It also devastated Egypt, Syria and North Africa."],["Symptoms","Bubonic plague caused fever, chills and painful swellings called buboes in the groin, armpits or neck. Many victims died within days. When plague infected the lungs, it could spread directly from person to person through coughing, and this pneumonic form was almost always fatal. Septicemic plague infected the blood."],["Fear and response","People did not know what caused the plague. Some blamed bad air, the stars or divine punishment. Groups called flagellants whipped themselves in public to seek forgiveness. Tragically, Jewish communities were falsely blamed and attacked in many places. Some cities isolated the sick and controlled arrivals, early forms of public health measures."]],
k:[["Years","1346–1353"],["Cause","Bacterium Yersinia pestis"],["Spread by","Fleas on rats; also person to person"],["Europe’s population lost","About 30–60 percent"],["Origin","Central Asia"]],
d:[["1338–1339","Plague deaths are recorded near Lake Issyk-Kul"],["1346","Plague strikes the siege of Caffa"],["1347","Plague reaches Messina in Sicily"],["1348","The Black Death sweeps through France, Italy and England"],["1377","Ragusa (Dubrovnik) introduces quarantine for travelers"],["1894","Alexandre Yersin identifies the plague bacterium"]],
s2:[["Quarantine","In 1377 the city of Ragusa, now Dubrovnik in Croatia, required ships and travelers from plague areas to wait 30 days before entering. Venice and other cities later extended the waiting time to 40 days, quaranta giorni in Italian, giving us the word quarantine. Quarantine remains an important tool against infectious diseases."],["Changes to society","With so many people dead, there were too few workers to farm the land. Surviving laborers demanded higher wages and more freedom, weakening the feudal system in Western Europe. Rulers tried to freeze wages, leading to unrest such as the Peasants’ Revolt in England in 1381. Some historians link these changes to the later rise of new economies."],["Writers of the plague","The Italian writer Giovanni Boccaccio began his Decameron with a vivid description of plague in Florence, then told stories shared by young people who fled the city. The North African historian Ibn Khaldun, who lost his parents to the plague, described how it emptied towns and changed civilizations."],["Discovering the cause","The cause of plague was discovered during a later pandemic. In 1894 in Hong Kong, the Swiss-French scientist Alexandre Yersin identified the bacterium now named Yersinia pestis. In 1898 Paul-Louis Simond showed that rat fleas spread it. DNA from Black Death burials, studied in the 2010s, confirmed that Yersinia pestis caused the medieval pandemic."],["Plague today","Plague still exists in wild rodents in parts of Africa, Asia and the Americas, causing occasional human cases. Today it can be treated successfully with antibiotics if caught early. Madagascar, the Democratic Republic of the Congo and Peru are among the countries that report cases."],["Art and memory","The Black Death inspired art showing the Dance of Death, in which skeletons lead people of all ranks, from kings to peasants, reminding viewers that death comes to everyone. Some historians think the nursery rhyme Ring a Ring o’ Roses refers to the plague, but folklorists have found no evidence for this."]],
sp:["The word quarantine","When plague kept returning to Europe after the Black Death, cities looked for ways to protect themselves. Ragusa made ships wait 30 days, a trentino, before landing. Venice built isolation hospitals on islands in its lagoon and later required ships to wait 40 days. The Italian word for 40, quaranta, gave English the word quarantine. During the COVID-19 pandemic, quarantine again became a familiar word around the world."],
g:[["Plague","A serious infectious disease caused by Yersinia pestis"],["Bubo","A swollen lymph node, a sign of bubonic plague"],["Quarantine","Isolating people who may carry a disease"],["Feudalism","A medieval system in which peasants worked land for lords"]],
f:["The Black Death was caused by bacteria spread by fleas.","It killed about a third to more than half of Europe’s people.","The word quarantine comes from the Italian for 40 days."],
fun:"The word quarantine comes from the 40 days ships had to wait before landing in Venice.",
tl:"Ang Black Death ay matinding salot noong 1340s at 1350s na kumalat sa Asya, Europa at Aprika. Dulot ito ng bakteryang dala ng mga pulgas sa daga. Napatay nito ang malaking bahagi ng populasyon ng Europa."});

ADDART({id:"greenrev",t:"The Green Revolution",c:"History",i:"🌾",w:"Green Revolution",r:["rice","wheat","agri","indiacountry","mexicocountry"],
p:[["More food for the world","The Green Revolution was a period from the 1940s to the 1970s when new crop varieties and farming methods greatly increased harvests."],["Miracle rice","Scientists at the International Rice Research Institute in Los Baños, Laguna, created high-yield rice that spread across Asia."],["Benefits and costs","It saved millions from hunger but also brought environmental and social problems."]],
l:"The Green Revolution was a period of rapid increases in crop yields, especially of wheat and rice, in developing countries from the 1940s to the 1970s. Scientists bred high-yielding varieties that produced far more grain when grown with fertilizers, irrigation and pesticides. Grain production in many countries soared, helping India, Mexico, the Philippines and others avoid feared famines as populations grew. The Green Revolution is credited with saving many millions of lives, but it also raised concerns about the environment, water use and fairness to small farmers.",
s:[["Borlaug and wheat","In 1944 the American scientist Norman Borlaug began working in Mexico on a program to improve wheat. He bred short, sturdy wheat varieties that resisted disease and did not fall over when heavily fertilized. Mexico became self-sufficient in wheat, and the new varieties spread to India and Pakistan in the 1960s. Borlaug won the Nobel Peace Prize in 1970."],["IRRI and miracle rice","The International Rice Research Institute, IRRI, was founded in 1960 in Los Baños, Laguna, Philippines, with support from the Ford and Rockefeller foundations and the Philippine government. In 1966 it released IR8, a semi-dwarf rice that could double yields with fertilizer and irrigation. Nicknamed miracle rice, it spread rapidly across Asia."],["India’s transformation","In the 1960s India faced severe food shortages and depended on imported grain. Scientists such as M. S. Swaminathan introduced high-yielding wheat and rice, and states such as Punjab and Haryana saw harvests soar. Within years, India became largely self-sufficient in grain, an achievement that shaped its development."],["How it worked","The Green Revolution combined several elements: high-yielding seeds, chemical fertilizers, irrigation, pesticides and government support through credit, research and guaranteed prices. The new varieties put more energy into grain rather than stalks and could be planted more than once a year in some places."]],
k:[["Period","About 1940s–1970s"],["Key scientist","Norman Borlaug"],["Rice institute","IRRI, Los Baños, founded 1960"],["Miracle rice","IR8, released 1966"],["Nobel Peace Prize","Borlaug, 1970"]],
d:[["1944","Norman Borlaug begins wheat research in Mexico"],["1960","IRRI is founded in Los Baños, Laguna"],["1966","IRRI releases IR8 rice"],["1968","Indian wheat harvests reach record levels"],["1970","Borlaug wins the Nobel Peace Prize"],["2021","The Philippines approves Golden Rice for planting"]],
s2:[["Feeding a growing world","Between the 1960s and 2000, grain production in developing countries roughly tripled while the area of farmland grew only modestly. In 1968 a famous book predicted that hundreds of millions would starve in the 1970s; instead, food production generally kept pace with population growth in much of Asia and Latin America."],["Environmental concerns","Heavy use of fertilizers and pesticides polluted rivers and harmed wildlife and human health. Irrigation drained groundwater in places such as Punjab. Planting the same few varieties over large areas reduced crop diversity and made crops vulnerable to pests. Today scientists aim for a more sustainable approach."],["Who benefited?","Farmers who could afford seeds, fertilizer and irrigation often gained most, while poorer farmers sometimes fell into debt or lost land. Regions with good water and roads benefited more than others. Much of Africa saw fewer gains, partly because of different crops, soils and conditions, and efforts continue to raise yields there."],["The Philippines and rice","IRRI made the Philippines a world center of rice research. Its scientists, including many Filipinos, have developed hundreds of varieties, including rice that survives floods for two weeks, tolerates salt or drought, and matures faster. IRRI’s gene bank stores more than 100,000 types of rice from around the world."],["Golden Rice","Scientists developed Golden Rice, genetically modified to produce beta-carotene, which the body turns into vitamin A, to help prevent blindness and illness caused by vitamin A deficiency. In 2021 the Philippines became the first country to approve it for commercial planting, but in 2024 a court halted its planting pending legal challenges, reflecting debates about genetically modified crops."],["Toward an evergreen revolution","M. S. Swaminathan called for an evergreen revolution: increasing food production while protecting soil, water and biodiversity. Modern approaches include precise use of fertilizer and water, crop rotation, improved varieties bred with genetic tools, and supporting small farmers, who produce much of the world’s food."]],
sp:["IR8, the miracle rice","IR8 was created at IRRI by crossing a tall, vigorous Indonesian rice called Peta with a short Chinese-Taiwanese variety, Dee-geo-woo-gen. The result was a short, sturdy plant that did not fall over when heavily fertilized and could produce up to 5 to 10 metric tons per hectare, compared with 1 to 2 metric tons for many traditional varieties. Released in 1966, it helped prevent food shortages across Asia. It became a symbol of hope across the continent."],
g:[["High-yielding variety","A crop bred to produce much more grain"],["Fertilizer","A substance added to soil to help plants grow"],["Irrigation","Supplying water to crops artificially"],["Gene bank","A collection that stores seeds for the future"]],
f:["IRRI was founded in Los Baños, Laguna, in 1960.","IR8 was called miracle rice.","Norman Borlaug won the Nobel Peace Prize in 1970."],
fun:"IRRI’s gene bank in Los Baños stores more than 100,000 types of rice.",
tl:"Ang Green Revolution ay panahon mula 1940s hanggang 1970s kung kailan lubhang tumaas ang ani dahil sa bagong binhi at paraan ng pagsasaka. Ginawa sa IRRI sa Los Baños, Laguna ang IR8, ang tinaguriang miracle rice."});

