ADDART({id:"tokyo",t:"Tokyo",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🗼",w:"Tokyo",r:["japancountry","japan","meiji","fuji","urban"],
p:[["Japan’s capital","Tokyo is the capital of Japan and the heart of one of the largest urban areas on Earth."],["Old and new","Ancient temples and shrines stand among skyscrapers, neon signs and busy train stations."],["Rebuilt twice","Tokyo was rebuilt after a great earthquake in 1923 and after bombing in World War II."]],
l:"Tokyo is the capital of Japan, located on Tokyo Bay on the island of Honshu. The Tokyo Metropolis has about 14 million people, and the Greater Tokyo Area, with about 37 million by common measures, has long been considered the world’s largest metropolitan area; a 2025 United Nations study using a new method ranked Jakarta and Dhaka larger. Once a fishing village called Edo, it became the seat of the shoguns in 1603 and Japan’s capital in 1868. Tokyo is a world center of finance, technology, fashion, food and popular culture.",
s:[["From Edo to Tokyo","In 1457 the warrior Ōta Dōkan built a castle at Edo. In 1603 Tokugawa Ieyasu made Edo the seat of his shogunate, and it grew into one of the largest cities in the world, with over a million people by the 1700s. When the emperor moved there in 1868, at the start of the Meiji era, it was renamed Tokyo, meaning eastern capital."],["Disaster and rebirth","On 1 September 1923, the Great Kantō earthquake and the fires that followed destroyed much of Tokyo and Yokohama, killing over 100,000 people. In March 1945, during World War II, American firebombing killed around 100,000 people in a single night and burned large areas. Each time, Tokyo was rebuilt, and after the war it grew into a modern megacity."],["A city of trains","Tokyo has one of the world’s busiest and most punctual public transport systems, with dozens of train and subway lines. Shinjuku Station is the busiest railway station in the world, used by millions of people each day. The first Shinkansen bullet train line opened between Tokyo and Osaka in 1964."],["Neighborhoods","Tokyo is made up of many distinct areas. Shibuya is known for its famous scramble crossing, Shinjuku for skyscrapers and nightlife, Ginza for shopping, Akihabara for electronics, anime and games, and Asakusa for the ancient Sensō-ji temple. Harajuku is famous for youth fashion."]],
k:[["Country","Japan"],["Tokyo Metropolis population","About 14 million"],["Greater Tokyo","About 37 million (common estimates)"],["Former name","Edo"],["Tallest structure","Tokyo Skytree, 634 m"]],
d:[["1457","Ōta Dōkan builds Edo Castle"],["1603","Edo becomes the seat of the Tokugawa shogunate"],["1868","Edo is renamed Tokyo and becomes the capital"],["1923","The Great Kantō earthquake devastates the city"],["1964","Tokyo hosts the Summer Olympics; the Shinkansen opens"],["2021","Tokyo hosts the postponed 2020 Summer Olympics"]],
s2:[["Sensō-ji","Sensō-ji in Asakusa is Tokyo’s oldest temple, traditionally founded in 645 CE after fishermen found a statue of the bodhisattva Kannon in a river. Visitors pass through the Kaminarimon, the Thunder Gate, with its giant red lantern, and walk along Nakamise, a street of traditional shops, to reach the main hall."],["The 1964 Olympics","In 1964 Tokyo became the first Asian city to host the Summer Olympics. The Games showed the world Japan’s recovery after the war. In preparation, Tokyo built highways, hotels and the first Shinkansen line. Tokyo hosted the Games again in 2021, delayed by a year because of the COVID-19 pandemic."],["Food capital","Tokyo has more restaurants awarded Michelin stars than any other city. It is famous for sushi, ramen, tempura, soba and many other foods. The famous fish market moved from Tsukiji to a new site at Toyosu in 2018, while the old outer market still bustles with food stalls."],["Pop culture","Tokyo is a center of anime, manga, video games and music. Akihabara is packed with shops selling electronics, figures and games, and Ikebukuro and Nakano are also popular with fans. Character goods, themed cafés and karaoke are part of the city’s lively culture."],["Living with earthquakes","Tokyo lies in one of the world’s most earthquake-prone regions. Buildings follow strict codes, and many skyscrapers use special systems to absorb shaking. Schools hold regular drills, and 1 September, the anniversary of the 1923 earthquake, is Disaster Prevention Day in Japan."],["Green spaces","Despite its size, Tokyo has many parks and gardens. Ueno Park has museums and a zoo, Shinjuku Gyoen is famous for cherry blossoms and the Meiji Jingu shrine stands in a forest planted by volunteers from across Japan in the 1910s and 1920s. The Imperial Palace, on the site of Edo Castle, is surrounded by moats and gardens."]],
sp:["The Shibuya scramble","Outside Shibuya Station lies one of the busiest pedestrian crossings in the world. When the traffic lights turn red in all directions, pedestrians flood the intersection from every side at once, sometimes thousands at a time, crossing straight and diagonally. Despite the crowds, people rarely collide. Nearby stands a statue of Hachikō, a loyal dog who waited at the station for his owner every day for nearly ten years after the owner’s death in 1925."],
g:[["Megacity","A city with more than 10 million people"],["Shogunate","A government led by a shogun, a military ruler"],["Shinkansen","Japan’s high-speed bullet train"],["Metropolis","A very large city; also Tokyo’s official name as a prefecture"]],
f:["Tokyo was once called Edo.","Shinjuku Station is the busiest station in the world.","Tokyo has more Michelin-starred restaurants than any other city."],
fun:"Tokyo’s name means eastern capital.",
tl:"Ang Tokyo ang kabisera ng Hapon at isa sa pinakamalaking urbanong lugar sa mundo. Dati itong tinatawag na Edo. Itinayong muli ito matapos ang lindol noong 1923 at ang pambobomba noong Ikalawang Digmaang Pandaigdig."});

ADDART({id:"cairo",t:"Cairo",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🕌",w:"Cairo",r:["egyptcountry","egypt","pyramids","nile","islam"],
p:[["City on the Nile","Cairo is the capital of Egypt, spreading along the Nile River near where it fans out into its delta."],["City of a thousand minarets","Its historic Islamic quarter is filled with mosques, madrasas and markets."],["Gateway to the pyramids","The Pyramids of Giza stand on the city’s western edge."]],
l:"Cairo is the capital and largest city of Egypt and one of the largest cities in Africa and the Arab world, with more than 20 million people in its metropolitan area. It lies on the Nile River, close to the ancient capital of Memphis and the Pyramids of Giza. Founded in 969 CE, Cairo became a great center of Islamic learning, trade and architecture, and its historic quarter is a UNESCO World Heritage Site. Today it is a bustling, crowded city, a hub of government, culture, film and education in the Arab world.",
s:[["Ancient roots","Long before Cairo was founded, the area was home to Memphis, capital of ancient Egypt, and Heliopolis, a center of sun worship. In 641 CE Arab armies founded the city of Fustat on the Nile. In 969 the Fatimid dynasty built a new walled royal city nearby called al-Qāhira, the Victorious, from which the name Cairo comes."],["A center of learning","The Fatimids founded al-Azhar Mosque in 970, and it grew into one of the oldest continuously operating universities in the world, still a leading center of Islamic scholarship. Students from across the Muslim world have studied there for over a thousand years."],["Medieval splendor","In 1176 Saladin began building the Citadel on a hill overlooking the city. Under the Mamluk sultans, from 1250 to 1517, Cairo was one of the largest and richest cities in the world, filled with mosques, schools, hospitals and markets. Many of these buildings still stand in Islamic Cairo."],["Modern Cairo","In the 1800s, the ruler Khedive Ismail built new districts with wide boulevards modeled on Paris. Cairo grew rapidly in the 1900s as people moved from the countryside. In 1987 it opened the first metro system in Africa. Egypt is building a New Administrative Capital east of Cairo to ease crowding."]],
k:[["Country","Egypt"],["Metropolitan population","More than 20 million"],["Founded","969 CE"],["Name means","The Victorious"],["River","Nile"]],
d:[["641","Arab armies found Fustat"],["969","The Fatimids found al-Qāhira"],["970","Al-Azhar Mosque is founded"],["1176","Saladin begins building the Citadel"],["1979","Historic Cairo becomes a UNESCO World Heritage Site"],["2025","The Grand Egyptian Museum fully opens near the pyramids"]],
s2:[["The Pyramids of Giza","On the western edge of Greater Cairo stand the Pyramids of Giza, built about 4,500 years ago as tombs for the pharaohs Khufu, Khafre and Menkaure, along with the Great Sphinx. The Great Pyramid is the only one of the Seven Wonders of the Ancient World still standing. Millions of visitors come to see them each year."],["Museums of Cairo","The Egyptian Museum in Tahrir Square, opened in 1902, holds a vast collection of ancient artifacts. The Grand Egyptian Museum near Giza, one of the largest archaeological museums in the world, fully opened in 2025 and displays the complete treasures of Tutankhamun’s tomb."],["Khan el-Khalili","Khan el-Khalili is a famous bazaar in Islamic Cairo, dating back to the 1300s. Its narrow lanes are lined with shops selling spices, perfumes, lanterns, jewelry, textiles and souvenirs, and coffeehouses where people have gathered for centuries. The Nobel Prize-winning novelist Naguib Mahfouz set many stories in this area."],["Naguib Mahfouz","Naguib Mahfouz, born in Cairo in 1911, wrote novels about the lives of ordinary Cairenes, including the Cairo Trilogy, which follows a family through the early 1900s. In 1988 he became the first Arabic-language writer to win the Nobel Prize in Literature."],["Hollywood of the Arab world","Cairo has long been the center of Arab film, music and television. Egyptian films and songs, such as those of the singer Umm Kulthum, became popular across the Arab world, spreading the Egyptian dialect of Arabic. Cairo’s studios have produced thousands of films."],["Life in a megacity","Cairo is lively, noisy and crowded, with heavy traffic, busy markets and street food such as koshari, a dish of rice, lentils, pasta and tomato sauce. Many people live in informal neighborhoods. The city faces challenges of air pollution, housing and water, but its energy and history make it one of the world’s great cities."]],
sp:["The City of the Dead","In the east of Cairo lies a vast area of tombs and mausoleums built from the Mamluk period onward, called the City of the Dead, or the Qarafa. Some of its tombs are masterpieces of Islamic architecture. Over generations, many families have made homes among the tombs, living alongside the graves of the dead, creating a unique neighborhood where tens of thousands of people live."],
g:[["Delta","Land at a river’s mouth where it splits into branches"],["Madrasa","An Islamic school"],["Bazaar","A market with many shops"],["Fatimids","A dynasty that ruled Egypt from 969 to 1171"]],
f:["Cairo’s name means the Victorious.","Al-Azhar is one of the oldest universities in the world.","Cairo opened Africa’s first metro in 1987."],
fun:"Cairo is sometimes called the city of a thousand minarets.",
tl:"Ang Cairo ang kabisera ng Ehipto, nasa tabi ng Ilog Nile. Isa ito sa pinakamalaking lungsod sa Aprika at mundong Arabe. Nasa gilid nito ang mga Piramide ng Giza, at kilala ito sa mga moske at sa Unibersidad ng al-Azhar."});

ADDART({id:"istanbul",t:"Istanbul",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🕌",w:"Istanbul",r:["turkeycountry","byzant","ottoman","rome","silk"],
p:[["City on two continents","Istanbul spans the Bosphorus strait, with parts in both Europe and Asia."],["Three names","It has been called Byzantium, Constantinople and Istanbul."],["Capital of empires","It was the capital of the Byzantine and Ottoman empires for about 1,600 years."]],
l:"Istanbul is the largest city of Türkiye, with about 15.7 million people, making it one of the largest cities in Europe. It lies on both sides of the Bosphorus, a narrow strait connecting the Black Sea and the Sea of Marmara, so it is partly in Europe and partly in Asia. Founded as the Greek colony of Byzantium, it became Constantinople, capital of the Roman and Byzantine empires, and later the capital of the Ottoman Empire. Its skyline of domes and minarets, including Hagia Sophia, reflects its long history.",
s:[["Byzantium","According to tradition, Greek colonists from Megara founded Byzantium around 657 BCE on the European side of the Bosphorus. Its position, controlling the passage between the Black Sea and the Mediterranean and the crossing between Europe and Asia, made it valuable to every empire in the region."],["Constantinople","In 330 CE the Roman emperor Constantine the Great made the city his new capital and renamed it Constantinople. After the western Roman Empire fell, it remained the capital of the Eastern Roman, or Byzantine, Empire for over a thousand years, protected by massive walls built in the 400s. It was one of the largest and richest cities in the world."],["The Ottoman capital","On 29 May 1453, the Ottoman sultan Mehmed II captured Constantinople after a long siege, ending the Byzantine Empire. The city became the Ottoman capital and was gradually known as Istanbul. Sultans built magnificent mosques and palaces, such as Topkapı Palace and the Süleymaniye Mosque, designed by the great architect Mimar Sinan."],["Modern Istanbul","When the Republic of Turkey was founded in 1923, Ankara became the capital. But Istanbul remained the country’s largest city and center of business, culture and tourism. Bridges and tunnels now link its European and Asian sides, including the Marmaray rail tunnel under the Bosphorus, opened in 2013."]],
k:[["Country","Türkiye"],["Population","About 15.7 million"],["Former names","Byzantium, Constantinople"],["Waterway","Bosphorus Strait"],["Continents","Europe and Asia"]],
d:[["c. 657 BCE","Greek colonists found Byzantium"],["330 CE","Constantine makes the city his capital"],["537","Hagia Sophia is completed"],["1453","The Ottomans capture Constantinople"],["1923","Ankara becomes Turkey’s capital"],["1973","The first Bosphorus Bridge links Europe and Asia"]],
s2:[["Hagia Sophia","Hagia Sophia, meaning holy wisdom, was completed in 537 under the Byzantine emperor Justinian. For nearly a thousand years it was the largest church in the world, famous for its enormous dome that seems to float on light. After 1453 it became a mosque, in 1934 a museum and in 2020 a mosque again, while remaining open to visitors."],["The Blue Mosque","The Sultan Ahmed Mosque, completed in 1616, faces Hagia Sophia across a square. It is called the Blue Mosque because of the more than 20,000 blue tiles decorating its interior. It has six minarets and is still an active place of worship."],["The Grand Bazaar","The Grand Bazaar, begun in the 1450s soon after the Ottoman conquest, is one of the largest and oldest covered markets in the world, with about 4,000 shops along dozens of streets. It sells carpets, jewelry, ceramics, spices, lamps and leather goods."],["The walls of Theodosius","In the 400s, Emperor Theodosius II built massive land walls stretching about 6 kilometers across the peninsula, with moats, towers and double walls. They protected Constantinople from many sieges for about a thousand years, until Ottoman cannons breached them in 1453. Long sections still stand."],["Earthquake risk","Istanbul lies near the North Anatolian Fault, one of the world’s most active faults. A major earthquake in 1999 near İzmit, east of the city, killed more than 17,000 people. Scientists warn that a large earthquake near Istanbul is likely in the coming decades, and the city is strengthening buildings."],["A city of writers and cats","The novelist Orhan Pamuk, winner of the 2006 Nobel Prize in Literature, has written lovingly about his native Istanbul. The city is also famous for its many street cats, cared for by residents who leave food and water for them. They appear in films and have become a symbol of the city."]],
sp:["The fall of Constantinople","In 1453 the young Ottoman sultan Mehmed II besieged Constantinople with a huge army and giant cannons, including one that fired stone balls weighing hundreds of kilograms. When the defenders blocked the Golden Horn harbor with a great chain, Mehmed had ships dragged overland on greased logs into the harbor. On 29 May, Ottoman troops broke through the walls, and the last Byzantine emperor, Constantine XI, died fighting. Mehmed became known as the Conqueror."],
g:[["Strait","A narrow waterway connecting two seas"],["Byzantine Empire","The Eastern Roman Empire, centered on Constantinople"],["Minaret","A tall tower of a mosque"],["Bazaar","A market of many shops"]],
f:["Istanbul lies in both Europe and Asia.","Hagia Sophia was completed in 537.","Constantinople fell to the Ottomans in 1453."],
fun:"In 1453 the Ottomans dragged warships overland to get around a chain blocking the harbor.",
tl:"Ang Istanbul ang pinakamalaking lungsod ng Türkiye, nasa magkabilang panig ng Bosphorus, kaya bahagi ito ng Europa at Asya. Dati itong tinawag na Byzantium at Constantinople at naging kabisera ng mga imperyong Byzantine at Ottoman."});

ADDART({id:"rio",t:"Rio de Janeiro",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🏖️",w:"Rio de Janeiro",r:["brazilcountry","soccer","olymp","festivals","amazon"],
p:[["The marvelous city","Rio de Janeiro, in Brazil, is famous for its beaches, mountains and the giant statue of Christ the Redeemer."],["Samba parades","Its Carnival, with samba parades, is one of the biggest festivals in the world."],["Former capital","Rio was the capital of Brazil until 1960 and briefly the seat of the Portuguese Empire."]],
l:"Rio de Janeiro is a city in southeastern Brazil, with about 6.2 million people and over 12 million in its metropolitan area. Set between green mountains and the Atlantic Ocean around Guanabara Bay, it is known as the Cidade Maravilhosa, the marvelous city. Rio is famous for Copacabana and Ipanema beaches, Sugarloaf Mountain, the statue of Christ the Redeemer and its spectacular Carnival. It was Brazil’s capital until 1960 and hosted the 2016 Olympic Games, the first in South America.",
s:[["River of January","Portuguese explorers arrived in January 1502 and mistook the entrance of Guanabara Bay for the mouth of a river, naming it Rio de Janeiro, River of January. The city was founded in 1565 by Estácio de Sá. It grew as a port for gold and diamonds from Minas Gerais and became the capital of colonial Brazil in 1763."],["A royal capital","In 1808, fleeing Napoleon’s invasion of Portugal, the Portuguese royal family and court sailed to Rio, making it the capital of the Portuguese Empire, the only European empire ruled from the Americas. After Brazil’s independence in 1822, Rio was the capital of the Empire of Brazil and then of the republic until Brasília replaced it in 1960."],["Mountains and sea","Rio is famous for its dramatic landscape, recognized by UNESCO in 2012. Sugarloaf Mountain rises from the bay, with cable cars running since 1912. The Tijuca Forest, one of the world’s largest urban forests, was replanted in the 1860s after coffee farming had cleared it. Beaches such as Copacabana and Ipanema are centers of daily life."],["People and inequality","Rio is a city of great contrasts. Wealthy neighborhoods stand near favelas, informal communities built on hillsides, where a large share of the population lives. Favelas are home to vibrant culture, music and community life, but many face poverty and violence. Efforts continue to improve services and safety."]],
k:[["Country","Brazil"],["City population","About 6.2 million (2022 census)"],["Founded","1565"],["Name means","River of January"],["Famous statue","Christ the Redeemer (1931)"]],
d:[["1502","Portuguese explorers arrive at Guanabara Bay"],["1565","Estácio de Sá founds the city"],["1808","The Portuguese royal court moves to Rio"],["1931","The statue of Christ the Redeemer is completed"],["1960","Brasília replaces Rio as Brazil’s capital"],["2016","Rio hosts the first Olympic Games in South America"]],
s2:[["Christ the Redeemer","The statue of Christ the Redeemer stands with outstretched arms on top of Corcovado mountain, 700 meters above the city. Completed in 1931, it is about 30 meters tall, not counting its pedestal, and made of reinforced concrete covered with soapstone tiles. It was designed by the French sculptor Paul Landowski and the Brazilian engineer Heitor da Silva Costa, and was named one of the New Seven Wonders of the World in 2007."],["Carnival","Rio’s Carnival, held before Lent, attracts millions of people. The highlight is the parade of samba schools in the Sambadrome, designed by the architect Oscar Niemeyer and opened in 1984. Each school presents thousands of dancers, drummers and giant floats telling a story. Street parties called blocos fill neighborhoods across the city."],["Samba and bossa nova","Samba, with roots in Afro-Brazilian music, developed in Rio in the early 1900s and became Brazil’s national rhythm. In the late 1950s, musicians such as Antônio Carlos Jobim and João Gilberto created bossa nova, a gentle blend of samba and jazz. The song The Girl from Ipanema became one of the most recorded songs in history."],["The Maracanã","The Maracanã stadium was built for the 1950 FIFA World Cup. In the deciding match, about 200,000 people watched Brazil lose to Uruguay, a shock known as the Maracanaço. The stadium later hosted the 2014 World Cup final and the opening and closing ceremonies of the 2016 Olympics."],["The 2016 Olympics","In 2016 Rio hosted the Summer Olympics, the first in South America, followed by the Paralympics. The Games featured the first-ever Refugee Olympic Team. They brought new transport lines but also debate about costs and their lasting benefits for residents."],["Afro-Brazilian heritage","Rio was once a major port of the transatlantic slave trade. The Valongo Wharf, where hundreds of thousands of enslaved Africans landed, was rediscovered in 2011 and named a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 2017. Afro-Brazilian culture deeply shapes Rio’s music, religion, food and Carnival."]],
sp:["Capital of an empire","When Napoleon’s armies invaded Portugal in 1807, the Portuguese royal family boarded ships with thousands of courtiers and sailed to Brazil, arriving in Rio in 1808. Rio suddenly became the capital of a global empire, gaining a royal library, botanical garden, printing press and bank. In 1815 Brazil was raised to a kingdom equal to Portugal. When King João VI returned to Lisbon in 1821, his son Pedro stayed and in 1822 declared Brazil independent."],
g:[["Favela","An informal settlement in a Brazilian city"],["Samba","A Brazilian music and dance style"],["Carnival","A festival before Lent, with parades and parties"],["Bossa nova","A Brazilian music style mixing samba and jazz"]],
f:["Rio de Janeiro means River of January.","Christ the Redeemer was completed in 1931.","Rio hosted South America’s first Olympics in 2016."],
fun:"Rio was once the capital of the Portuguese Empire, the only European empire ruled from the Americas.",
tl:"Ang Rio de Janeiro ay lungsod sa Brazil na kilala sa mga dalampasigan, kabundukan at estatwa ni Kristo na Manunubos. Tanyag ang Carnival nito. Ito ang kabisera ng Brazil hanggang 1960."});

