/* data/history4.js */
// History articles, part 4: human origins, the Stone Age, the Philippine–American War, the Enlightenment, Charlemagne and medieval Europe, the Khmer Empire.
ADDART({id:"humanorigins",t:"Human Origins",c:"History",i:"🦴",w:"Human evolution",r:["evo","dna","caveart","ethiopiacountry","kenyacountry"],
p:[["Where we came from","Humans evolved in Africa over millions of years from earlier ape-like ancestors."],["Many human species","For most of prehistory, several kinds of humans lived on Earth at the same time."],["Spreading across the world","Modern humans, Homo sapiens, spread out of Africa to every continent except Antarctica."]],
l:"Human origins is the story of how our species, Homo sapiens, evolved. Fossils, stone tools and DNA show that the human family tree began in Africa between about 6 and 7 million years ago, when our ancestors split from the lineage leading to chimpanzees. Over millions of years, many human species appeared, learned to walk upright, make tools and control fire. Homo sapiens arose in Africa about 300,000 years ago and later spread across the world, meeting and sometimes mixing with other humans such as Neanderthals. One of these other species, Homo luzonensis, lived in the Philippines.",
s:[["Our closest relatives","Humans are primates, closely related to chimpanzees and bonobos, with whom we share about 98 to 99 percent of our DNA. We did not evolve from chimpanzees; rather, humans and chimpanzees share a common ancestor that lived about 6 to 7 million years ago. Members of the human branch are called hominins."],["Walking upright","One of the earliest changes was walking on two legs. Australopithecus species, which lived in eastern and southern Africa from about 4 to 2 million years ago, walked upright but had small brains. The famous fossil Lucy, found in Ethiopia in 1974, is about 3.2 million years old. Footprints at Laetoli in Tanzania, 3.6 million years old, show upright walking."],["The genus Homo","The first members of our genus, Homo, appeared about 2.5 million years ago. Homo habilis made simple stone tools. Homo erectus, from about 1.9 million years ago, had a larger brain and body like ours, used fire and was the first human species to spread out of Africa, reaching as far as Georgia, China and Java."],["Homo sapiens","Fossils from Jebel Irhoud in Morocco show that Homo sapiens existed about 300,000 years ago. Modern humans developed art, ornaments, complex tools and probably language. From about 60,000 to 70,000 years ago, groups spread out of Africa, reaching Asia, Australia, Europe and eventually the Americas."]],
k:[["Split from chimpanzee lineage","About 6–7 million years ago"],["Famous early fossil","Lucy, about 3.2 million years old"],["First Homo species","About 2.5 million years ago"],["Homo sapiens","About 300,000 years ago"],["Philippine species","Homo luzonensis, announced 2019"]],
d:[["c. 7 million years ago","Sahelanthropus, a possible early hominin, lives in Chad"],["c. 3.2 million years ago","Lucy lives in Ethiopia"],["c. 1.9 million years ago","Homo erectus appears"],["c. 300,000 years ago","Homo sapiens lives in Africa"],["c. 40,000 years ago","Neanderthals disappear"],["2019","Homo luzonensis is announced from Callao Cave, Cagayan"]],
s2:[["Homo luzonensis","In Callao Cave in Cagayan, northern Luzon, archaeologists found teeth and bones of a small human species, announced in 2019 as Homo luzonensis. It lived at least 50,000 years ago and had a mix of ancient and more modern features, such as curved finger and toe bones suited for climbing. Its discovery showed that early humans somehow crossed the sea to Luzon."],["Neanderthals and Denisovans","Neanderthals lived in Europe and western Asia from about 400,000 to 40,000 years ago. They made tools, used fire, buried their dead and probably made art. Denisovans, known mainly from DNA, lived in Asia. Modern humans met and had children with both. Most people of non-African ancestry carry about 1 to 2 percent Neanderthal DNA, and some people in Southeast Asia and Oceania, including some Filipino groups, carry Denisovan DNA."],["The hobbit of Flores","In 2004 scientists announced the discovery of Homo floresiensis on the island of Flores in Indonesia, a tiny human about a meter tall, nicknamed the hobbit. It lived until about 50,000 years ago. Like Homo luzonensis, it shows that isolated islands could produce unusual human species."],["Fire and cooking","Early humans learned to control fire, perhaps a million years ago or more. Fire provided warmth, protection from predators and light, and cooking made food easier to chew and digest, which may have helped human brains grow larger. Gathering around fires may also have encouraged social life and storytelling."],["Reading ancient DNA","Since the 2000s, scientists have been able to recover DNA from fossils tens of thousands of years old. The Swedish geneticist Svante Pääbo led the sequencing of the Neanderthal genome and identified the Denisovans from a finger bone, winning the 2022 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine. Ancient DNA has transformed the study of human origins."],["Peopling the world","Modern humans reached Australia at least 50,000 years ago, crossing open sea. They reached Europe about 45,000 years ago and the Americas, probably by way of a land bridge from Siberia or along the coast, more than 15,000 years ago. The ancestors of many Filipinos arrived in waves over tens of thousands of years, including later Austronesian seafarers."]],
sp:["One human family","All living humans belong to a single species, Homo sapiens, and are remarkably similar genetically: any two people share about 99.9 percent of their DNA. Because humans lived longest in Africa, African populations hold the greatest genetic diversity. Differences in skin color and other features developed relatively recently as people adapted to different climates. Studies of DNA show that the idea of separate human races has no basis in biology."],
g:[["Hominin","A member of the human branch of the family tree"],["Fossil","Remains or traces of ancient life preserved in rock"],["Genus","A group of closely related species"],["Neanderthal","A human species that lived in Europe and western Asia"]],
f:["Humans and chimpanzees share a common ancestor.","Homo sapiens appeared about 300,000 years ago.","Homo luzonensis lived in the Philippines."],
fun:"Many people today carry a small amount of Neanderthal DNA.",
tl:"Nagmula ang tao sa Aprika sa loob ng milyon-milyong taon. Noong unang panahon, ilang uri ng tao ang sabay na nabuhay. Isa rito ang Homo luzonensis na natagpuan sa Callao Cave, Cagayan."});

ADDART({id:"stoneage",t:"The Stone Age",c:"History",i:"🪓",w:"Stone Age",r:["humanorigins","caveart","agri","metals","banaue"],
p:[["Tools of stone","The Stone Age is the long period of prehistory when people made tools mainly from stone."],["Hunters to farmers","It began with hunter-gatherers and ended after people learned to farm and build villages."],["The longest age","It lasted more than three million years, over 99 percent of human history."]],
l:"The Stone Age is the broad period of prehistory during which humans and their ancestors made tools mainly from stone, along with wood, bone and other materials. It began over 3 million years ago with the first stone tools and lasted until people began working metals, at different times in different places, from about 3300 BCE in the Middle East. Archaeologists divide it into the Paleolithic, or Old Stone Age, the Mesolithic, or Middle Stone Age, and the Neolithic, or New Stone Age, when farming and settled villages began.",
s:[["The Old Stone Age","The Paleolithic began with the first stone tools, at least 2.6 million and possibly 3.3 million years ago, and lasted until about 12,000 years ago. People lived as hunter-gatherers, moving with the seasons to hunt animals and gather plants. Tools developed from simple sharp flakes to carefully shaped hand axes, spear points, needles and harpoons."],["Art and belief","Late in the Paleolithic, people created stunning cave paintings, carved figurines, made jewelry from shells and teeth and buried their dead with care. These show imagination, symbolism and possibly religious beliefs. Cave paintings in Indonesia and Spain are among the oldest known, over 40,000 years old."],["The New Stone Age","The Neolithic began about 12,000 years ago in the Middle East, when people began to farm crops and keep animals. Farming developed independently in several regions, including China, New Guinea, Mesoamerica and the Andes. People built permanent villages, made pottery and polished stone tools. This change is called the Neolithic Revolution."],["The end of the Stone Age","The Stone Age ended when people began to use metals: first copper, then bronze, an alloy of copper and tin, and later iron. This happened around 3300 BCE in the Middle East and later elsewhere. Some societies continued to use stone tools much longer, and the Stone Age did not end at the same time everywhere."]],
k:[["Main periods","Paleolithic, Mesolithic, Neolithic"],["Oldest stone tools","About 3.3 million years old, Kenya"],["Farming begins","About 12,000 years ago"],["Followed by","Copper and Bronze Ages"],["Philippine site","Tabon Caves, Palawan"]],
d:[["c. 3.3 million years ago","The oldest known stone tools are made in Kenya"],["c. 1.76 million years ago","Hand axes appear"],["c. 9500 BCE","Göbekli Tepe is built in Türkiye"],["c. 7400 BCE","Çatalhöyük, an early town, grows in Anatolia"],["c. 3000 BCE","Construction of Stonehenge begins"],["1991","Ötzi, a 5,300-year-old mummy, is found in the Alps"]],
s2:[["Göbekli Tepe","Göbekli Tepe, in southeastern Türkiye, is a site of huge carved stone pillars arranged in circles, built about 11,500 years ago, before farming was well established. The pillars are decorated with foxes, snakes, boars and birds. It surprised archaeologists by showing that hunter-gatherers could organize large building projects, perhaps for gatherings or rituals."],["Ötzi the Iceman","In 1991 hikers in the Alps between Austria and Italy found the frozen body of a man who died about 5,300 years ago. Called Ötzi, he carried a copper axe, a bow, arrows, a fire-making kit and medicinal mushrooms, and wore clothes of leather and grass. An arrowhead in his shoulder showed that he was killed. He is one of the best-preserved prehistoric humans."],["Stone Age Philippines","The Tabon Caves in Palawan contain some of the oldest human remains in the Philippines, with fossils dated to tens of thousands of years ago, as well as stone tools and burial jars. The Manunggul Jar, a burial jar from about 800 BCE with a lid showing two figures in a boat of souls, is a national treasure. The Angono Petroglyphs in Rizal are carvings made in prehistoric times."],["Stonehenge","Stonehenge, in southern England, is a circle of huge standing stones built in stages from about 3000 to 2000 BCE. Some stones were brought from Wales, over 200 kilometers away. It is aligned with the sunrise at midsummer and sunset at midwinter. Its exact purpose is still debated."],["Life in a Neolithic village","At Çatalhöyük, in Anatolia, thousands of people lived in mud-brick houses packed so closely that they entered through holes in the roofs. They farmed wheat and barley, herded sheep and cattle, painted walls with hunting scenes and buried their dead beneath their floors."],["How we know","Archaeologists learn about the Stone Age by excavating sites, studying tools, bones, seeds and pollen, and using dating methods such as radiocarbon dating. Experimental archaeologists try making stone tools themselves to understand how they were made and used."]],
sp:["The first tools","At a site called Lomekwi 3 in Kenya, scientists announced in 2015 the discovery of stone tools about 3.3 million years old, older than any known member of our genus Homo. Some were large cores and flakes made by striking rocks together. They suggest that earlier hominins, perhaps related to Lucy’s species, were already making tools. Toolmaking was a key step in human evolution."],
g:[["Prehistory","The time before written records"],["Paleolithic","The Old Stone Age"],["Neolithic","The New Stone Age, when farming began"],["Hunter-gatherer","A person who lives by hunting and gathering wild food"]],
f:["The Stone Age lasted more than three million years.","Farming began in the Neolithic.","Ötzi the Iceman is about 5,300 years old."],
fun:"Ötzi the Iceman carried a first-aid kit of medicinal mushrooms.",
tl:"Ang Panahon ng Bato ay mahabang panahon ng prehistorya kung kailan gumamit ang tao ng kasangkapang bato. Nagsimula ito sa mga mangangaso at natapos nang matuto ang tao na magsaka. Sa Pilipinas, mahalaga ang Tabon Caves sa Palawan."});

ADDART({id:"phamwar",t:"The Philippine–American War",c:"History",i:"⚔️",w:"Philippine–American War",r:["phrev","malolos","spanph","commonwealth","aguinaldo"],
p:[["A war for independence","The Philippine–American War was fought from 1899 between the First Philippine Republic and the United States."],["After Spain","It began after Spain ceded the Philippines to the United States in 1898."],["A long struggle","Organized fighting ended in 1902, but resistance continued for years in some regions."]],
l:"The Philippine–American War was an armed conflict between the First Philippine Republic and the United States that began on 4 February 1899. After the Spanish–American War, Spain ceded the Philippines to the United States in the Treaty of Paris of 1898, even though Filipino revolutionaries had already declared independence. Filipinos fought to defend their republic, first in conventional battles and then through guerrilla warfare. The United States declared the war over in 1902 after the capture of Emilio Aguinaldo, but resistance continued in some areas for years. The war cost many lives and led to decades of American colonial rule.",
s:[["Causes","Filipino revolutionaries had fought Spain since 1896 and declared independence on 12 June 1898. Under the Treaty of Paris, signed on 10 December 1898, Spain ceded the Philippines to the United States for 20 million dollars. The First Philippine Republic was proclaimed in Malolos on 23 January 1899. Tensions grew as American troops occupied Manila and Filipino forces surrounded it."],["Outbreak","On the night of 4 February 1899, American soldiers fired on Filipino soldiers near San Juan, starting fighting around Manila. American forces, better armed and supplied, pushed the Filipino army north, capturing Malolos in March 1899. General Antonio Luna, one of the ablest Filipino commanders, was assassinated by fellow Filipino soldiers in June 1899."],["Guerrilla war","From late 1899, Filipino forces turned to guerrilla warfare, attacking in small groups and relying on support from villages. At Tirad Pass in December 1899, the young general Gregorio del Pilar and his men fought a rearguard action to cover Aguinaldo’s retreat; del Pilar was killed. American forces responded with harsh measures, including burning villages and moving civilians into guarded zones."],["The end of the republic","On 23 March 1901, American forces led by Frederick Funston captured Emilio Aguinaldo in Palanan, Isabela, using a ruse. Aguinaldo swore allegiance to the United States and called on Filipinos to stop fighting. President Theodore Roosevelt declared the war over on 4 July 1902, though fighting continued, including resistance led by Macario Sakay until 1906 and conflicts in Mindanao and Sulu until 1913."]],
k:[["Began","4 February 1899"],["Declared over by the US","4 July 1902"],["Sides","First Philippine Republic and the United States"],["Filipino leader","Emilio Aguinaldo"],["American soldiers killed","About 4,200"]],
d:[["12 June 1898","Filipino revolutionaries declare independence"],["10 December 1898","The Treaty of Paris cedes the Philippines to the US"],["4 February 1899","Fighting breaks out near Manila"],["2 December 1899","The Battle of Tirad Pass"],["28 September 1901","Filipino fighters attack US troops at Balangiga, Samar"],["4 July 1902","The US declares the war over"]],
s2:[["The human cost","About 4,200 American soldiers died, and estimates of Filipino soldiers killed are around 20,000. Many more Filipino civilians died from violence, famine and diseases such as cholera, worsened by the war; estimates commonly start at about 200,000, and some historians give much higher figures. The war left deep scars in many communities."],["Balangiga","On 28 September 1901, townspeople and fighters in Balangiga, Samar, attacked an American garrison, killing about 48 soldiers. In retaliation, American forces under General Jacob Smith launched a brutal campaign on Samar, ordering the area turned into a howling wilderness. US soldiers took three church bells from Balangiga as trophies. After a long campaign by Filipinos, the bells were returned to the Philippines in December 2018."],["Debate in America","The war divided Americans. Supporters argued that the United States had a duty or interest in governing the islands. The Anti-Imperialist League, whose members included the writer Mark Twain, opposed the war and annexation, arguing it contradicted American ideals of self-government. Reports of abuses led to Senate hearings in 1902."],["Heroes of the resistance","Besides Aguinaldo, Luna and del Pilar, leaders such as Miguel Malvar in Batangas, Vicente Lukbán in Samar and Macario Sakay, who led a resistance government until 1906, are remembered as heroes. Sakay surrendered under a promise of amnesty but was hanged in 1907. Many ordinary Filipinos, including women, supported the fighters."],["American colonial rule","After the war, the United States governed the Philippines as a colony. It set up a civil government, public schools teaching in English, staffed at first by American teachers called Thomasites, and a legislature. Filipinos continued to press for independence, which was promised in 1934 and granted on 4 July 1946, after the Commonwealth period and World War II."],["Remembering the war","For many years, the war was little discussed in American textbooks and sometimes called the Philippine Insurrection. Filipino historians and writers have worked to restore its memory as a war for independence. Today it is studied in both countries as an important and painful chapter in their shared history."]],
sp:["The Battle of Tirad Pass","On 2 December 1899, General Gregorio del Pilar, just 24 years old, held the narrow Tirad Pass in the mountains of Ilocos Sur with about 60 men against a much larger American force, to give Emilio Aguinaldo time to escape. After hours of fighting, the Americans found a path around the Filipino position, and del Pilar was killed along with most of his men. He became known as the hero of Tirad Pass, a symbol of youthful sacrifice."],
g:[["Cede","To give up territory, usually by treaty"],["Guerrilla warfare","Fighting in small, mobile groups using surprise attacks"],["Annexation","Taking over a territory and making it part of a country"],["Anti-imperialist","Opposed to building an empire by ruling other lands"]],
f:["The war began on 4 February 1899.","Aguinaldo was captured in Palanan, Isabela, in 1901.","General Gregorio del Pilar died at Tirad Pass aged 24."],
fun:"The Balangiga bells, taken as trophies in 1901, returned to the Philippines in 2018.",
tl:"Ang Digmaang Pilipino–Amerikano ay nagsimula noong 4 Pebrero 1899 sa pagitan ng Unang Republika ng Pilipinas at Estados Unidos. Ipinaglaban ng mga Pilipino ang kanilang kasarinlan. Idineklarang tapos ito ng Amerika noong 1902, ngunit nagpatuloy ang paglaban sa ilang lugar."});

ADDART({id:"enlightenment",t:"The Enlightenment",c:"History",i:"💡",w:"Age of Enlightenment",r:["scimeth","frrev","usrev","hrights","democ"],
p:[["The Age of Reason","The Enlightenment was a movement of ideas in the 1600s and 1700s that emphasized reason, science and individual rights."],["Questioning authority","Thinkers challenged the power of kings and churches and argued for freedom and equality."],["Changing the world","Enlightenment ideas inspired revolutions and modern democracy, human rights and science."]],
l:"The Enlightenment was an intellectual and cultural movement that flourished in Europe and the Americas from the late 1600s through the 1700s. Its thinkers believed that reason, observation and science could improve human understanding and society. They questioned traditional authority, argued for religious tolerance, freedom of speech, equality before the law and government by consent of the governed. Enlightenment ideas influenced the American and French revolutions, the abolition of slavery and later independence movements, including those in Latin America and the Philippines.",
s:[["Roots","The Enlightenment grew out of the Scientific Revolution. Isaac Newton’s discovery that simple laws governed the motion of planets and falling objects suggested that reason could uncover the laws of nature, and perhaps of society too. Philosophers such as René Descartes and Francis Bacon emphasized doubt, reasoning and experiment."],["Government by consent","The English philosopher John Locke argued in 1689 that people have natural rights to life, liberty and property, and that governments exist to protect these rights; if they fail, people may replace them. The French thinker Montesquieu proposed separating government powers. Jean-Jacques Rousseau wrote of the social contract and the general will."],["Tolerance and free speech","Voltaire, a French writer, used wit to attack religious intolerance, censorship and injustice. Enlightenment thinkers called for freedom of religion and of the press. Coffeehouses, salons, newspapers and academies spread these ideas among educated people."],["The Encyclopédie","Between 1751 and 1772, Denis Diderot and Jean le Rond d’Alembert published the Encyclopédie in France, a huge work summarizing knowledge in science, arts and crafts, written by many contributors. It aimed to change the way people think and was censored by authorities. It inspired later encyclopedias."]],
k:[["Period","Late 1600s to late 1700s"],["Key ideas","Reason, science, rights, tolerance"],["Key thinkers","Locke, Voltaire, Montesquieu, Rousseau, Kant, Smith"],["Famous work","The Encyclopédie (1751–1772)"],["Motto (Kant)","Dare to know"]],
d:[["1687","Newton publishes his Principia"],["1689","Locke publishes Two Treatises of Government"],["1748","Montesquieu publishes The Spirit of the Laws"],["1751","The first volume of the Encyclopédie appears"],["1776","Adam Smith publishes The Wealth of Nations; the US Declaration of Independence"],["1784","Kant publishes What Is Enlightenment?"]],
s2:[["Dare to know","In 1784 the German philosopher Immanuel Kant answered the question What is Enlightenment? He wrote that it was humanity’s escape from immaturity, the inability to use one’s own understanding without guidance from others. Its motto, he said, was sapere aude, dare to know, have the courage to use your own reason."],["Economics","The Scottish thinker Adam Smith, in The Wealth of Nations in 1776, argued that free markets, competition and the division of labor could create wealth, guided as if by an invisible hand. The Scottish Enlightenment, including David Hume, made Edinburgh a center of ideas in philosophy, science and economics."],["Women and the Enlightenment","Women hosted salons where ideas were debated, and some, like Émilie du Châtelet, who translated Newton into French, made scientific contributions. In 1792 Mary Wollstonecraft published A Vindication of the Rights of Woman, arguing that women deserved equal education and rights. Many male thinkers, however, still excluded women from equality."],["Limits and contradictions","Enlightenment ideals of liberty and equality were often not applied to everyone. Some thinkers defended or ignored slavery and colonialism, and some held racist views. Yet others used Enlightenment arguments to campaign against slavery, and enslaved people in Haiti fought for the rights proclaimed in the French Revolution."],["Enlightenment ideas in the Philippines","In the 1800s, Enlightenment and liberal ideas reached the Philippines, especially through Filipino students in Europe, the ilustrados. José Rizal, Marcelo H. del Pilar and others drew on ideas of reason, education, equality and rights to call for reform under Spanish rule. These ideas helped shape the Philippine Revolution and the Malolos Constitution."],["Legacy","Many features of the modern world, such as constitutions, human rights, freedom of expression, separation of church and state, public education and the scientific method, have roots in the Enlightenment. Critics, then and now, have argued that reason alone cannot answer every human question, and debate about its legacy continues."]],
sp:["An encyclopedia that changed minds","The Encyclopédie, edited by Denis Diderot, eventually filled 28 volumes with more than 70,000 articles and thousands of illustrations of crafts and machines. Its contributors included Voltaire, Rousseau and Montesquieu. By giving detailed attention to the work of ordinary craftspeople and questioning religious and political authority, it spread Enlightenment ideas across Europe. French authorities banned it more than once, but publication continued in secret."],
g:[["Reason","The power of the mind to think logically"],["Natural rights","Rights that all people have by nature"],["Social contract","An agreement by which people form a society and government"],["Salon","A gathering in a private home for discussion"]],
f:["The Enlightenment emphasized reason and rights.","Diderot edited the Encyclopédie.","Kant’s motto for the Enlightenment was dare to know."],
fun:"The Encyclopédie was banned by French authorities but kept being published in secret.",
tl:"Ang Enlightenment ay kilusan ng mga ideya noong 1600s at 1700s na nagbigay-diin sa katwiran, agham at karapatan ng tao. Nakaimpluwensiya ito sa mga rebolusyon at sa mga ilustrado gaya ni José Rizal."});

ADDART({id:"medieurope",t:"Charlemagne and Medieval Europe",c:"History",i:"🏰",w:"Feudalism",r:["gothic","crusades","viking","byzant","renais"],
p:[["Lords, knights and peasants","Medieval Europe was organized around lords, knights, peasants and the Church."],["Charlemagne","In 800, Charlemagne was crowned emperor, uniting much of western Europe."],["Castles and cathedrals","The Middle Ages produced castles, universities, cathedrals and lasting laws."]],
l:"The Middle Ages, or medieval period, in Europe lasted roughly from the fall of the Western Roman Empire in 476 to about 1500. In western Europe, society was shaped by the Christian Church, local lords and a system historians often call feudalism, in which land was exchanged for loyalty and military service. Charlemagne, crowned emperor in 800, briefly united much of the region. The period saw castles and knights, but also growing towns, trade, universities, Gothic cathedrals and early forms of parliament.",
s:[["The emperor of the West","Charlemagne, king of the Franks, conquered much of western and central Europe, from northern Spain to Germany and Italy. On Christmas Day 800, Pope Leo III crowned him emperor in Rome. Charlemagne supported learning, gathering scholars at his court; this revival of education and copying of books is called the Carolingian Renaissance."],["Lords and vassals","After Charlemagne’s empire divided, power became local. Kings granted land, called fiefs, to nobles in return for loyalty and military service. These vassals in turn granted land to knights. Historians debate the word feudalism, since arrangements varied widely across Europe, but such ties of land and loyalty were common."],["Life on the manor","Most people were peasants who farmed land on manors owned by lords. Many were serfs, bound to the land and owing labor and payments to their lord. Villages centered on the church, and life followed the farming seasons and religious festivals. Life was hard, and famine and disease were constant dangers."],["The Church","The Catholic Church was the most powerful institution in western Europe. It ran monasteries, schools and hospitals, owned large estates and influenced kings. Monks copied books and preserved learning. In the east, the Byzantine Empire and Orthodox Christianity continued Roman traditions."]],
k:[["Period","About 476 to 1500"],["Charlemagne crowned","25 December 800"],["Key system","Feudalism (land for loyalty)"],["Most people","Peasants, many of them serfs"],["Holy Roman Empire","962–1806"]],
d:[["476","The Western Roman Empire falls"],["800","Charlemagne is crowned emperor"],["962","Otto I founds the Holy Roman Empire"],["1088","The University of Bologna is founded"],["1215","Magna Carta is sealed in England"],["1337–1453","The Hundred Years’ War between England and France"]],
s2:[["Knights and chivalry","Knights were mounted warriors who served lords and kings. They trained from childhood as pages and squires. Chivalry was a code of conduct that expected knights to be brave, loyal, generous and protective of the weak, though real knights often fell short. Tournaments let knights show off their skills."],["Castles","Castles were fortified homes for lords, built first of wood and earth and later of stone, with thick walls, towers, moats and gatehouses. They protected against attack and controlled the surrounding land. As cannons improved in the late Middle Ages, castles became less useful for defense."],["Towns and trade","From about 1000, farming improved, populations grew and towns expanded. Merchants and craftspeople formed guilds to regulate trade and quality. Trade fairs and routes connected Europe with the Byzantine Empire, the Islamic world and Asia, bringing spices, silk and new ideas."],["Universities","Medieval Europe created the first universities: Bologna, founded around 1088, Paris and Oxford in the 1100s, and many others. Students studied law, medicine, theology and the liberal arts, often in Latin. Universities drew on knowledge preserved and expanded by scholars in the Islamic world."],["Joan of Arc","During the Hundred Years’ War, a peasant girl named Joan of Arc said that saints told her to help the French king drive out the English. In 1429 she helped lift the siege of Orléans. Captured and tried by pro-English church judges, she was burned at the stake in 1431 at age 19. She was later declared a saint."],["Were they dark ages?","The early Middle Ages were once called the Dark Ages, suggesting a time of ignorance. Historians now reject this view, pointing to medieval achievements in architecture, art, music, philosophy, law and technology, such as the heavy plow, windmills, mechanical clocks and eyeglasses."]],
sp:["The crowning of Charlemagne","On Christmas Day in the year 800, as Charlemagne knelt in prayer at Old St Peter’s Basilica in Rome, Pope Leo III placed a crown on his head and proclaimed him emperor of the Romans. It was the first time in over 300 years that western Europe had an emperor. The event linked the Frankish kingdom, the papacy and the memory of Rome, and shaped European politics for centuries."],
g:[["Feudalism","A system of land granted in return for loyalty and service"],["Vassal","A person who held land from a lord in exchange for loyalty"],["Serf","A peasant bound to work a lord’s land"],["Guild","An association of merchants or craftspeople"]],
f:["Charlemagne was crowned emperor in 800.","The University of Bologna dates from about 1088.","Joan of Arc helped lift the siege of Orléans in 1429."],
fun:"Medieval Europe invented eyeglasses and mechanical clocks.",
tl:"Ang Middle Ages sa Europa ay mula 476 hanggang mga 1500. Hinubog ang lipunan ng Simbahan, mga panginoon at sistemang piyudal. Kinoronahan si Charlemagne bilang emperador noong 800."});

ADDART({id:"khmer",t:"The Khmer Empire",c:"History",i:"🛕",w:"Khmer Empire",r:["angkor","seaempires","buddh","hindu","sea"],
p:[["Builders of Angkor","The Khmer Empire ruled much of mainland Southeast Asia from the 800s to the 1400s."],["Temples and reservoirs","Its capital, Angkor, had magnificent temples and huge reservoirs for water."],["A great city","Angkor was one of the largest cities of the pre-industrial world."]],
l:"The Khmer Empire was a powerful state centered in present-day Cambodia that flourished from 802 until the 1400s. At its height, it controlled much of mainland Southeast Asia, including parts of present-day Thailand, Laos and Vietnam. Its capital region, Angkor, contained hundreds of temples, including Angkor Wat, the largest religious monument in the world, and a vast network of canals and reservoirs. The empire’s kings blended Hindu and Buddhist beliefs, and its art and architecture remain among the greatest achievements of Asia.",
s:[["Founding","In 802 King Jayavarman II proclaimed himself a universal monarch and god-king on the sacred mountain of Phnom Kulen, declaring independence from Java and uniting Khmer lands. His successors built capitals near the Tonlé Sap, the great lake of Cambodia, eventually centered at Angkor."],["Angkor Wat","King Suryavarman II built Angkor Wat in the early 1100s as a temple to the Hindu god Vishnu and possibly his tomb. Its five towers represent Mount Meru, the home of the gods, and its walls are carved with long reliefs of Hindu epics, battles and heavenly dancers. It later became a Buddhist temple and is a symbol of Cambodia, appearing on its flag."],["Jayavarman VII","Jayavarman VII, who ruled from about 1181 to around 1218, drove out invaders from Champa and expanded the empire. A devout Buddhist, he built the city of Angkor Thom, with the Bayon temple famous for its giant smiling stone faces. He also built hospitals and rest houses along roads across the empire."],["Water and rice","Angkor’s rulers built enormous reservoirs called barays, canals and moats to manage water from the monsoon rains. The West Baray is about 8 kilometers long. This system helped supply water for the city and may have supported rice farming and religious ceremonies."]],
k:[["Period","802 to the 1400s"],["Capital","Angkor"],["Most famous temple","Angkor Wat (early 1100s)"],["Religions","Hinduism and Buddhism"],["World Heritage Site","Angkor, 1992"]],
d:[["802","Jayavarman II founds the Khmer Empire"],["c. 1113–1150","Suryavarman II builds Angkor Wat"],["1181","Jayavarman VII becomes king"],["1296–1297","Chinese envoy Zhou Daguan visits Angkor"],["1431","Ayutthaya forces sack Angkor"],["1992","Angkor becomes a UNESCO World Heritage Site"]],
s2:[["A giant city","Laser scanning from aircraft, called lidar, used in the 2010s revealed that Angkor was surrounded by a huge urban landscape of roads, canals, houses and fields stretching over about 1,000 square kilometers, making it one of the largest settlements of the pre-industrial world. Most houses were made of wood and have disappeared."],["Zhou Daguan’s account","In 1296 and 1297, the Chinese envoy Zhou Daguan stayed at Angkor and later wrote a description of the city, its king, festivals, markets and daily life. He described golden towers, royal processions with elephants and women traders in the markets. His account is the only detailed eyewitness description of Angkor at its height."],["Decline","From the 1300s, the empire weakened. Wars with the Thai kingdom of Ayutthaya, which sacked Angkor in 1431, changes in trade and religion, and long droughts followed by intense monsoons that damaged the water system may all have contributed. The Khmer court moved south toward Phnom Penh, closer to sea trade."],["Art and dance","Khmer sculptors created graceful statues of gods, Buddhas and kings, and temple walls show thousands of apsaras, heavenly dancers. Cambodia’s classical dance, recognized by UNESCO, preserves movements seen in these carvings. Many statues were looted during periods of war and are now being returned to Cambodia."],["Rediscovery and restoration","Angkor Wat was never fully abandoned, as Buddhist monks continued to use it. In the 1860s, the French explorer Henri Mouhot’s descriptions drew European attention. Since the 1990s, international teams and Cambodian experts have worked to restore temples damaged by time, jungle and war."],["Angkor today","Angkor attracts millions of visitors each year. Sunrise over Angkor Wat is one of the world’s most famous sights. The Ta Prohm temple, where giant tree roots grip the ruins, has been left partly in its jungle state. Tourism supports the local economy but also brings challenges of conservation."]],
sp:["The faces of the Bayon","The Bayon temple, at the heart of Angkor Thom, has more than 200 giant stone faces carved on its towers, gazing calmly in all directions with gentle smiles. They may represent the bodhisattva Avalokiteshvara, a figure of compassion, or King Jayavarman VII himself, or both. The temple’s walls also show scenes of daily life, markets, fishing and battles with the Cham, giving a vivid picture of the Khmer world."],
g:[["Baray","A large reservoir built by the Khmer"],["God-king","A ruler believed to be divine or linked to the gods"],["Lidar","Laser scanning used to map the ground"],["Apsara","A heavenly dancer in Hindu and Buddhist art"]],
f:["Angkor Wat is the largest religious monument in the world.","Angkor Wat appears on Cambodia’s flag.","The Bayon has more than 200 stone faces."],
fun:"Angkor Wat appears on the national flag of Cambodia.",
tl:"Ang Imperyong Khmer ay namuno sa malaking bahagi ng Timog-silangang Asya mula 800s hanggang 1400s. Itinayo nito ang Angkor Wat, ang pinakamalaking relihiyosong monumento sa mundo."});

