// History articles, part 5: the Opium Wars, the colonization of Africa, the American Civil War, China since 1949, the modern Middle East, terrorism after 2001.
ADDART({id:"opium",t:"The Opium Wars",c:"History",i:"⛴️",w:"Opium Wars",r:["china","trade","indiacountry","chinacountry","meiji"],
p:[["Wars over trade","The Opium Wars were two wars in the 1800s in which Britain, and later France, fought China over trade."],["A dangerous drug","Britain sold opium grown in India to China, causing widespread addiction."],["Unequal treaties","China lost, and was forced to sign treaties that gave foreign powers special rights, including Hong Kong."]],
l:"The Opium Wars were two conflicts between China’s Qing dynasty and Western powers: the First Opium War from 1839 to 1842 with Britain, and the Second Opium War from 1856 to 1860 with Britain and France. They grew out of British merchants’ trade in opium, grown in British-ruled India and smuggled into China, which caused addiction and drained China’s silver. China’s defeats forced it to open ports, give up Hong Kong and accept unequal treaties. In China, the wars are remembered as the start of a century of humiliation by foreign powers.",
s:[["The opium trade","In the 1700s and 1800s, Britain bought huge amounts of Chinese tea, silk and porcelain, but China wanted few British goods, so silver flowed into China. To reverse this, British merchants, especially the East India Company and its agents, sold opium grown in India to Chinese smugglers. By the 1830s, millions of Chinese were addicted, and silver was flowing out of China."],["Lin Zexu","The Qing emperor sent the official Lin Zexu to Guangzhou, then called Canton, to stop the trade. In 1839 he seized and destroyed more than 1,000 metric tons of opium at Humen, mixing it with lime and salt and flushing it into the sea. He also wrote to Queen Victoria asking how Britain would feel if another country sold poison to its people."],["The First Opium War","Britain responded by sending warships. Its steam-powered gunboats and modern weapons defeated Chinese forces along the coast and rivers. In the Treaty of Nanjing of 1842, China paid a large indemnity, ceded Hong Kong Island to Britain and opened five ports, including Shanghai, to British trade and residents."],["The Second Opium War","In 1856 Britain and France went to war with China again, seeking more trade rights. In 1860 their troops reached Beijing and looted and burned the Old Summer Palace, a vast complex of palaces and gardens. New treaties opened more ports, legalized the opium trade, allowed Christian missionaries to travel and gave Britain the Kowloon Peninsula."]],
k:[["First Opium War","1839–1842"],["Second Opium War","1856–1860"],["Chinese dynasty","Qing"],["Key treaty","Treaty of Nanjing, 1842"],["Hong Kong returned","1997"]],
d:[["1839","Lin Zexu destroys opium at Humen"],["1842","The Treaty of Nanjing ends the First Opium War"],["1850–1864","The Taiping Rebellion devastates China"],["1856","The Second Opium War begins"],["1860","British and French troops burn the Old Summer Palace"],["1997","Hong Kong is returned to China"]],
s2:[["Lasting privileges","The treaties forced on China gave foreigners extraterritoriality, meaning they were judged by their own countries’ laws rather than China’s, and let foreign powers control tariffs and set up concessions in Chinese cities. Japan and other powers later gained similar privileges. These unequal treaties were not fully abolished until the 1940s."],["The century of humiliation","Chinese historians describe the period from the First Opium War to the founding of the People’s Republic in 1949 as the century of humiliation, marked by defeats, foreign occupation and loss of territory. This memory continues to shape Chinese views of the West and national pride."],["The Taiping Rebellion","While China fought foreign powers, it faced a far deadlier internal conflict. The Taiping Rebellion, led by Hong Xiuquan, who believed he was the brother of Jesus, raged from 1850 to 1864 and killed perhaps 20 to 30 million people, one of the deadliest wars in history. It further weakened the Qing dynasty."],["Hong Kong","Hong Kong grew from a small island into a great trading port under British rule. In 1898 Britain leased the New Territories for 99 years. In 1997 Britain returned Hong Kong to China under the principle of one country, two systems, which promised it a high degree of autonomy. How this has been applied has been a subject of controversy."],["Japan takes note","Japan watched China’s defeats with alarm. When American warships arrived in 1853 demanding trade, Japan eventually chose to modernize rapidly, leading to the Meiji Restoration of 1868, so that it would not suffer China’s fate."],["Legacy of opium","Opium addiction remained a serious problem in China into the 20th century. Lin Zexu became a national hero, and 3 June, the anniversary of his destruction of opium, is remembered in campaigns against drugs. The International Day against Drug Abuse on 26 June also honors his action."]],
sp:["Lin Zexu’s letter","In 1839 Lin Zexu wrote a letter to Queen Victoria. He pointed out that opium was forbidden in Britain and asked why British merchants should sell it in China. Let us suppose that foreigners came from another country and brought opium into England, he wrote; would you not be greatly angered? It is not known whether the queen ever read the letter, but it became a famous statement of China’s case."],
g:[["Opium","An addictive drug made from poppies"],["Indemnity","A payment demanded as compensation"],["Treaty port","A port opened to foreign trade by treaty"],["Extraterritoriality","Exemption from local laws for foreigners"]],
f:["The First Opium War ended with the Treaty of Nanjing in 1842.","Hong Kong was returned to China in 1997.","Lin Zexu destroyed over 1,000 metric tons of opium."],
fun:"Lin Zexu wrote a letter to Queen Victoria asking Britain to stop selling opium to China.",
tl:"Ang Opium Wars ay dalawang digmaan noong 1800s sa pagitan ng Tsina at Britanya, at kalaunan ng Pransiya. Ibinenta ng Britanya ang opyo sa Tsina. Natalo ang Tsina at napilitang ibigay ang Hong Kong at buksan ang mga daungan."});

ADDART({id:"africacolon",t:"The Colonization of Africa",c:"History",i:"🌍",w:"Scramble for Africa",r:["decol","slavetrade","ethiopiacountry","drccountry","nkrumah"],
p:[["The Scramble for Africa","Between about 1880 and 1914, European powers divided almost all of Africa among themselves."],["Borders drawn in Europe","At the Berlin Conference of 1884–1885, European leaders set rules for claiming African land."],["Resistance","Africans resisted colonial rule in many ways, and Ethiopia defeated an Italian invasion."]],
l:"The colonization of Africa refers to the conquest and rule of most of the African continent by European powers, especially during the Scramble for Africa between about 1880 and 1914. Britain, France, Germany, Belgium, Portugal, Italy and Spain claimed territories, often drawing borders with little regard for African peoples. By 1914 only Ethiopia and Liberia remained independent. Colonial rule brought violence, forced labor and the exploitation of land and resources, as well as railways, schools and new borders that still shape Africa. Most African countries won independence in the 1950s and 1960s.",
s:[["Before the scramble","For centuries, Europeans had traded on Africa’s coasts, including in enslaved people, but had controlled little territory inland, apart from areas such as the Cape in South Africa and Algeria, conquered by France from 1830. Diseases such as malaria and strong African states limited European expansion."],["Why the scramble began","In the late 1800s, European powers competed for raw materials such as rubber, cotton, gold and diamonds, for markets and for national prestige. New technologies, including steamships, railways, machine guns and the malaria medicine quinine, made conquest easier. Some Europeans claimed a civilizing mission, an idea now seen as a justification for domination."],["The Berlin Conference","From November 1884 to February 1885, representatives of European powers and the United States met in Berlin, without any African representatives, to set rules for claiming African territory. Claims had to be backed by effective occupation. The conference recognized the Congo Free State as the personal possession of King Leopold II of Belgium."],["Colonial rule","Colonial governments ruled directly or through local leaders. Africans were forced to pay taxes, work on plantations, mines and roads, and grow cash crops for export. Land was taken for European settlers in places such as Kenya, Algeria and southern Africa. Missions and colonial governments set up some schools and hospitals, mostly serving limited groups."]],
k:[["Main period","About 1880–1914"],["Berlin Conference","1884–1885"],["Independent in 1914","Ethiopia and Liberia"],["Main colonial powers","Britain, France, Germany, Belgium, Portugal, Italy, Spain"],["Independence wave","1950s–1960s"]],
d:[["1830","France begins the conquest of Algeria"],["1884–1885","The Berlin Conference is held"],["1896","Ethiopia defeats Italy at the Battle of Adwa"],["1900","Yaa Asantewaa leads the Asante against the British"],["1904–1908","German forces carry out genocide against the Herero and Nama"],["1957","Ghana becomes independent"]],
s2:[["The Congo Free State","From 1885 to 1908, King Leopold II ruled the Congo Free State as his personal property. To extract rubber and ivory, his agents used forced labor, hostage-taking and terrible violence, including cutting off hands. Millions of Congolese died from violence, famine and disease. International outrage, led by campaigners such as E. D. Morel and Roger Casement, forced Belgium to take over the colony in 1908."],["Adwa","In 1896 Emperor Menelik II of Ethiopia defeated an invading Italian army at the Battle of Adwa, one of the greatest victories of an African state over a European power. Ethiopia kept its independence and became a symbol of African pride, and its colors inspired the flags of many later African nations."],["Fighting back","Africans resisted conquest in many ways. Samori Touré fought the French in West Africa for nearly two decades. The Asante queen mother Yaa Asantewaa led a war against the British in 1900. The Maji Maji rebellion in German East Africa and uprisings in Zimbabwe, Algeria and elsewhere showed widespread opposition. Others resisted through religion, strikes and politics."],["The Herero and Nama genocide","In German South West Africa, now Namibia, the Herero and Nama peoples rose against colonial rule. From 1904 to 1908, German forces killed tens of thousands of them, driving many into the desert and confining others in camps. It is considered the first genocide of the 20th century. Germany formally recognized it as genocide in 2021."],["Lasting effects","Colonial borders cut across ethnic groups and joined rivals, contributing to later conflicts. Economies were organized to export raw materials, a pattern that persisted after independence. European languages became official languages in many countries. Historians continue to study and debate colonialism’s long-term effects on African development."],["The road to independence","After World War II, African independence movements grew rapidly. Ghana, led by Kwame Nkrumah, became independent in 1957, and in 1960 alone 17 African countries gained independence. Some, such as Algeria, Kenya, Angola and Mozambique, won freedom only after long wars. By the 1990s, colonial rule had ended across the continent."]],
sp:["Africa divided on a map","At the Berlin Conference, European diplomats discussed Africa over maps, often of places they had never visited. Many borders were drawn as straight lines along lines of latitude and longitude or rivers, splitting peoples such as the Maasai between Kenya and Tanzania and the Ewe between Ghana and Togo. When African countries became independent, the Organisation of African Unity decided in 1964 to keep colonial borders to avoid endless disputes."],
g:[["Colony","A territory ruled by another country"],["Cash crop","A crop grown to sell rather than to eat"],["Genocide","The deliberate destruction of a people"],["Effective occupation","Actual control of a territory, required for claims at Berlin"]],
f:["By 1914 only Ethiopia and Liberia were independent in Africa.","Ethiopia defeated Italy at Adwa in 1896.","In 1960 alone, 17 African countries gained independence."],
fun:"Ethiopia’s colors inspired the flags of many African countries.",
tl:"Ang kolonisasyon ng Aprika ay ang pananakop ng mga bansang Europeo sa halos buong Aprika mula 1880 hanggang 1914. Tanging Ethiopia at Liberia ang nanatiling malaya. Naging malaya ang karamihan ng bansang Aprikano noong 1950s at 1960s."});

ADDART({id:"uscivilwar",t:"The American Civil War",c:"History",i:"🎖️",w:"American Civil War",r:["lincoln","tubman","slavetrade","usacountry","mlk"],
p:[["A nation divided","The American Civil War was fought from 1861 to 1865 between the United States and 11 Southern states that had broken away."],["Slavery at its heart","The main cause was the conflict over slavery and its spread into new territories."],["Freedom for millions","The Union won, the country stayed united and slavery was abolished."]],
l:"The American Civil War was a conflict in the United States from 1861 to 1865 between the Union, the northern states that remained loyal to the federal government, and the Confederate States of America, formed by 11 southern states that seceded. The central cause was slavery: southern states, whose economies relied on the labor of nearly four million enslaved African Americans, feared that the federal government would restrict or end it. The war was the deadliest in American history. The Union victory preserved the nation and led to the abolition of slavery.",
s:[["Causes","By the mid-1800s, the North had growing industries and free labor, while the South depended on plantations worked by enslaved people. Bitter disputes arose over whether new western territories would allow slavery. When Abraham Lincoln, who opposed the expansion of slavery, was elected president in 1860, southern states began to secede, arguing that states had the right to leave the Union."],["The war begins","On 12 April 1861, Confederate forces attacked Fort Sumter in South Carolina. Both sides expected a short war, but it became a long and bloody struggle. The Confederacy, led by President Jefferson Davis, had skilled generals such as Robert E. Lee, while the Union had more people, factories and railways."],["Turning points","In 1863 the Union won crucial victories at Gettysburg in Pennsylvania and Vicksburg on the Mississippi River. General Ulysses S. Grant took command of Union armies, and General William T. Sherman marched through Georgia in 1864, destroying railways and supplies. On 9 April 1865, Lee surrendered to Grant at Appomattox Court House in Virginia."],["Emancipation","On 1 January 1863, Lincoln issued the Emancipation Proclamation, declaring enslaved people in Confederate-held areas free. About 180,000 Black soldiers fought for the Union. The 13th Amendment to the Constitution, ratified in December 1865, abolished slavery throughout the United States."]],
k:[["Years","1861–1865"],["Sides","Union and Confederacy"],["Main cause","Slavery and its expansion"],["Deaths","About 620,000–750,000"],["Slavery abolished","13th Amendment, 1865"]],
d:[["1860","Abraham Lincoln is elected president"],["12 April 1861","Confederates attack Fort Sumter"],["1 January 1863","The Emancipation Proclamation takes effect"],["July 1863","Union victories at Gettysburg and Vicksburg"],["9 April 1865","Lee surrenders at Appomattox"],["December 1865","The 13th Amendment abolishes slavery"]],
s2:[["The Gettysburg Address","On 19 November 1863, at the dedication of a cemetery at Gettysburg, Lincoln gave a speech of only about 270 words. He described the nation as conceived in liberty and dedicated to the proposition that all men are created equal, and called for a new birth of freedom, so that government of the people, by the people, for the people, shall not perish from the earth. It is one of the most famous speeches in history."],["The human cost","Between about 620,000 and 750,000 soldiers died, more than in any other American war, many from disease. Railways, telegraphs, rifles and ironclad warships made it one of the first industrial wars. Photographers such as Mathew Brady brought images of the battlefield dead to the public for the first time."],["Lincoln’s assassination","On 14 April 1865, just days after Lee’s surrender, Lincoln was shot by John Wilkes Booth, a Confederate sympathizer, while watching a play at Ford’s Theatre in Washington. He died the next morning, the first US president to be assassinated."],["Reconstruction","After the war, during Reconstruction from 1865 to 1877, the federal government worked to rebuild the South and protect the rights of freed people. The 14th Amendment granted citizenship and equal protection, and the 15th gave Black men the right to vote. Many Black Americans were elected to office. But violence and new laws later stripped many of these rights."],["After Reconstruction","After federal troops left the South in 1877, southern states passed Jim Crow laws enforcing racial segregation and blocking Black citizens from voting. These injustices lasted until the civil rights movement of the 1950s and 1960s, led by figures such as Martin Luther King Jr."],["Memory and debate","Americans have long debated how to remember the war. Some monuments to Confederate leaders were built decades later, during the Jim Crow era, and in recent years many have been removed. Historians agree that slavery was the central cause of the war, as Confederate leaders themselves stated at the time."]],
sp:["Harriet Tubman’s raid","Harriet Tubman, who had escaped slavery and guided many others to freedom on the Underground Railroad, served the Union army as a scout, spy and nurse. In June 1863 she helped lead the Combahee River Raid in South Carolina, guiding Union gunboats past hidden mines. The raid freed more than 700 enslaved people, making her one of the first women to help lead an armed military operation in American history."],
g:[["Secede","To formally leave a country or union"],["Union","The northern states loyal to the United States government"],["Confederacy","The southern states that seceded in 1860–1861"],["Emancipation","Being set free, especially from slavery"]],
f:["The Civil War lasted from 1861 to 1865.","The Emancipation Proclamation took effect on 1 January 1863.","The 13th Amendment abolished slavery in 1865."],
fun:"Lincoln’s Gettysburg Address was only about 270 words long.",
tl:"Ang Digmaang Sibil ng Amerika ay mula 1861 hanggang 1865 sa pagitan ng Union at ng mga estadong humiwalay sa Timog. Pangunahing sanhi nito ang pang-aalipin. Nanalo ang Union at inalis ang pang-aalipin sa Estados Unidos."});

ADDART({id:"modernchina",t:"China since 1949",c:"History",i:"🏮",w:"History of the People's Republic of China",r:["chinacountry","china","coldwar","koreanwar","trade"],
p:[["A new republic","The People’s Republic of China was founded in 1949 by the Communist Party under Mao Zedong."],["Upheavals","The Great Leap Forward and the Cultural Revolution brought famine and turmoil."],["Economic rise","Reforms from 1978 turned China into the world’s second-largest economy."]],
l:"The People’s Republic of China was proclaimed on 1 October 1949 by Mao Zedong, after the Communist Party won a long civil war against the Nationalist government, which retreated to Taiwan. Under Mao, China experienced radical campaigns, including the Great Leap Forward, which caused a devastating famine, and the Cultural Revolution. After Mao’s death in 1976, Deng Xiaoping launched economic reforms that opened China to the world and produced decades of rapid growth, lifting hundreds of millions of people out of poverty. Today China is a major global power, governed by the Communist Party.",
s:[["Founding","After decades of war with Japan and civil war, the Communist Party, led by Mao Zedong, defeated the Nationalists under Chiang Kai-shek. Mao proclaimed the People’s Republic in Beijing on 1 October 1949. The Nationalist government moved to Taiwan, where the Republic of China continued. The status of Taiwan remains a major international issue."],["The Mao years","The new government carried out land reform, taking land from landlords, and built industries with Soviet help. In 1958 Mao launched the Great Leap Forward to rapidly industrialize and collectivize farming. Unrealistic targets and policies led to a famine from 1959 to 1961 in which tens of millions of people died. In 1966 Mao launched the Cultural Revolution, in which young Red Guards attacked officials, teachers and traditional culture; it caused widespread suffering until 1976."],["Reform and opening","After Mao died in 1976, Deng Xiaoping became the leading figure. From 1978 he introduced market reforms, allowing farmers to sell surplus crops, encouraging private businesses and opening Special Economic Zones, such as Shenzhen, to foreign investment. China’s economy grew rapidly for decades, becoming the world’s factory."],["China today","China is the world’s second-largest economy and a leader in manufacturing, trade, infrastructure and technology, with high-speed railways, a space station and major tech companies. It is governed by the Chinese Communist Party, and Xi Jinping has been its leader since 2012. China’s growing power has brought cooperation and competition with other countries."]],
k:[["Founded","1 October 1949"],["Founding leader","Mao Zedong"],["Reform era began","1978, under Deng Xiaoping"],["Population","About 1.4 billion"],["World Trade Organization","Joined 2001"]],
d:[["1949","The People’s Republic of China is founded"],["1958","The Great Leap Forward begins"],["1966","The Cultural Revolution begins"],["1978","Deng Xiaoping begins economic reforms"],["1997","Hong Kong returns to Chinese rule"],["2008","Beijing hosts the Summer Olympics"]],
s2:[["Tiananmen Square, 1989","In the spring of 1989, students and workers gathered in Beijing’s Tiananmen Square and other cities to call for political reform and an end to corruption. On the night of 3 to 4 June, the government sent troops and tanks to clear the protests, killing hundreds, possibly thousands, of people. The events remain heavily censored in China."],["Lifting people out of poverty","According to the World Bank, since 1978 China has lifted around 800 million people out of extreme poverty, the largest reduction in history. Hundreds of millions of people moved from the countryside to cities to work in factories and construction. Rapid growth also brought pollution, inequality and environmental challenges."],["One-child policy","From 1980, China limited most families to one child to slow population growth. The policy was enforced, sometimes harshly, and contributed to an aging population and a gender imbalance. It was relaxed to two children in 2016 and three in 2021. In 2023 India overtook China as the world’s most populous country."],["Hong Kong and Macau","Britain returned Hong Kong to China in 1997, and Portugal returned Macau in 1999, under the principle of one country, two systems. Large protests in Hong Kong in 2019 opposed a proposed law and called for democratic reforms, and a national security law was imposed in 2020. Supporters and critics strongly disagree about these developments."],["China and its neighbors","China has territorial disputes with several neighbors, including in the South China Sea, where its claims overlap with those of the Philippines, Vietnam, Malaysia, Brunei and others. In 2016 an international tribunal ruled in a case brought by the Philippines that China’s broad historical claims had no legal basis; China rejected the ruling. China is also the largest trading partner of many Asian countries, including the Philippines."],["Global role","China has become a major lender and builder of infrastructure abroad through the Belt and Road Initiative, launched in 2013. It is a permanent member of the UN Security Council. Its relationship with the United States, marked by both trade ties and rivalry, is one of the most important in the world."]],
sp:["Shenzhen","In 1980 Shenzhen, a small border town near Hong Kong, became one of China’s first Special Economic Zones, where foreign companies could invest and market rules applied. It grew at astonishing speed into a city of more than 17 million people, famous for electronics, technology companies and skyscrapers. Its transformation is often seen as a symbol of China’s economic rise."],
g:[["People’s Republic","The official name of China since 1949"],["Collectivization","Merging private farms into large collective farms"],["Special Economic Zone","An area with special rules to attract investment"],["Communist Party","The ruling party of China"]],
f:["The People’s Republic of China was founded in 1949.","Deng Xiaoping began reforms in 1978.","India overtook China in population in 2023."],
fun:"Shenzhen grew from a small town into a city of over 17 million people in about 40 years.",
tl:"Itinatag ang People's Republic of China noong 1 Oktubre 1949 ni Mao Zedong. Dumanas ito ng taggutom at kaguluhan, ngunit mula 1978, dahil sa mga reporma ni Deng Xiaoping, naging pangalawang pinakamalaking ekonomiya ito sa mundo."});

ADDART({id:"middleeast",t:"The Modern Middle East",c:"History",i:"🕊️",w:"Modern history of the Middle East",r:["ottoman","islam","judaism","un","ofw"],
p:[["Crossroads of three continents","The Middle East links Asia, Africa and Europe and is the birthplace of Judaism, Christianity and Islam."],["New states","After the Ottoman Empire collapsed in 1918, new countries were formed, many under European control at first."],["Oil and conflict","The region has vast oil reserves and has experienced repeated wars and peace efforts."]],
l:"The modern Middle East includes countries such as Egypt, Israel, the Palestinian territories, Jordan, Lebanon, Syria, Iraq, Iran, Saudi Arabia and the Gulf states. After World War I, the Ottoman Empire’s lands were divided, mostly under British and French control, and new states gradually became independent. The discovery of oil made the region strategically important. Its modern history includes the creation of Israel and the long Israeli–Palestinian conflict, revolutions, wars, and periods of reform and peacemaking. Millions of Filipinos have worked in the Middle East.",
s:[["After the Ottomans","During World War I, Britain and France secretly agreed in 1916 to divide Ottoman lands, the Sykes–Picot Agreement. After the war, Britain received mandates over Palestine, Transjordan and Iraq, and France over Syria and Lebanon. Turkey became a republic in 1923, and Saudi Arabia was unified in 1932. Most mandates became independent states by the late 1940s."],["Oil","Oil was discovered in Persia in 1908, in Iraq in 1927 and in Saudi Arabia in 1938. The Gulf region holds a large share of the world’s oil reserves. Oil wealth transformed countries such as Saudi Arabia, Kuwait, Qatar and the United Arab Emirates, building modern cities and attracting workers from Asia, including the Philippines."],["Israel and Palestine","Jewish immigration to Palestine grew in the early 1900s, encouraged by the Zionist movement and later by persecution in Europe. In 1947 the UN proposed dividing Palestine into Jewish and Arab states. Israel declared independence on 14 May 1948, and war followed with neighboring Arab states. About 700,000 Palestinians fled or were expelled, an event Palestinians call the Nakba, meaning catastrophe. The conflict over land, security and Palestinian statehood continues."],["Wars and peace","Israel fought wars with Arab neighbors in 1948, 1956, 1967 and 1973. In the 1967 Six-Day War, Israel took control of the West Bank, Gaza, East Jerusalem, the Sinai and the Golan Heights. Egypt and Israel signed a peace treaty in 1979, and Jordan in 1994. The Oslo Accords of 1993 created the Palestinian Authority, but a final agreement has not been reached."]],
k:[["Region","Western Asia and northeastern Africa"],["Ottoman Empire ends","1922"],["Israel founded","14 May 1948"],["Iranian Revolution","1979"],["Arab Spring","2010–2012"]],
d:[["1916","The Sykes–Picot Agreement is made"],["1948","Israel declares independence; the first Arab–Israeli war"],["1967","The Six-Day War"],["1979","Egypt–Israel peace treaty; the Iranian Revolution"],["1993","The Oslo Accords are signed"],["2011","Protests of the Arab Spring spread; war begins in Syria"]],
s2:[["The Iranian Revolution","In 1979 mass protests overthrew Iran’s monarch, the Shah, and Ayatollah Ruhollah Khomeini established an Islamic Republic. The revolution changed Iran’s society and foreign relations. From 1980 to 1988, Iran fought a devastating war with Iraq, led by Saddam Hussein, in which hundreds of thousands died."],["The Gulf Wars","In 1990 Iraq invaded Kuwait, and in 1991 a US-led coalition drove Iraqi forces out. In 2003 the United States and allies invaded Iraq, overthrowing Saddam Hussein, citing weapons of mass destruction that were never found. The war and its aftermath brought years of violence, including the rise of the extremist group ISIS."],["The Arab Spring","Beginning in Tunisia in December 2010, protests spread across the Arab world calling for freedom, jobs and an end to corruption. Leaders were overthrown in Tunisia, Egypt, Libya and Yemen. In Syria, protests turned into a civil war that killed hundreds of thousands and displaced millions; the government of Bashar al-Assad fell in December 2024. Results across the region varied widely."],["Recent conflict in Gaza and Israel","On 7 October 2023, Hamas, the group governing Gaza, led an attack on southern Israel, killing about 1,200 people and taking around 250 hostages. Israel responded with a military campaign in Gaza that killed tens of thousands of Palestinians, according to Gaza health authorities, and caused a severe humanitarian crisis. A ceasefire took effect in 2025, though the situation has remained fragile. The war deepened suffering and division across the region."],["Filipinos in the Middle East","Millions of Filipinos have worked in Middle Eastern countries since the 1970s, as engineers, nurses, domestic workers, construction workers and in many other jobs, especially in Saudi Arabia, the United Arab Emirates, Kuwait and Qatar. Their earnings support families at home. During wars and crises, the Philippine government has organized evacuations of its citizens."],["Hopes for peace","Despite conflict, there have been many efforts at peace and cooperation, from the Camp David Accords of 1978 to the Abraham Accords of 2020, in which Israel established relations with the United Arab Emirates, Bahrain and others. International organizations and many people in the region continue to work toward lasting peace and a just solution for Israelis and Palestinians."]],
sp:["Three faiths, one city","Jerusalem is holy to Jews, Christians and Muslims. Its Old City contains the Western Wall, the holiest place where Jews may pray; the Church of the Holy Sepulchre, where Christians believe Jesus was crucified and buried; and the Al-Aqsa Mosque and Dome of the Rock, among Islam’s holiest sites. Both Israelis and Palestinians claim Jerusalem as their capital, and its status is one of the most sensitive issues in the conflict."],
g:[["Mandate","A territory governed by another country on behalf of the League of Nations"],["Zionism","A movement for a Jewish homeland in the Land of Israel"],["Nakba","The displacement of Palestinians in 1948"],["Ceasefire","An agreement to stop fighting"]],
f:["The Middle East is the birthplace of Judaism, Christianity and Islam.","Israel was founded in 1948.","Millions of Filipinos have worked in the Middle East."],
fun:"Jerusalem’s Old City is less than one square kilometer in area but holy to three faiths.",
tl:"Ang Gitnang Silangan ay rehiyon na nag-uugnay sa Asya, Aprika at Europa at pinagmulan ng Hudaismo, Kristiyanismo at Islam. Mayaman ito sa langis at nakaranas ng maraming digmaan at pagsisikap para sa kapayapaan. Milyon-milyong Pilipino ang nagtrabaho rito."});

ADDART({id:"terrorism",t:"Terrorism and the World after 2001",c:"History",i:"🏙️",w:"Terrorism",r:["middleeast","un","hrights","coldwar","cybersecurity"],
p:[["Violence for political goals","Terrorism is the use of violence against civilians to spread fear and pursue political, religious or ideological aims."],["11 September 2001","The attacks on the United States in 2001 changed world politics and security."],["Many forms","Terrorism has come from many different groups and ideologies around the world."]],
l:"Terrorism generally means the deliberate use or threat of violence, often against civilians, to create fear and achieve political, religious or ideological goals. There is no single agreed international definition. Terrorism has a long history, but the attacks of 11 September 2001 on the United States, carried out by the group al-Qaeda, led to wars in Afghanistan and Iraq, new security measures around the world and debates about how to balance safety and freedom. Terrorist groups of many kinds, including religious extremists, ethno-nationalists and far-right and far-left movements, have caused suffering in many countries.",
s:[["The 11 September attacks","On 11 September 2001, 19 al-Qaeda hijackers seized four passenger planes in the United States. Two were flown into the World Trade Center towers in New York, which collapsed, one into the Pentagon near Washington and one crashed in Pennsylvania after passengers fought back. Nearly 3,000 people were killed, from more than 90 countries."],["War in Afghanistan","Because Afghanistan’s Taliban government sheltered al-Qaeda’s leader Osama bin Laden, the United States and allies invaded Afghanistan in October 2001 and overthrew the Taliban. Bin Laden was killed by US forces in Pakistan in 2011. After 20 years, foreign forces withdrew in August 2021, and the Taliban returned to power."],["ISIS","The extremist group known as the Islamic State, or ISIS, seized large areas of Iraq and Syria in 2014 and declared a so-called caliphate. It carried out brutal killings and inspired attacks abroad. Iraqi, Kurdish, Syrian and international forces drove it from its territory by 2019, though it continues to operate in some regions."],["Many sources of terrorism","Terrorism is not linked to any single religion or region. Groups have been driven by nationalism, separatism, religious extremism, racism and political ideologies. Attacks have included the 2008 Mumbai attacks, the 2011 attacks in Norway by a far-right extremist, the 2015 Paris attacks and the 2019 Christchurch mosque attacks in New Zealand. Most victims of terrorism live in countries in Asia, Africa and the Middle East."]],
k:[["Definition","Violence against civilians for political aims (no single agreed definition)"],["11 September 2001 deaths","Nearly 3,000"],["US-led war in Afghanistan","2001–2021"],["ISIS territorial defeat","2019"],["Marawi siege","May–October 2017"]],
d:[["2001","The 11 September attacks; the war in Afghanistan begins"],["2003","The US-led invasion of Iraq"],["2004","Bombings in Madrid kill 193; SuperFerry 14 is bombed in Manila Bay"],["2011","Osama bin Laden is killed"],["2017","The Marawi siege in Mindanao"],["2021","Foreign forces withdraw from Afghanistan"]],
s2:[["Terrorism in the Philippines","The Philippines has suffered terrorist violence, especially in Mindanao and Sulu. The Abu Sayyaf group carried out kidnappings and bombings, including the bombing of SuperFerry 14 in Manila Bay in 2004, which killed 116 people, one of the deadliest terrorist attacks at sea. In 2017, militants linked to ISIS seized parts of Marawi City; a five-month battle left the city center in ruins and displaced hundreds of thousands of people."],["Peace in Mindanao","Alongside security efforts, peace agreements have sought to address the roots of conflict in Mindanao. After decades of negotiations, the government and the Moro Islamic Liberation Front signed a peace agreement, and in 2019 the Bangsamoro Autonomous Region in Muslim Mindanao was created, giving the region greater self-government."],["Security and freedom","After 2001, many countries introduced new security measures: airport screening, surveillance, terrorist watchlists and new laws allowing detention of suspects. Critics argued that some measures, such as detention without trial at Guantánamo Bay and harsh interrogation methods, violated human rights. Societies continue to debate how to balance security with civil liberties."],["Why people join","Researchers study why people join extremist groups. Factors can include a sense of injustice or humiliation, personal crises, the search for identity and belonging, propaganda, especially online, and recruitment by friends or family. Most people who share grievances never turn to violence."],["Preventing violent extremism","Efforts to prevent terrorism include policing and intelligence, but also education, community programs, removing extremist propaganda online, supporting victims, helping people leave extremist groups and addressing grievances through peaceful politics and development. International cooperation, including through the United Nations, is important."],["Remembering victims","Memorials honor victims of terrorism, such as the 9/11 Memorial in New York, built where the twin towers stood, with waterfalls in their footprints. The United Nations marks 21 August as the International Day of Remembrance of and Tribute to the Victims of Terrorism."]],
sp:["Flight 93","On 11 September 2001, United Airlines Flight 93 was hijacked after leaving Newark, New Jersey. Through phone calls, passengers and crew learned of the attacks in New York and Washington. They decided to fight back and stormed the cockpit. The plane crashed into a field near Shanksville, Pennsylvania, killing all aboard, but its hijackers failed to reach their target, believed to be the US Capitol or the White House. A national memorial now stands at the site."],
g:[["Terrorism","Violence against civilians to spread fear for political aims"],["Extremism","Holding extreme views, sometimes supporting violence"],["Counterterrorism","Actions taken to prevent terrorism"],["Radicalization","The process by which people adopt extremist views"]],
f:["The 11 September 2001 attacks killed nearly 3,000 people.","The Marawi siege lasted five months in 2017.","There is no single agreed international definition of terrorism."],
fun:"The 9/11 Memorial in New York has two waterfalls in the footprints of the twin towers.",
tl:"Ang terorismo ay paggamit ng karahasan laban sa mga sibilyan para maghasik ng takot at makamit ang layuning pampolitika o panrelihiyon. Binago ng pag-atake noong 11 Setyembre 2001 ang pandaigdigang politika. Nakaranas din ang Pilipinas ng terorismo, gaya ng pagkubkob sa Marawi noong 2017."});

