ADDART({id:"thailandcountry",t:"Thailand",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🐘",w:"Thailand",r:["asean","buddh","rice","khmer","martial","sea"],
p:[["Land of smiles","Thailand is a kingdom in the heart of mainland Southeast Asia, famous for its temples, food and hospitality."],["Free from European rule","It is the only Southeast Asian country never colonized by a European power."],["Birthplace of ASEAN","ASEAN was founded in Bangkok in 1967, with Thailand and the Philippines among its five founders."]],
l:"Thailand, known as Siam until 1939, is a country in mainland Southeast Asia with about 72 million people. Its capital, Bangkok, is one of Asia’s great cities. Thailand is a constitutional monarchy where most people are Theravada Buddhists, and its golden temples, royal palaces, beaches and cuisine attract tens of millions of visitors each year. It is a major exporter of rice, cars and electronics. Thailand was a founding member of the Association of Southeast Asian Nations, established in Bangkok in 1967.",
s:[["Land","Thailand stretches from mountains in the north, where Doi Inthanon rises to 2,565 meters, through the fertile central plains of the Chao Phraya River to the long, narrow southern peninsula between the Andaman Sea and the Gulf of Thailand. The northeast, called Isan, is a dry plateau bordering the Mekong River."],["History","The kingdom of Sukhothai, founded in the 1200s, is often seen as the first Thai state. Ayutthaya, founded in 1351, became a rich trading capital until Burmese armies destroyed it in 1767. In 1782 the Chakri dynasty founded Bangkok, which remains the capital. Through skillful diplomacy and reforms, Siam kept its independence while its neighbors were colonized."],["Government and society","Thailand became a constitutional monarchy after a revolution in 1932. Its modern politics has included elected governments, mass protests and several military coups. The monarchy is deeply respected and protected by strict laws. About 93 percent of Thais are Buddhist, and young men often spend time as monks."],["Economy","Thailand is one of the world’s largest rice exporters and a major producer of cars, electronics, rubber and seafood. Tourism is a major industry, with tens of millions of visitors to Bangkok, Chiang Mai, Phuket and other places. Thai food, with its balance of sweet, sour, salty and spicy flavors, is loved worldwide."]],
k:[["Capital","Bangkok"],["Population","About 72 million (2024)"],["Area","About 513,000 km²"],["Highest point","Doi Inthanon, 2,565 m"],["Currency","Baht"]],
d:[["1238","The Sukhothai kingdom is founded"],["1351","Ayutthaya is founded"],["1767","Burmese armies destroy Ayutthaya"],["1782","Bangkok becomes the capital under the Chakri dynasty"],["1932","A revolution makes Thailand a constitutional monarchy"],["1939","Siam is renamed Thailand"],["1967","ASEAN is founded in Bangkok"]],
s2:[["Bangkok","Bangkok, called Krung Thep by Thais, has a ceremonial name that is one of the longest place names in the world. The city is home to the Grand Palace, the Temple of the Emerald Buddha and Wat Arun on the Chao Phraya River. Its floating markets, street food and nightlife are famous, and its greater urban area has well over 10 million people."],["Never colonized","In the 1800s, Britain controlled Burma and Malaya, and France controlled Indochina. Kings Mongkut, known as Rama IV, and Chulalongkorn, Rama V, modernized Siam, building railways, schools and a new government, and gave up some border territories, allowing Siam to remain a buffer between British and French empires."],["Muay Thai","Muay Thai, Thai boxing, is called the art of eight limbs because fighters strike with fists, elbows, knees and shins. Before a fight, boxers perform a ritual dance, the wai khru, to honor their teachers. It is Thailand’s national sport and is now practiced worldwide."],["Festivals","Songkran, the Thai New Year in April, is celebrated with water splashing, recognized by UNESCO in 2023. During Loy Krathong, people float small decorated baskets with candles on rivers. In 2024 UNESCO also recognized tom yum kung, the famous spicy and sour shrimp soup."],["Elephants","The elephant is a national symbol of Thailand. For centuries elephants were used in warfare, logging and royal ceremonies. Today wild Asian elephants live in protected forests, and sanctuaries care for elephants once used for logging or tourist rides."],["The 2004 tsunami","On 26 December 2004, a tsunami caused by a huge earthquake off Sumatra struck Thailand’s Andaman coast, killing about 5,400 people, including many tourists. Thailand has since built tsunami warning towers and evacuation routes along its coasts."]],
sp:["Ayutthaya","For over four centuries, Ayutthaya was one of the largest and richest cities in Asia, built on an island in the Chao Phraya River and crisscrossed by canals. Merchants came from China, Japan, Persia, Portugal, the Netherlands and France. In 1767 Burmese armies captured and burned the city. Its ruins of temples, headless Buddha statues and a Buddha head entwined in tree roots are now a UNESCO World Heritage Site."],
g:[["Siam","The former name of Thailand"],["Theravada","The main form of Buddhism in Thailand and mainland Southeast Asia"],["Wat","A Buddhist temple in Thailand"],["Muay Thai","Thai boxing"],["Chakri dynasty","Thailand’s royal family since 1782"]],
f:["Thailand was never colonized by Europeans.","ASEAN was founded in Bangkok in 1967.","Muay Thai is called the art of eight limbs."],
fun:"Bangkok’s full ceremonial name is one of the longest place names in the world.",
tl:"Ang Thailand ay kaharian sa Timog-silangang Asya na kilala sa mga templo, pagkain at magiliw na mamamayan. Ito lamang ang bansa sa rehiyon na hindi nasakop ng Europeo. Itinatag ang ASEAN sa Bangkok noong 1967."});

ADDART({id:"malaysiacountry",t:"Malaysia",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🌺",w:"Malaysia",r:["borneo","asean","islam","spice","sea","indonesiacountry"],
p:[["Two lands, one nation","Malaysia is made up of the Malay Peninsula and the states of Sabah and Sarawak on the island of Borneo."],["Many cultures","Malays, Chinese, Indians and Indigenous peoples live together, sharing festivals and food."],["Twin towers","Kuala Lumpur’s Petronas Towers were the tallest buildings in the world from 1998 to 2004."]],
l:"Malaysia is a federation in Southeast Asia made up of 13 states and three federal territories, split between the Malay Peninsula and northern Borneo. About 34 million people live there. Its capital is Kuala Lumpur, while many government offices are in Putrajaya. Malaysia is a multicultural country where Malays, Chinese, Indians and many Indigenous groups live together, and Islam is the official religion, with freedom of religion for others. It has one of Southeast Asia’s strongest economies, based on manufacturing, palm oil, rubber, oil, gas and tourism.",
s:[["Land","Peninsular Malaysia has mountains covered in rainforest, plantations and coastal plains. Sabah and Sarawak, on Borneo, have vast rainforests, rivers and Mount Kinabalu, at 4,095 meters one of the highest peaks in Southeast Asia. Malaysia’s rainforests, among the oldest on Earth, are home to orangutans, hornbills and the giant Rafflesia flower."],["History","Around 1400 a Malay prince founded the Sultanate of Malacca, which grew into a great trading port on the Strait of Malacca. The Portuguese captured Malacca in 1511, followed by the Dutch in 1641 and later the British, who controlled the region by the 1800s. Malaya became independent on 31 August 1957, and Malaysia was formed on 16 September 1963 with Sabah, Sarawak and, until 1965, Singapore."],["Government","Malaysia is a constitutional monarchy with a parliamentary government. Uniquely, its king, the Yang di-Pertuan Agong, is chosen every five years from among the hereditary rulers of nine Malay states. The prime minister leads the government."],["People and culture","About 70 percent of Malaysians are Bumiputera, Malays and Indigenous peoples, along with large Chinese and Indian communities. Malay is the national language, and English, Chinese dialects and Tamil are widely spoken. Hari Raya, Chinese New Year, Deepavali and Christmas are all public holidays, and people often visit each other during open houses."]],
k:[["Capital","Kuala Lumpur (administrative center: Putrajaya)"],["Population","About 34 million (2024)"],["Area","About 330,800 km²"],["Highest point","Mount Kinabalu, 4,095 m"],["Currency","Ringgit"]],
d:[["c. 1400","The Sultanate of Malacca is founded"],["1511","The Portuguese capture Malacca"],["1641","The Dutch take Malacca"],["1826","Britain forms the Straits Settlements"],["1957","Malaya becomes independent"],["1963","Malaysia is formed"],["1998","The Petronas Towers are completed"]],
s2:[["The Petronas Towers","The Petronas Twin Towers in Kuala Lumpur, 452 meters tall, were the tallest buildings in the world from 1998 to 2004. Designed by the architect César Pelli with patterns inspired by Islamic art, they are linked by a skybridge on the 41st and 42nd floors. In 2023 another Kuala Lumpur skyscraper, Merdeka 118, became the world’s second-tallest building."],["Malaysian food","Malaysian cuisine blends Malay, Chinese, Indian and Indigenous traditions. Popular dishes include nasi lemak, rice cooked in coconut milk often called the national dish, satay, laksa, roti canai and char kway teow. Hawker stalls and night markets are central to daily life."],["Rainforests and wildlife","Taman Negara on the peninsula is a rainforest thought to be about 130 million years old. Borneo’s forests shelter orangutans, proboscis monkeys, pygmy elephants and clouded leopards. Large areas of forest have been cleared for oil palm plantations, raising concerns about wildlife, and Malaysia has pledged to protect a large share of its forests."],["Palm oil and industry","Malaysia is one of the world’s top producers of palm oil, used in foods, cosmetics and fuels, and a major producer of rubber, electronics and semiconductors. Penang is a hub for chip manufacturing. Oil and gas from the seas off Sarawak, Sabah and the peninsula are important exports."],["Malaysia and the Philippines","Malaysia and the Philippines are neighbors and fellow founders of ASEAN, linked by trade, migration and shared Malay cultural roots. Many Filipinos work in Sabah. The Philippines maintains a historic claim to parts of Sabah, based on the former Sultanate of Sulu’s rights, which Malaysia rejects; the two countries have kept close relations despite this dispute."],["Formula One and sport","Badminton is Malaysia’s most successful Olympic sport, and stars such as Lee Chong Wei are national heroes. Sepak takraw, a game like volleyball played with the feet using a rattan ball, is popular across Southeast Asia, including in Malaysia and the Philippines."]],
sp:["The Strait of Malacca","The Strait of Malacca, between the Malay Peninsula and Sumatra, is one of the most important shipping lanes in the world, carrying a large share of global trade, including oil for China, Japan and South Korea. For centuries, whoever controlled the strait could grow rich from trade, which is why Malacca, Srivijaya and later European powers fought for it. Today Malaysia, Indonesia and Singapore cooperate to keep it safe from piracy."],
g:[["Federation","A union of states with a central government"],["Bumiputera","Malays and other Indigenous peoples of Malaysia"],["Yang di-Pertuan Agong","The king of Malaysia"],["Hawker","A seller of street food"],["Strait","A narrow sea passage between two lands"]],
f:["Malaysia’s king is chosen every five years.","The Petronas Towers are 452 meters tall.","Taman Negara is one of the world’s oldest rainforests."],
fun:"Malaysia’s king is elected from among nine hereditary state rulers.",
tl:"Ang Malaysia ay pederasyon sa Timog-silangang Asya na binubuo ng Malay Peninsula at ng Sabah at Sarawak sa Borneo. Maraming kultura ang magkakasama rito. Kilala ang Petronas Towers sa Kuala Lumpur."});

ADDART({id:"singaporecountry",t:"Singapore",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🦁",w:"Singapore",r:["asean","malaysiacountry","trade","urban","ships","ofw"],
p:[["A city-state","Singapore is an island country that is also a single city, at the southern tip of the Malay Peninsula."],["From port to powerhouse","It grew from a small trading post into one of the world’s richest and busiest ports."],["Garden city","Despite its density, Singapore is famous for its greenery, gardens and clean streets."]],
l:"Singapore is a city-state on a group of islands at the southern tip of the Malay Peninsula, with about 6 million people. It was founded as a British trading post in 1819, became part of Malaysia in 1963 and an independent country in 1965. With few natural resources, Singapore built a highly successful economy based on trade, shipping, finance, manufacturing and technology, becoming one of the wealthiest countries in the world. Its people are mainly of Chinese, Malay and Indian descent, and it has four official languages.",
s:[["Land","Singapore consists of one main island and about 60 smaller islets, with a total area of about 735 square kilometers, about a quarter of which has been added by land reclamation. The highest point, Bukit Timah Hill, is only about 164 meters high. The climate is hot and humid all year, with frequent rain."],["History","Singapore was a trading settlement called Temasek in the 1300s. In 1819 Stamford Raffles of the British East India Company set up a trading post there, and it became a busy free port. Japan occupied Singapore from 1942 to 1945 after the fall of the British fortress, a major defeat for Britain. Singapore joined Malaysia in 1963 but separated and became independent on 9 August 1965."],["Economy","Singapore’s port is one of the world’s busiest, and Changi Airport is regularly ranked among the best. It is a global center of finance, oil refining, electronics, biomedical research and technology. Its government built strong public schools, efficient services and clean, safe cities. It has one of the highest incomes per person in the world."],["People and languages","About three-quarters of Singaporeans are of Chinese descent, with large Malay and Indian communities and many foreign residents. English, Malay, Mandarin and Tamil are official languages, and Malay is the national language. Many people speak Singlish, a lively local form of English mixing in Malay, Chinese and Tamil words."]],
k:[["Capital","Singapore (city-state)"],["Population","About 6 million (2024)"],["Area","About 735 km²"],["Highest point","Bukit Timah Hill, about 164 m"],["Currency","Singapore dollar"]],
d:[["1300s","The trading settlement of Temasek flourishes"],["1819","Stamford Raffles founds a British trading post"],["1942","Japan captures Singapore"],["1959","Singapore gains self-government"],["1963","Singapore joins Malaysia"],["1965","Singapore becomes independent"],["2020","Singapore’s hawker culture is recognized by UNESCO"]],
s2:[["Lee Kuan Yew","Lee Kuan Yew was Singapore’s first prime minister, from 1959 to 1990. He led the country through independence and rapid development, promoting education, discipline, anti-corruption and economic openness. His government was also criticized for limiting political opposition and press freedom. He died in 2015."],["Public housing","About 80 percent of Singaporeans live in public housing flats built by the Housing and Development Board, most of which residents own. Neighborhoods are planned with schools, markets, parks and transport links. The system helped turn Singapore from a city of crowded slums into a modern city."],["Hawker culture","Singapore’s hawker centers are open-air food courts where vendors sell affordable dishes such as Hainanese chicken rice, laksa, satay and chili crab. In 2020 UNESCO recognized Singapore’s hawker culture as intangible cultural heritage. Some hawker stalls have even received Michelin stars."],["City in a garden","Singapore has planted millions of trees and built parks connected by green corridors. Gardens by the Bay features giant Supertrees, vertical gardens up to 50 meters tall, and huge glass conservatories. The Singapore Botanic Gardens, founded in 1859, is a UNESCO World Heritage Site."],["Filipinos in Singapore","Singapore is home to around 200,000 Filipinos, working as nurses, engineers, domestic workers, professionals and in many other jobs. Lucky Plaza on Orchard Road is a popular gathering place on Sundays. The two countries are close partners in ASEAN."],["The Merlion","The Merlion, a mythical creature with a lion’s head and a fish’s body, is Singapore’s symbol. Its lion head recalls the city’s name, Singapura, meaning lion city in Sanskrit, and its fish body represents its origins as a fishing village. A large Merlion statue spouts water into Marina Bay."]],
sp:["How Singapore got its name","According to the Malay Annals, a prince from Sumatra named Sang Nila Utama landed on the island in the 1200s or 1300s and saw a strange animal he believed was a lion. He named the place Singapura, from Sanskrit words meaning lion city. Lions have never lived in Singapore, so the animal may have been a tiger. The name stuck, and the lion became the country’s symbol."],
g:[["City-state","A city that is also an independent country"],["Land reclamation","Creating new land from the sea"],["Hawker center","An open-air complex of food stalls"],["Singlish","A colloquial English spoken in Singapore"],["Free port","A port where goods can be traded without customs duties"]],
f:["Singapore became independent in 1965.","About a quarter of Singapore’s land was reclaimed from the sea.","Singapore has four official languages."],
fun:"Singapore means lion city, though lions never lived there.",
tl:"Ang Singapore ay lungsod-estado sa dulo ng Malay Peninsula. Mula sa maliit na daungan, naging isa ito sa pinakamayamang bansa sa mundo. Mga 200,000 Pilipino ang naninirahan at nagtatrabaho rito."});

ADDART({id:"myanmarcountry",t:"Myanmar",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🛕",w:"Myanmar",r:["asean","buddh","bangladeshcountry","thailandcountry","hrights","sea"],
p:[["Land of pagodas","Myanmar, also called Burma, is famous for thousands of Buddhist temples and golden pagodas."],["A land of many nations","It is home to more than 100 ethnic groups, including the Bamar, Shan, Karen, Rakhine and Kachin."],["A troubled path","Myanmar has struggled with military rule and long civil conflicts since independence in 1948."]],
l:"Myanmar, also known as Burma, is the largest country in mainland Southeast Asia, with about 54 million people. Its capital is Naypyidaw, and its largest city is Yangon. Myanmar is a mainly Buddhist country with a rich heritage of temples, including the thousands of ancient monuments of Bagan and the golden Shwedagon Pagoda. Since independence from Britain in 1948, it has experienced long periods of military rule and armed conflicts with ethnic groups. A military takeover in 2021 led to widespread resistance and a humanitarian crisis.",
s:[["Land","Myanmar stretches from the snowy Himalayan foothills in the north, where Hkakabo Razi rises to about 5,880 meters, to tropical islands in the south. The Irrawaddy River flows through its heartland to a fertile delta, where much rice is grown. Mountains along the borders are home to many ethnic minority peoples."],["History","The Bagan Kingdom, founded in the 1000s, united much of the region and built thousands of temples. Later kingdoms rose and fell, and in the 1700s and 1800s Burmese kings fought Thailand and others. Britain conquered Burma in three wars between 1824 and 1885 and ruled it as part of British India until 1937. It became independent on 4 January 1948."],["Military rule","Independence leader Aung San was assassinated in 1947, before independence. In 1962 the military seized power and ruled for decades, isolating the country. Aung San Suu Kyi, his daughter, led a democracy movement and spent many years under house arrest, winning the Nobel Peace Prize in 1991. Reforms from 2011 allowed elections, which her party won in 2015 and 2020."],["The 2021 coup","On 1 February 2021, the military seized power again, detaining elected leaders including Aung San Suu Kyi. Mass protests were met with force, and armed resistance groups and ethnic armies have fought the military in many parts of the country. Millions of people have been displaced, and the conflict has caused a severe humanitarian crisis."]],
k:[["Capital","Naypyidaw (largest city: Yangon)"],["Population","About 54 million (2024)"],["Area","About 677,000 km²"],["Highest point","Hkakabo Razi, about 5,880 m"],["Currency","Kyat"]],
d:[["1044","King Anawrahta founds the Bagan Kingdom"],["1885","Britain completes its conquest of Burma"],["1948","Burma becomes independent"],["1962","The military seizes power"],["1991","Aung San Suu Kyi wins the Nobel Peace Prize"],["2017","Hundreds of thousands of Rohingya flee to Bangladesh"],["2021","The military takes power in a coup"]],
s2:[["Bagan","On a plain beside the Irrawaddy River, more than 2,000 Buddhist temples and pagodas survive from the Bagan Kingdom, built mostly between the 11th and 13th centuries. Watching hot-air balloons drift over the temples at sunrise is one of Asia’s most famous sights. Bagan became a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 2019."],["Shwedagon Pagoda","The Shwedagon Pagoda in Yangon, about 99 meters tall, is covered in gold and topped with thousands of diamonds and other gems. Buddhists believe it contains relics of the Buddha, including strands of his hair. It is the most sacred Buddhist site in Myanmar and a center of community life."],["The Rohingya crisis","The Rohingya, a mostly Muslim minority in Rakhine State, have long been denied citizenship in Myanmar. In 2017 a military crackdown, following attacks by an armed group, drove more than 700,000 Rohingya to flee to Bangladesh, where about a million now live in refugee camps. United Nations investigators accused the military of grave crimes, and the case was taken to the International Court of Justice."],["The 2025 earthquake","On 28 March 2025, a magnitude 7.7 earthquake struck central Myanmar near Mandalay, killing more than 3,700 people and damaging buildings, bridges and pagodas. The shaking was felt as far away as Bangkok, where a building under construction collapsed. The disaster added to the suffering caused by the ongoing conflict."],["Thanaka and longyi","Many people in Myanmar wear thanaka, a pale yellow paste made from ground tree bark, on their faces as sunscreen and decoration. Both men and women often wear a longyi, a long cloth wrapped around the waist like a sarong. Tea shops are popular gathering places."],["Many peoples","Myanmar officially recognizes 135 ethnic groups. The Bamar are the largest, and others include the Shan, Karen, Rakhine, Kachin, Chin and Mon, each with its own language and traditions. Many ethnic groups have long sought greater autonomy, and some have fought the central government for decades."]],
sp:["Aung San Suu Kyi","Aung San Suu Kyi, the daughter of independence hero Aung San, returned to Burma in 1988 to care for her mother and became a leader of the democracy movement. She spent about 15 of the next 21 years under house arrest, becoming a world symbol of peaceful resistance and winning the 1991 Nobel Peace Prize. After her party won elections, she led the government from 2016, but her international reputation suffered when she defended Myanmar against genocide accusations over the Rohingya. After the 2021 coup, she was detained again and sentenced to many years in prison."],
g:[["Pagoda","A tiered Buddhist tower, often holding relics"],["Coup","A sudden, illegal seizure of power"],["Junta","A military government"],["Longyi","A wrap-around cloth worn in Myanmar"],["Thanaka","A cosmetic paste made from tree bark"]],
f:["Bagan has more than 2,000 ancient temples.","Myanmar became independent in 1948.","Aung San Suu Kyi won the Nobel Peace Prize in 1991."],
fun:"Many people in Myanmar wear thanaka paste, made from tree bark, as natural sunscreen.",
tl:"Ang Myanmar, o Burma, ay bansang Budista sa Timog-silangang Asya na kilala sa libu-libong templo at pagoda. Mahigit 100 pangkat-etniko ang naninirahan dito. Dumanas ito ng mahabang pamumuno ng militar at mga digmaang sibil."});

ADDART({id:"cambodiacountry",t:"Cambodia",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🛕",w:"Cambodia",r:["khmer","angkor","asean","mekong","buddh","vietnam"],
p:[["Kingdom of Angkor","Cambodia is the heir of the Khmer Empire, whose temple of Angkor Wat appears on the national flag."],["A tragic past","Under the Khmer Rouge from 1975 to 1979, perhaps a quarter of the population died."],["A new start","Since the 1990s, Cambodia has rebuilt its economy, and tourism and garment industries have grown."]],
l:"Cambodia is a country in mainland Southeast Asia with about 17 million people. Its capital is Phnom Penh, on the Mekong River. Cambodia is the heir of the great Khmer Empire, which built Angkor Wat. In the 20th century, it suffered war and genocide: under the Khmer Rouge regime from 1975 to 1979, about 1.5 to 2 million people died. Since peace agreements in 1991, Cambodia has rebuilt, becoming a constitutional monarchy with growing tourism, farming and garment industries. Most Cambodians are Khmer and Theravada Buddhist.",
s:[["Land and water","Much of Cambodia is a low plain around the Tonlé Sap lake and the Mekong River, surrounded by mountains. During the rainy season, the Mekong floods so much that the Tonlé Sap River reverses direction, and the lake swells to several times its dry-season size, creating one of the world’s richest freshwater fisheries."],["From empire to colony","After Angkor declined in the 1400s, Cambodia was squeezed between powerful Thai and Vietnamese neighbors. In 1863 it became a French protectorate. Cambodia gained independence on 9 November 1953 under King Norodom Sihanouk, who dominated its politics for decades."],["War and the Khmer Rouge","Cambodia was drawn into the Vietnam War, suffering heavy American bombing. In 1975 the Khmer Rouge, a radical communist movement led by Pol Pot, took power. They emptied the cities, abolished money, schools and religion, and forced people into labor camps. Through execution, starvation, forced labor and disease, about 1.5 to 2 million people died."],["Rebuilding","In 1979 Vietnamese forces drove the Khmer Rouge from power, but conflict continued. The Paris Peace Agreements of 1991 and UN-run elections in 1993 brought peace, and the monarchy was restored. Since then, Cambodia has grown rapidly, though questions about democracy and human rights remain."]],
k:[["Capital","Phnom Penh"],["Population","About 17.6 million (2024)"],["Area","About 181,000 km²"],["Highest point","Phnom Aural, 1,810 m"],["Currency","Riel (US dollar widely used)"]],
d:[["802","The Khmer Empire is founded"],["1863","Cambodia becomes a French protectorate"],["1953","Cambodia becomes independent"],["1975","The Khmer Rouge seize power"],["1979","Vietnamese forces drive the Khmer Rouge from Phnom Penh"],["1991","The Paris Peace Agreements are signed"],["1993","UN-organized elections; the monarchy is restored"]],
s2:[["Remembering the genocide","The Tuol Sleng Genocide Museum in Phnom Penh, a former school used as the S-21 prison, and the Choeung Ek killing fields memorial help Cambodians and visitors remember the victims of the Khmer Rouge. A UN-backed tribunal convicted senior Khmer Rouge leaders, including of genocide, between 2010 and 2018."],["Landmines and hero rats","Decades of war left Cambodia heavily contaminated with landmines and unexploded bombs, which have killed or injured tens of thousands of people. Clearing them is slow, dangerous work. Since 2015, specially trained giant African pouched rats, which are too light to set off mines, have helped sniff them out."],["The Tonlé Sap","The Tonlé Sap lake feeds millions of people with fish. Floating villages of houses on boats and stilts move with the seasons. Each year, the Bon Om Touk water festival in Phnom Penh celebrates the reversal of the river’s flow with boat races."],["Khmer culture","The Khmer language has its own script, descended from ancient Indian writing. Cambodia’s classical Royal Ballet, with dancers in golden costumes performing graceful hand movements, almost disappeared under the Khmer Rouge, when many artists were killed, but survivors revived it. It is recognized by UNESCO."],["Economy","Cambodia’s economy relies on garment and shoe factories, which employ hundreds of thousands of workers, mostly women; farming, especially rice; tourism to Angkor; and construction. Fragrant jasmine rice from Cambodia has won international awards."],["Cambodia and its neighbors","Cambodia borders Thailand, Laos and Vietnam and is a member of ASEAN, which it joined in 1999. It has close relationships with China, which has invested heavily in its infrastructure. Border disputes with Thailand have sometimes caused tension, including fighting near ancient temples on the border."]],
sp:["The river that flows backward","Most of the year, the Tonlé Sap River carries water from the great Tonlé Sap lake into the Mekong. But during the monsoon, from about June to October, the Mekong rises so high that it pushes water back up the Tonlé Sap River into the lake, which can grow from about 2,500 to more than 10,000 square kilometers. Flooded forests become nurseries for fish. When the waters fall, the river flows back again, and fishermen gather a huge harvest."],
g:[["Khmer Rouge","The radical regime that ruled Cambodia from 1975 to 1979"],["Genocide","The deliberate destruction of a group of people"],["Protectorate","A territory controlled and protected by another country"],["Floating village","A community of houses on boats or stilts"],["Landmine","A hidden explosive device placed in the ground"]],
f:["Angkor Wat appears on Cambodia’s flag.","The Tonlé Sap River reverses direction each year.","Rats are trained to detect landmines in Cambodia."],
fun:"Trained giant rats help find landmines in Cambodia.",
tl:"Ang Cambodia ay tagapagmana ng Imperyong Khmer na nagtayo ng Angkor Wat. Dumanas ito ng trahedya sa ilalim ng Khmer Rouge mula 1975 hanggang 1979. Mula 1990s, muling bumangon ang bansa."});

ADDART({id:"laoscountry",t:"Laos",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🐘",w:"Laos",r:["mekong","asean","buddh","vietnam","hydropower","sea"],
p:[["Land of a million elephants","Laos was once the kingdom of Lan Xang, meaning a million elephants."],["Landlocked","It is the only landlocked country in Southeast Asia, with the Mekong River as its lifeline."],["A heavy legacy","Laos is the most heavily bombed country per person in history, from the Vietnam War era."]],
l:"Laos, officially the Lao People’s Democratic Republic, is a landlocked country in mainland Southeast Asia with about 7.7 million people. Its capital is Vientiane, on the Mekong River. Laos is mountainous and forested, with a slow-paced, mainly Buddhist way of life. Once the kingdom of Lan Xang, it later came under Thai and French rule, became independent in 1953 and has been a one-party communist state since 1975. Laos still suffers from millions of unexploded bombs dropped during the Vietnam War era.",
s:[["Land","About 70 percent of Laos is mountains and plateaus, covered with forests. The Mekong River runs along much of its western border with Thailand and is used for transport, fishing and farming. Phou Bia, at 2,819 meters, is the highest point. Laos has many waterfalls, caves and rare wildlife."],["The kingdom of Lan Xang","In 1353 Fa Ngum founded the kingdom of Lan Xang, the land of a million elephants, which became a major Buddhist kingdom. In the 1700s it split into rival kingdoms, which later came under Siamese control. France made Laos part of French Indochina in 1893, and it became independent in 1953."],["Civil war and revolution","From the 1950s, Laos was torn by civil war between the royal government and the communist Pathet Lao, backed by North Vietnam. In 1975 the Pathet Lao took power, ended the monarchy and founded the Lao People’s Democratic Republic, governed by the Lao People’s Revolutionary Party."],["Life and economy","Most Lao people live in rural villages, growing sticky rice, the staple food. Laos exports electricity from hydropower dams to its neighbors, as well as minerals, timber and farm products. The Laos–China Railway, opened in 2021, links Vientiane with China. Tourism is growing."]],
k:[["Capital","Vientiane"],["Population","About 7.7 million (2024)"],["Area","About 236,800 km²"],["Highest point","Phou Bia, 2,819 m"],["Currency","Kip"]],
d:[["1353","Fa Ngum founds the kingdom of Lan Xang"],["1893","Laos becomes part of French Indochina"],["1953","Laos becomes independent"],["1964–1973","Heavy US bombing during the Vietnam War era"],["1975","The Lao People’s Democratic Republic is founded"],["1997","Laos joins ASEAN"],["2021","The Laos–China Railway opens"]],
s2:[["The secret war","During the Vietnam War, the United States conducted a secret bombing campaign in Laos to attack North Vietnamese supply routes, the Ho Chi Minh Trail, and Pathet Lao forces. From 1964 to 1973, it flew about 580,000 bombing missions, dropping more than 2 million metric tons of bombs, making Laos the most heavily bombed country per person in history."],["Unexploded bombs","About a third of the cluster bomblets dropped did not explode. They still lie in fields, forests and villages, killing or injuring people, often children and farmers, decades later. Teams work to clear them, and Laos promotes the international treaty banning cluster munitions."],["Luang Prabang","Luang Prabang, the former royal capital on the Mekong, is a UNESCO World Heritage Site known for its gilded temples, French colonial buildings and calm atmosphere. Every morning at dawn, saffron-robed monks walk through the streets to receive offerings of sticky rice from residents, a ceremony called tak bat."],["The Plain of Jars","On the Xieng Khouang plateau, thousands of large stone jars, some up to 3 meters tall, are scattered across the land. They are about 1,500 to 2,500 years old and were probably used in funeral practices. The site became a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 2019, though some areas still contain unexploded bombs."],["Battery of Southeast Asia","Laos has built many dams on the Mekong and its tributaries to generate hydroelectricity, much of which is sold to Thailand and other neighbors. Supporters see this as a path to development; critics warn that dams affect fish migration, sediment and the livelihoods of millions of people downstream."],["Lao culture","Lao food features sticky rice, eaten by hand, and dishes such as larb, a minced meat salad with herbs and lime, considered a national dish. The khaen, a bamboo mouth organ, is central to Lao music, and Lao khaen music is recognized by UNESCO. The New Year festival, Pi Mai, is celebrated with water splashing in April."]],
sp:["Morning alms in Luang Prabang","Before sunrise in Luang Prabang, hundreds of Buddhist monks in orange robes walk silently in long lines through the streets. Local people kneel along the sidewalks and place small balls of sticky rice and other food into the monks’ bowls. The ritual is a way of making merit, and the monks bless the givers. Visitors are welcome to watch respectfully, without flash photography or getting in the way."],
g:[["Landlocked","Having no coastline"],["Lan Xang","The ancient kingdom of a million elephants"],["Sticky rice","A rice that becomes sticky when cooked, the Lao staple"],["Unexploded ordnance","Bombs and shells that did not explode"],["Alms","Gifts of food or money to monks or the poor"]],
f:["Laos is the only landlocked country in Southeast Asia.","Laos is the most heavily bombed country per person.","Lan Xang means a million elephants."],
fun:"Sticky rice is so important in Laos that Lao people sometimes call themselves children of sticky rice.",
tl:"Ang Laos ay bansang walang dagat sa Timog-silangang Asya. Dati itong kaharian ng Lan Xang, ang lupain ng isang milyong elepante. Ito ang pinakamaraming binomba bawat tao sa kasaysayan noong panahon ng Digmaang Vietnam."});

ADDART({id:"bruneicountry",t:"Brunei",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🕌",w:"Brunei",r:["borneo","asean","islam","malaysiacountry","precol","sulu"],
p:[["A small sultanate","Brunei Darussalam is a small, oil-rich sultanate on the northern coast of Borneo."],["Rainforest nation","Most of the country is still covered in tropical rainforest."],["An old kingdom","In the 1500s, Brunei’s sultans had influence across Borneo and as far as the Philippines."]],
l:"Brunei Darussalam is a small country on the northern coast of the island of Borneo, surrounded by the Malaysian state of Sarawak and the South China Sea. About 460,000 people live there. Brunei is an Islamic sultanate ruled by a sultan as head of state and government. Oil and natural gas, discovered in the 1920s, made it one of the wealthiest countries in Asia, allowing free education and health care and no personal income tax. Much of the country remains covered by pristine rainforest.",
s:[["Land","Brunei is divided into two parts separated by a strip of Malaysian territory. It has a narrow coastal plain, swampy lowlands and hilly, forested interior. The Ulu Temburong National Park protects untouched rainforest, where a canopy walkway lets visitors walk among the treetops."],["The Bruneian Empire","In the 1400s and 1500s, Brunei was a powerful trading sultanate whose influence extended over much of Borneo and parts of the Philippines. Its rulers had ties with Muslim rulers in the Philippines, including in Manila before the Spanish arrived. In 1578 Spanish forces from Manila briefly attacked Brunei in the Castilian War."],["Protectorate and independence","Brunei’s territory shrank in the 1800s as Sarawak and Sabah came under other rulers. In 1888 it became a British protectorate. Oil was found at Seria in 1929. Brunei chose not to join Malaysia in 1963 and became fully independent on 1 January 1984, joining ASEAN that same year."],["Government and society","Brunei is an absolute monarchy based on a national philosophy called Melayu Islam Beraja, Malay Islamic Monarchy. The sultan has ruled since 1967, one of the longest reigns in the world. Most people are Malay Muslims, with Chinese and Indigenous minorities. Islamic law plays an important role."]],
k:[["Capital","Bandar Seri Begawan"],["Population","About 460,000 (2024)"],["Area","About 5,800 km²"],["Highest point","Bukit Pagon, about 1,850 m"],["Currency","Brunei dollar"]],
d:[["1400s","The Sultanate of Brunei rises as a trading power"],["1578","Spanish forces attack Brunei in the Castilian War"],["1888","Brunei becomes a British protectorate"],["1929","Oil is discovered at Seria"],["1967","The current sultan begins his reign"],["1984","Brunei becomes independent and joins ASEAN"],["2020","The Temburong Bridge opens"]],
s2:[["Kampong Ayer","Kampong Ayer, a water village on stilts over the Brunei River in the capital, has been inhabited for centuries and is sometimes called the Venice of the East. Thousands of people live in houses connected by wooden walkways, with schools, mosques and shops, and water taxis carry residents to the city."],["Oil wealth","Oil and gas make up most of Brunei’s exports and government income. The wealth has paid for free health care and education, subsidized housing and no personal income tax. Because oil will not last forever, Brunei is working to diversify its economy into areas such as tourism, fisheries and petrochemicals."],["Mosques","The Omar Ali Saifuddien Mosque, completed in 1958, with its golden dome rising beside a lagoon, is a symbol of Brunei. The larger Jame’ Asr Hassanil Bolkiah Mosque has 29 golden domes. Islam shapes daily life, and the sale of alcohol is banned."],["Rainforest protection","Unlike many tropical countries, Brunei has kept most of its rainforest intact, partly because oil wealth reduced pressure to log or farm the land. Its forests are home to proboscis monkeys, hornbills and clouded leopards, and they form part of the Heart of Borneo conservation initiative."],["Links with the Philippines","Brunei and the Philippines share long historical ties through trade, Islam and royal families. Some Filipino Muslim rulers traced connections to Brunei’s sultans, and Brunei is home to many Filipino workers today. Both countries are ASEAN members."],["The Temburong Bridge","Opened in 2020, the Temburong Bridge, about 30 kilometers long, crosses Brunei Bay to link the main part of the country with the separate Temburong district, which previously could be reached mainly by boat or by driving through Malaysia."]],
sp:["A water village capital","Kampong Ayer has been described by visitors for over 500 years. When the expedition of Ferdinand Magellan reached Brunei in 1521, after Magellan’s death in the Philippines, the chronicler Antonio Pigafetta described a great city built over water, with tens of thousands of houses on stilts. Today the village has modern amenities, including electricity, piped water and even fire stations, but keeps its traditional way of life on the water."],
g:[["Sultanate","A state ruled by a sultan"],["Absolute monarchy","A monarchy in which the ruler has full power"],["Protectorate","A territory protected and partly controlled by another country"],["Stilt house","A house raised on posts above water or ground"],["Diversify","To develop a wider range of industries"]],
f:["Brunei has no personal income tax.","Kampong Ayer is a village built on stilts over water.","Brunei became independent in 1984."],
fun:"Pigafetta, Magellan’s chronicler, described Brunei’s water village in 1521.",
tl:"Ang Brunei ay maliit na sultanato sa hilagang baybayin ng Borneo na mayaman sa langis. Karamihan nito ay natatakpan pa ng gubat. Noong 1500s, umabot ang impluwensiya ng sultan ng Brunei hanggang sa Pilipinas."});

ADDART({id:"timorlestecountry",t:"Timor-Leste",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🕊️",w:"Timor-Leste",r:["asean","indonesiacountry","christ","decol","hrights","australiacountry"],
p:[["A young nation","Timor-Leste, or East Timor, became independent in 2002, one of the world’s newest countries."],["Hard-won freedom","Its people endured a long struggle under Indonesian occupation before voting for independence in 1999."],["ASEAN’s newest member","In 2025 Timor-Leste became the 11th member of ASEAN."]],
l:"Timor-Leste, also called East Timor, is a small country occupying the eastern half of the island of Timor, north of Australia. About 1.4 million people live there, and its capital is Dili. A Portuguese colony for centuries, it was invaded by Indonesia in 1975 and occupied until 1999, a period in which very many Timorese died. After a UN-organized vote in which Timorese overwhelmingly chose independence, it became a sovereign state on 20 May 2002. Like the Philippines, it is a mainly Catholic country, and in 2025 it joined ASEAN.",
s:[["Land","Timor-Leste includes the eastern half of Timor, the enclave of Oecusse in the west, and the islands of Atauro and Jaco. It is mountainous, with Mount Ramelau rising to about 2,986 meters. The waters around it are part of the Coral Triangle and are among the most biodiverse in the world, visited by whales and dolphins."],["Colonial history","Portuguese traders arrived in the 1500s, drawn by sandalwood. Portugal ruled East Timor for centuries, while the Dutch controlled West Timor, now part of Indonesia. When Portugal began to withdraw from its colonies in 1975, Timorese parties competed for power, and Fretilin declared independence on 28 November 1975."],["Occupation","On 7 December 1975, Indonesia invaded and later annexed East Timor. During the occupation, which lasted until 1999, a very large number of Timorese died from violence, famine and disease; a truth commission estimated more than 100,000 conflict-related deaths. Timorese resistance continued in the mountains and through a clandestine network and diplomacy abroad."],["Independence","After political change in Indonesia, a UN-organized referendum was held on 30 August 1999, and 78.5 percent voted for independence. Pro-Indonesian militias then carried out widespread violence and destruction, until an international peacekeeping force led by Australia arrived. After a period of UN administration, Timor-Leste became independent on 20 May 2002."]],
k:[["Capital","Dili"],["Population","About 1.4 million (2024)"],["Area","About 14,900 km²"],["Highest point","Mount Ramelau, about 2,986 m"],["Currency","US dollar"]],
d:[["1500s","Portuguese traders arrive"],["1975","Independence is declared; Indonesia invades"],["1991","The Santa Cruz massacre in Dili draws world attention"],["1996","Carlos Belo and José Ramos-Horta win the Nobel Peace Prize"],["1999","Timorese vote for independence"],["2002","Timor-Leste becomes independent"],["2025","Timor-Leste joins ASEAN"]],
s2:[["Nobel Peace Prize","In 1996 Bishop Carlos Filipe Ximenes Belo and José Ramos-Horta, a resistance diplomat, were awarded the Nobel Peace Prize for their work toward a just and peaceful solution to the conflict. Ramos-Horta later served as president and prime minister. The resistance leader Xanana Gusmão became the first president."],["The Santa Cruz massacre","On 12 November 1991, Indonesian troops opened fire on a peaceful procession at the Santa Cruz cemetery in Dili, killing many young Timorese. Foreign journalists filmed the shooting, and the footage, smuggled out of the country, brought worldwide attention to the occupation."],["Languages","Timor-Leste has two official languages, Tetum and Portuguese, while Indonesian and English are working languages, and more than a dozen local languages are spoken. Most Timorese are Catholic, and the Church played an important role in supporting people during the occupation."],["Building a nation","After independence, Timor-Leste had to build institutions almost from scratch, as much infrastructure had been destroyed in 1999. It faces challenges of poverty, malnutrition and jobs for young people. It has strong democratic elections and is ranked among the freest countries in Asia for press freedom."],["Oil and the sea border","Timor-Leste depends heavily on revenue from oil and gas in the Timor Sea, saved in a petroleum fund. In 2018, after years of dispute and a UN conciliation process, Timor-Leste and Australia signed a treaty setting their maritime boundary. Diversifying the economy beyond oil is a key goal."],["Joining ASEAN","Timor-Leste applied to join ASEAN in 2011 and was admitted as its 11th member in October 2025, at a summit in Kuala Lumpur. The Philippines was among its supporters. Membership is expected to boost trade, investment and regional ties."]],
sp:["Two Catholic nations of Asia","Timor-Leste and the Philippines are the only two predominantly Catholic countries in Asia, and both received their faith through Iberian colonizers, Portugal and Spain. During Timor-Leste’s struggle, the Catholic Church became a refuge and a voice for its people. Pope Francis visited Timor-Leste in 2024, where an outdoor Mass drew about 600,000 people, almost half the country’s population."],
g:[["Referendum","A vote by all citizens on a single question"],["Occupation","Control of a territory by a foreign military"],["Tetum","An official language of Timor-Leste"],["Peacekeeping force","International troops sent to keep peace"],["Truth commission","A body that investigates past abuses"]],
f:["Timor-Leste became independent in 2002.","Its official languages are Tetum and Portuguese.","Timor-Leste joined ASEAN in 2025."],
fun:"A papal Mass in Timor-Leste in 2024 drew nearly half the country’s population.",
tl:"Ang Timor-Leste ay batang bansa na naging malaya noong 2002 matapos ang mahabang pakikibaka. Tulad ng Pilipinas, karamihan ng mamamayan nito ay Katoliko. Noong 2025, naging ika-11 miyembro ito ng ASEAN."});

