ADDART({id:"bangladeshcountry",t:"Bangladesh",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🐅",w:"Bangladesh",r:["ganges","partition","wetlands","indiacountry","storms","myanmarcountry"],
p:[["Land of rivers","Bangladesh sits on the world’s largest river delta, formed by the Ganges, Brahmaputra and Meghna rivers."],["Many people","With about 173 million people, it is one of the most densely populated countries on Earth."],["Mother language","Its fight to use the Bengali language gave the world International Mother Language Day."]],
l:"Bangladesh is a country in South Asia, almost surrounded by India, with a short border with Myanmar and a coast on the Bay of Bengal. About 173 million people live there, making it the eighth most populous country in the world. Its capital is Dhaka. Bangladesh lies mostly on the vast delta of the Ganges, Brahmaputra and Meghna rivers, making it fertile but vulnerable to floods and cyclones. Once East Pakistan, it won independence in 1971 after a war of liberation. It has become a major exporter of clothing and has made great progress in health and education.",
s:[["Land and water","Most of Bangladesh is low, flat delta land crossed by hundreds of rivers. Monsoon rains and melting Himalayan snow bring floods that renew the soil but can also destroy homes and crops. In the southwest, the Sundarbans, the largest mangrove forest in the world, is home to the Bengal tiger. Hills rise in the southeast."],["History","Bengal was home to ancient kingdoms and, from the 1200s, Muslim sultanates and later the Mughal Empire, becoming one of the richest regions of South Asia. Under British rule, Bengal was partitioned in 1947: its eastern part became East Pakistan, separated from West Pakistan by about 1,600 kilometers of India."],["Language and liberation","When Pakistan’s government declared Urdu the only national language, Bengali speakers protested. On 21 February 1952, police killed several student protesters in Dhaka. Tensions grew, and in 1971 the Pakistani army launched a crackdown. Bangladesh declared independence on 26 March 1971 and, with Indian help, won the war on 16 December 1971. Hundreds of thousands, by many estimates far more, were killed, and millions fled."],["Economy","Bangladesh is the world’s second-largest exporter of clothing, after China, employing millions of workers, most of them women. Its economy has grown rapidly, and poverty has fallen sharply. Remittances from Bangladeshis working abroad, rice and fish farming, and jute, the golden fiber, are also important."]],
k:[["Capital","Dhaka"],["Population","About 173 million (2024)"],["Area","About 148,000 km²"],["Language","Bengali (Bangla)"],["Currency","Taka"]],
d:[["1947","Bengal is partitioned; East Pakistan is created"],["1952","Students are killed in the Language Movement on 21 February"],["1970","The Bhola cyclone kills hundreds of thousands"],["1971","Bangladesh wins independence"],["1999","UNESCO declares International Mother Language Day"],["2006","Muhammad Yunus and Grameen Bank win the Nobel Peace Prize"],["2024","Student-led protests lead to a change of government"]],
s2:[["International Mother Language Day","To honor the students killed on 21 February 1952 while defending the Bengali language, UNESCO declared 21 February International Mother Language Day in 1999. It is observed worldwide to promote linguistic diversity and education in mother tongues. In Bangladesh, people lay flowers at the Shaheed Minar, the martyrs’ monument, in Dhaka."],["Microcredit","In 1976 the economist Muhammad Yunus began lending small amounts of money to poor women in villages to start small businesses, without requiring collateral. His Grameen Bank grew to serve millions of borrowers, and the idea of microcredit spread worldwide. Yunus and the bank won the Nobel Peace Prize in 2006."],["The Sundarbans","The Sundarbans, shared with India, is a maze of mangrove forests, tidal rivers and islands covering about 10,000 square kilometers. It protects the coast from storms and is home to Bengal tigers that swim between islands, as well as crocodiles, river dolphins and spotted deer. It is a UNESCO World Heritage Site."],["Climate and disasters","Bangladesh is among the countries most vulnerable to climate change. Rising seas threaten coastal land with saltwater, and cyclones and floods affect millions. Thanks to early warning systems and thousands of cyclone shelters, deaths from cyclones have fallen dramatically since the 1970s."],["Garment workers and safety","In April 2013, the Rana Plaza building near Dhaka, which housed garment factories, collapsed, killing more than 1,100 workers. The disaster led to international agreements to improve factory safety and workers’ rights in the clothing industry."],["Rohingya refugees","Since 2017, Bangladesh has hosted about a million Rohingya refugees who fled violence in Myanmar, mostly in camps near Cox’s Bazar, one of the largest refugee settlements in the world. Cox’s Bazar is also known for one of the world’s longest natural sea beaches."]],
sp:["The 1971 Liberation War","In March 1971, after the Awami League led by Sheikh Mujibur Rahman won elections in Pakistan but was not allowed to form a government, the Pakistani army launched a brutal crackdown in East Pakistan. Bangladeshi freedom fighters, the Mukti Bahini, resisted, and about 10 million refugees fled to India. In December India entered the war, and on 16 December 1971 Pakistani forces surrendered in Dhaka. The day is celebrated as Victory Day, and Sheikh Mujibur Rahman is honored as the founding father of Bangladesh."],
g:[["Delta","Land formed where a river splits and meets the sea"],["Mangrove","A tree that grows in salty coastal water"],["Microcredit","Small loans to poor people to start businesses"],["Jute","A plant fiber used for sacks and rope"],["Remittance","Money sent home by workers abroad"]],
f:["Bangladesh lies on the world’s largest river delta.","21 February is International Mother Language Day.","The Sundarbans is the largest mangrove forest in the world."],
fun:"International Mother Language Day began with Bangladesh’s struggle for the Bengali language.",
tl:"Ang Bangladesh ay bansa sa Timog Asya na nasa pinakamalaking delta ng ilog sa mundo. Isa ito sa pinakamataong bansa. Naging malaya ito noong 1971, at dahil sa pakikibaka nito para sa wikang Bengali, ipinagdiriwang ang International Mother Language Day."});

ADDART({id:"israelcountry",t:"Israel",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🕍",w:"Israel",r:["middleeast","judaism","holocaust","lakes","christ","islam"],
p:[["A small, ancient land","Israel lies on the eastern shore of the Mediterranean, in a land sacred to Jews, Christians and Muslims."],["A modern state","The State of Israel was founded in 1948 as a homeland for the Jewish people."],["A land in conflict","Its history has been marked by conflict with Palestinians and neighboring states, and by efforts at peace."]],
l:"Israel is a country in the Middle East on the eastern coast of the Mediterranean Sea, with about 10 million people. It declared independence in 1948 as a Jewish state, following centuries of Jewish connection to the land, the rise of the Zionist movement and the Holocaust. About three-quarters of its citizens are Jewish and about a fifth are Arab. Israel is a parliamentary democracy with a high-technology economy. Its founding and policies are closely tied to the long Israeli–Palestinian conflict over land, security and Palestinian statehood.",
s:[["Land","Israel is small but varied, with a fertile coastal plain, hills around Jerusalem and Galilee, the Jordan Valley and the Negev Desert, which covers more than half its land. The Dead Sea, on its border with Jordan, is the lowest point on land. The Sea of Galilee is its main freshwater lake."],["Ancient roots","The Hebrew Bible tells of the ancient Israelites, and the kingdoms of Israel and Judah existed in the region about 3,000 years ago. After revolts against Rome in the first and second centuries CE, many Jews were exiled, forming communities around the world, called the diaspora. Small Jewish communities remained, and the land later came under Byzantine, Arab, Crusader, Mamluk and Ottoman rule."],["Founding","In the late 1800s, the Zionist movement called for a Jewish homeland in the Land of Israel, and Jewish immigration grew under Ottoman and then British rule, as did conflict with the Arab population. After the Holocaust, the UN proposed partition in 1947. Israel declared independence on 14 May 1948, followed by war with Arab states and the displacement of hundreds of thousands of Palestinians."],["Society and economy","Israel’s population includes Jews whose families came from Europe, the Middle East, North Africa, Ethiopia and elsewhere, as well as Arab citizens, Druze and others. Hebrew and Arabic are widely spoken. Israel has a strong economy in technology, medicine, agriculture and defense, and is known for many start-up companies."]],
k:[["Capital","Jerusalem (status disputed internationally)"],["Population","About 10 million (2025)"],["Area","About 22,000 km² (within pre-1967 lines)"],["Lowest point","Dead Sea shore, about 430 m below sea level"],["Currency","New shekel"]],
d:[["c. 1000 BCE","The kingdom of Israel is established"],["70 CE","The Romans destroy the Second Temple in Jerusalem"],["1917","The Balfour Declaration supports a Jewish national home"],["1948","Israel declares independence"],["1967","The Six-Day War"],["1979","Israel and Egypt sign a peace treaty"],["2023","The Hamas attack of 7 October and the war in Gaza"]],
s2:[["Reviving Hebrew","For centuries, Hebrew was used mainly in prayer and study. In the late 1800s, Eliezer Ben-Yehuda and others worked to revive it as an everyday spoken language, creating new words for modern life. Today Hebrew is spoken by millions, one of the only examples of a language revived for daily use."],["Jerusalem","Jerusalem is holy to Jews, Christians and Muslims. Israel considers Jerusalem its capital, while Palestinians seek East Jerusalem as the capital of a future state. Most countries keep their embassies in Tel Aviv, though some have moved them to Jerusalem. Its status is among the most sensitive issues in the conflict."],["The Palestinians","The West Bank and Gaza Strip, occupied by Israel in 1967, are home to millions of Palestinians. The Palestinian Authority administers parts of the West Bank, while Hamas has governed Gaza since 2007. Most UN member states recognize a State of Palestine, though its borders and status remain unresolved. Israeli settlements in the West Bank are considered illegal under international law by most of the international community, which Israel disputes."],["Water and farming","Israel is largely dry, so it has become a world leader in water technology. Drip irrigation, developed there in the 1960s, delivers water directly to plant roots and is used worldwide. Israel desalinates seawater for much of its drinking water and recycles most of its wastewater for farming."],["Filipinos in Israel","Tens of thousands of Filipinos work in Israel, many as caregivers for elderly people, where they are widely appreciated. Filipino workers were among those killed and taken hostage in the attack of 7 October 2023, and the Philippine government has assisted its citizens during conflicts."],["Kibbutzim","A kibbutz is a community where members traditionally shared property and work, often in farming. The first was founded in 1910. Kibbutzim played a large role in early Israeli society; many have since changed, with members earning individual salaries, but the idea of communal living remains influential."]],
sp:["The Dead Sea","The Dead Sea, between Israel, the West Bank and Jordan, has a shore about 430 meters below sea level, the lowest point on land. Its water is almost ten times saltier than the ocean, so people float easily, and almost nothing lives in it except some microbes. Its mineral-rich mud is used in cosmetics. The sea is shrinking by about a meter a year because water from the Jordan River is diverted for farming and drinking."],
g:[["Zionism","A movement for a Jewish homeland in the Land of Israel"],["Diaspora","A people living scattered away from their homeland"],["Kibbutz","A communal settlement in Israel"],["Occupation","Military control of a territory"],["Drip irrigation","Watering plants slowly at their roots"]],
f:["Israel was founded in 1948.","Hebrew was revived as a spoken language.","The Dead Sea is the lowest point on land."],
fun:"Israel recycles most of its wastewater to water crops.",
tl:"Ang Israel ay bansa sa silangang baybayin ng Mediterranean na itinatag noong 1948 bilang tahanan ng mga Hudyo. Banal ang lupain nito sa mga Hudyo, Kristiyano at Muslim. Kaugnay ng kasaysayan nito ang matagal na tunggalian sa mga Palestino."});

ADDART({id:"ukrainecountry",t:"Ukraine",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🌻",w:"Ukraine",r:["russiacountry","ussrfall","wheat","eu","nuclear","coldwar"],
p:[["Largest in Europe","Ukraine is the largest country entirely within Europe."],["Breadbasket","Its rich black soil makes it one of the world’s great grain and sunflower oil producers."],["Defending independence","Since 2022, Ukraine has been defending itself against a full-scale Russian invasion."]],
l:"Ukraine is a country in Eastern Europe, bordering Russia, Belarus, Poland, Slovakia, Hungary, Romania, Moldova and the Black Sea. Before 2022, about 41 million people lived in government-controlled areas; millions have since fled abroad or been displaced. Its capital is Kyiv, one of the oldest cities in Eastern Europe. Ukraine has a rich history going back to the medieval state of Kyivan Rus’. Part of the Soviet Union for most of the 20th century, it became independent in 1991. Russia annexed Crimea in 2014 and launched a full-scale invasion in February 2022.",
s:[["Land","Ukraine is mostly flat, with vast fertile plains called steppes and a famous rich black soil, chornozem. The Carpathian Mountains rise in the west, where Hoverla reaches 2,061 meters. The Dnipro River flows through the middle of the country into the Black Sea. The Crimean Peninsula juts into the Black Sea in the south."],["Kyivan Rus’","In the 800s, a state called Kyivan Rus’ grew around Kyiv, trading along rivers between Scandinavia and the Byzantine Empire. In 988 Prince Volodymyr adopted Orthodox Christianity. Ukrainians, Belarusians and Russians all trace part of their heritage to Kyivan Rus’. It was destroyed by the Mongol invasion of 1240."],["Under other powers","For centuries, Ukrainian lands were ruled by Lithuania, Poland, the Russian Empire and Austria-Hungary. In the 1600s, Cossacks formed a self-governing Hetmanate. After World War I, Ukraine briefly declared independence but became part of the Soviet Union in 1922. In 1932 and 1933, a famine caused by Soviet policies, known as the Holodomor, killed millions of Ukrainians."],["Independence and war","Ukraine declared independence on 24 August 1991, confirmed by more than 90 percent of voters in a referendum. Protests led to political changes in 2004 and 2014. In 2014 Russia annexed Crimea, a move not recognized by most countries, and backed separatists in the eastern Donbas region. On 24 February 2022, Russia launched a full-scale invasion, starting the largest war in Europe since World War II."]],
k:[["Capital","Kyiv"],["Population","About 41 million before 2022; now uncertain because of war"],["Area","About 603,500 km²"],["Highest point","Hoverla, 2,061 m"],["Currency","Hryvnia"]],
d:[["988","Prince Volodymyr adopts Christianity"],["1240","The Mongols destroy Kyiv"],["1922","Ukraine becomes part of the Soviet Union"],["1932–1933","The Holodomor famine"],["1986","The Chernobyl nuclear disaster"],["1991","Ukraine becomes independent"],["2022","Russia launches a full-scale invasion"]],
s2:[["The Holodomor","In 1932 and 1933, Soviet policies of forced collectivization and grain seizures caused a catastrophic famine in Ukraine. Millions of people starved to death. Ukraine and many other countries recognize the Holodomor, meaning death by hunger, as a genocide against Ukrainians. It is remembered each year on the fourth Saturday of November."],["Chernobyl","On 26 April 1986, a reactor at the Chernobyl nuclear power plant in northern Ukraine exploded during a test, releasing radiation across Europe. The nearby city of Pripyat was evacuated, and an exclusion zone of about 2,600 square kilometers remains. It was the worst nuclear accident in history and contributed to distrust of the Soviet government."],["The war since 2022","Russia’s full-scale invasion in February 2022 was met by fierce Ukrainian resistance, which prevented the capture of Kyiv. The war has killed and wounded very large numbers of soldiers and civilians, destroyed cities and infrastructure and forced millions of Ukrainians to flee abroad. Many countries have supported Ukraine with aid and imposed sanctions on Russia, while efforts toward negotiations have continued."],["Breadbasket of the world","Ukraine is one of the world’s largest exporters of wheat, maize, barley and sunflower oil, feeding people in Asia, Africa and the Middle East. The war disrupted these exports through the Black Sea, raising food prices worldwide. The national flag’s blue and yellow are often said to represent the sky over golden wheat fields."],["Culture","Ukraine has a rich culture of folk music, embroidery called vyshyvanka, painted Easter eggs called pysanky, and dishes such as borscht, a beet soup recognized by UNESCO in 2022. The poet Taras Shevchenko, of the 1800s, is a national hero. Ukrainian bands have won the Eurovision Song Contest three times."],["Toward Europe","Ukraine has sought closer ties with the European Union. It received EU candidate status in 2022, and accession negotiations formally opened in 2024. Ukrainians see this as part of a long effort to secure their independence and democracy."]],
sp:["The black soil","About two-thirds of Ukraine is covered by chornozem, black earth, one of the most fertile soils in the world. Formed over thousands of years from grasses that grew and decayed on the steppe, it is deep, dark and rich in humus. It is said that during World War II, soldiers carried trainloads of this soil away. This soil is why Ukraine has long been called the breadbasket of Europe."],
g:[["Steppe","A large, flat, treeless grassland"],["Kyivan Rus’","A medieval state centered on Kyiv"],["Holodomor","The man-made famine of 1932–1933 in Ukraine"],["Annexation","Taking over a territory by force or decree"],["Chornozem","Fertile black soil of the steppes"]],
f:["Ukraine is the largest country entirely in Europe.","The Chernobyl disaster happened in 1986.","Ukraine became independent in 1991."],
fun:"Ukrainian borscht was recognized by UNESCO as intangible cultural heritage in 2022.",
tl:"Ang Ukraine ay pinakamalaking bansa na buong nasa Europa. Kilala ito bilang taniman ng trigo at mirasol. Naging malaya ito noong 1991, at mula 2022, ipinagtatanggol nito ang sarili laban sa malawakang pagsalakay ng Russia."});

ADDART({id:"greececountry",t:"Greece",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🏛️",w:"Greece",r:["greece","gmyth","olymp","byzant","ottoman","eu"],
p:[["Cradle of the West","Greece is the birthplace of democracy, philosophy, the Olympic Games and theater."],["Thousands of islands","It has about 6,000 islands and islets, of which around 200 are inhabited."],["Sun and sea","Its beaches, ancient ruins and food attract tens of millions of visitors."]],
l:"Greece is a country in southeastern Europe at the southern end of the Balkan Peninsula, with about 10.4 million people. Its capital is Athens. Greece has thousands of islands in the Aegean and Ionian seas. Ancient Greece gave the world democracy, philosophy, mathematics, theater and the Olympic Games. After centuries of Roman, Byzantine and Ottoman rule, modern Greece won independence in the 1820s. Today it is a member of the European Union and NATO, with a large shipping industry and tourism.",
s:[["Land","Greece is mountainous, with Mount Olympus, the legendary home of the gods, rising to 2,917 meters. Its long, jagged coastline and many islands, including Crete, Rhodes, Corfu and the Cyclades, mean that no place in Greece is far from the sea. The climate is Mediterranean, with hot, dry summers."],["From ancient to Byzantine","Ancient Greek city-states such as Athens and Sparta flourished from about 800 BCE. Greek culture spread widely after Alexander the Great. Greece came under Roman rule, and later the Greek-speaking Byzantine Empire, centered on Constantinople, ruled for about a thousand years. The Ottoman Turks conquered Greece in the 1400s."],["Independence","Greeks rose against Ottoman rule in 1821. With help from Britain, France and Russia, Greece became independent, recognized in 1830. Over the following century, it gradually expanded to its current borders. Greece suffered occupation in World War II, a civil war from 1946 to 1949 and a military dictatorship from 1967 to 1974, before restoring democracy."],["Modern Greece","Greece joined the European Community, now the EU, in 1981 and adopted the euro in 2001. A severe debt crisis from 2009 led to bailouts, deep spending cuts and high unemployment, but the economy has since recovered. Tourism, shipping, farming and services are its main industries."]],
k:[["Capital","Athens"],["Population","About 10.4 million (2024)"],["Area","About 132,000 km²"],["Highest point","Mount Olympus, 2,917 m"],["Currency","Euro"]],
d:[["c. 776 BCE","The first recorded Olympic Games"],["c. 508 BCE","Democracy begins in Athens"],["1453","Constantinople falls to the Ottomans"],["1821","The Greek War of Independence begins"],["1896","Athens hosts the first modern Olympic Games"],["1974","Democracy is restored"],["1981","Greece joins the European Community"]],
s2:[["The Acropolis","Above Athens rises the Acropolis, a rocky hill crowned by the Parthenon, a temple to the goddess Athena built in the 400s BCE. It is one of the most famous buildings in the world and a symbol of Western civilization. Greece has long called for the return of the Parthenon sculptures taken to Britain in the early 1800s and now in the British Museum."],["The Olympics come home","The ancient Olympic Games were held at Olympia from 776 BCE. In 1896 Athens hosted the first modern Olympics, and in 2004 it hosted them again. At every Olympics, the flame is lit at Olympia using the Sun’s rays and carried to the host city, and Greece leads the parade of nations at the opening ceremony."],["A seafaring nation","Greek shipowners control one of the largest merchant fleets in the world. Many of the sailors on Greek-owned ships are Filipinos, as the Philippines is one of the world’s largest suppliers of seafarers. Ports such as Piraeus, near Athens, are among the busiest in the Mediterranean."],["Greek food","Greek cuisine is based on olive oil, vegetables, grains, fish, cheese such as feta, yogurt, herbs and wine. Popular dishes include moussaka, souvlaki, Greek salad and spanakopita. The Mediterranean diet, recognized by UNESCO, is linked to good health."],["The Greek language","Greek has been written for over 3,000 years, one of the longest-recorded languages. The Greek alphabet, adapted from the Phoenician, gave rise to the Latin and Cyrillic alphabets. Many English words in science and medicine, such as biology, democracy and telephone, come from Greek."],["Islands","The Greek islands are famous for whitewashed villages, blue-domed churches and clear seas, as on Santorini and Mykonos. Crete, the largest island, was home to the ancient Minoan civilization, whose palace at Knossos is linked to the myth of the Minotaur."]],
sp:["Democracy is born","In about 508 BCE, the Athenian leader Cleisthenes introduced reforms that gave power to an assembly of citizens. All free adult male citizens could attend, speak and vote on laws and decisions, a system called demokratia, rule by the people. Women, enslaved people and foreigners were excluded, so it was far from complete. But the idea that citizens could govern themselves became one of the most influential in history."],
g:[["City-state","An independent city with its own government"],["Acropolis","A fortified hill in an ancient Greek city"],["Democracy","Government by the people"],["Archipelago","A group of islands"],["Bailout","Financial help to prevent collapse"]],
f:["Democracy began in Athens around 508 BCE.","Greece has about 6,000 islands and islets.","Athens hosted the first modern Olympics in 1896."],
fun:"The Olympic flame for every Games is lit at Olympia in Greece using the Sun’s rays.",
tl:"Ang Gresya ay bansa sa timog-silangang Europa na pinagmulan ng demokrasya, pilosopiya at Palarong Olimpiko. Mayroon itong libu-libong isla. Maraming Pilipinong mandaragat ang nagtatrabaho sa mga barkong pag-aari ng mga Griyego."});

ADDART({id:"swedencountry",t:"Sweden",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🌲",w:"Sweden",r:["viking","eu","arctic","indig","trees","bicycle"],
p:[["Land of forests and lakes","Sweden is a large Nordic country covered by forests and nearly 100,000 lakes."],["Midnight sun","In its far north, the Sun does not set for weeks in summer."],["Nobel’s homeland","Sweden is home to the Nobel Prizes, founded by the inventor Alfred Nobel."]],
l:"Sweden is a Nordic country in northern Europe on the Scandinavian Peninsula, with about 10.6 million people. Its capital is Stockholm. Most of Sweden is covered by forests and lakes, and its far north lies within the Arctic Circle. Sweden is known for its high standard of living, strong welfare system, environmental protection and companies such as Volvo, IKEA, Ericsson and Spotify. It is a constitutional monarchy and parliamentary democracy, a member of the European Union since 1995 and of NATO since 2024.",
s:[["Land","Sweden stretches about 1,600 kilometers from north to south. The north has mountains, including Kebnekaise, about 2,100 meters, the highest peak, and is home to reindeer and the Sámi people. The south has farmland and many lakes. About 70 percent of the country is forest. Winters are long and dark in the north, while summers have very long days."],["History","Swedish Vikings traveled east along the rivers of Russia and Ukraine from the 700s to the 1000s. In 1523 Gustav Vasa became king, ending the Kalmar Union with Denmark. In the 1600s, Sweden became a great power controlling much of the Baltic region, until its defeat by Russia in the Great Northern War, ending in 1721."],["Neutrality","Sweden stayed out of wars for more than 200 years, from 1814, remaining neutral in both world wars. During the Cold War, it kept a policy of non-alignment. After Russia’s full-scale invasion of Ukraine in 2022, Sweden applied to join NATO and became a member in March 2024."],["Society","Sweden has a strong welfare state, with free education, universal health care, generous parental leave and pensions, funded by high taxes. It has a high level of gender equality. Swedes value lagom, meaning just the right amount, and fika, a coffee break with pastries."]],
k:[["Capital","Stockholm"],["Population","About 10.6 million (2024)"],["Area","About 450,000 km²"],["Highest point","Kebnekaise, about 2,100 m"],["Currency","Swedish krona"]],
d:[["700s–1000s","The Viking Age"],["1523","Gustav Vasa becomes king"],["1814","Sweden’s last war; a long period of peace begins"],["1901","The first Nobel Prizes are awarded"],["1995","Sweden joins the European Union"],["2018","Greta Thunberg begins her school strike for climate"],["2024","Sweden joins NATO"]],
s2:[["The Nobel Prizes","Alfred Nobel, a Swedish chemist who invented dynamite, left most of his fortune in his will to fund prizes for those who have conferred the greatest benefit to humankind. The first Nobel Prizes were awarded in 1901. The prizes in physics, chemistry, medicine, literature and economics are presented in Stockholm each December, while the Peace Prize is awarded in Oslo, Norway."],["The Sámi","The Sámi are the Indigenous people of northern Sweden, Norway, Finland and Russia’s Kola Peninsula. Many have traditionally herded reindeer. The Sámi have their own languages, colorful clothing and a singing tradition called joik. Sweden has a Sámi Parliament, and the Sámi continue to work for their land and cultural rights."],["The right to roam","Sweden’s allemansrätten, the right of public access, allows everyone to walk, camp, swim and pick berries and mushrooms on most land, even privately owned, as long as they respect nature and privacy. This tradition reflects Swedes’ love of outdoor life."],["Swedish innovations","Swedish inventions and companies include the safety match, the three-point seatbelt, introduced by Volvo in 1959, the pacemaker, the Tetra Pak carton and the music streaming service Spotify, founded in 2006. IKEA, founded in 1943, sells flat-pack furniture around the world."],["Music","Sweden is one of the world’s biggest exporters of pop music. ABBA won the Eurovision Song Contest in 1974 with Waterloo and became one of the best-selling bands in history. Swedish songwriters and producers have written hits for many international stars."],["Climate action","Sweden was one of the first countries to introduce a carbon tax, in 1991, and gets most of its electricity from hydropower and nuclear power. In 2018 the Swedish teenager Greta Thunberg began a school strike outside parliament, inspiring a global youth climate movement."]],
sp:["The Ice Hotel","Every winter since 1989, in the village of Jukkasjärvi in northern Sweden, artists build a hotel entirely from ice and snow taken from the Torne River. Guests sleep in rooms carved with ice sculptures, on beds covered with reindeer skins, at temperatures of around minus 5°C. In spring, the hotel melts back into the river. It inspired ice hotels in other cold countries."],
g:[["Nordic","Relating to Scandinavia, Finland and Iceland"],["Welfare state","A state that provides services such as health care and pensions"],["Sámi","The Indigenous people of northern Scandinavia"],["Neutrality","Not taking sides in wars"],["Fika","A Swedish coffee break"]],
f:["The first Nobel Prizes were awarded in 1901.","Sweden joined NATO in 2024.","Volvo introduced the three-point seatbelt in 1959."],
fun:"Sweden’s Ice Hotel is rebuilt from river ice every winter.",
tl:"Ang Sweden ay bansang Nordic sa hilagang Europa na natatakpan ng gubat at lawa. Dito nagmula ang Nobel Prize. Kilala ito sa mataas na pamantayan ng pamumuhay at sa mga kompanyang gaya ng IKEA at Volvo."});

ADDART({id:"cubacountry",t:"Cuba",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🎺",w:"Cuba",r:["cubancrisis","coldwar","sugar","slavetrade","phrev","usacountry"],
p:[["Pearl of the Antilles","Cuba is the largest island in the Caribbean."],["A communist state","In 1959 Fidel Castro’s revolution created a communist state, which still governs Cuba."],["Music and culture","Cuba is famous for its music, dance, baseball and classic cars."]],
l:"Cuba is an island country in the Caribbean Sea, about 150 kilometers south of Florida. About 10 million people live there, and its capital is Havana. A Spanish colony for nearly four centuries, Cuba became independent in 1902 after wars of independence and the Spanish–American War of 1898, the same war that ended Spanish rule in the Philippines. In 1959 Fidel Castro led a revolution that established a communist government closely tied to the Soviet Union. Cuba is known for its music, literacy, health care, cigars and colorful old cities, but also for economic hardship and limits on political freedom.",
s:[["Land","Cuba is long and narrow, about 1,200 kilometers from east to west. It has fertile plains where sugarcane and tobacco grow, and mountains, including the Sierra Maestra, where Pico Turquino rises to 1,974 meters. Its coasts have beaches, coral reefs and many small islands."],["Spanish colony","Columbus reached Cuba in 1492. The Indigenous Taíno population was devastated by violence and disease. Spain made Cuba a center of sugar production, worked by hundreds of thousands of enslaved Africans; slavery was abolished only in 1886. Cuba became one of Spain’s most valuable colonies."],["Independence","Cubans fought Spain in the Ten Years’ War from 1868 to 1878 and again from 1895, led by figures such as the poet José Martí, who was killed in battle in 1895. In 1898 the United States entered the war against Spain. Cuba became independent in 1902, but the United States kept the right to intervene and a naval base at Guantánamo Bay."],["Revolution","In 1959 Fidel Castro, his brother Raúl and Che Guevara led rebels who overthrew the dictator Fulgencio Batista. The new government nationalized businesses, allied with the Soviet Union and established a one-party communist state. The United States imposed a trade embargo in 1962, which largely continues. Fidel Castro led Cuba until 2008 and died in 2016."]],
k:[["Capital","Havana"],["Population","About 10 million (2024)"],["Area","About 110,000 km²"],["Highest point","Pico Turquino, 1,974 m"],["Currency","Cuban peso"]],
d:[["1492","Columbus reaches Cuba"],["1868","The Ten Years’ War for independence begins"],["1898","The Spanish–American War"],["1902","Cuba becomes independent"],["1959","The Cuban Revolution triumphs"],["1962","The Cuban Missile Crisis"],["2015","Cuba and the United States restore diplomatic relations"]],
s2:[["The Cuban Missile Crisis","In October 1962, the United States discovered Soviet nuclear missiles in Cuba. For 13 tense days, the world came close to nuclear war, until the Soviet Union agreed to remove the missiles in exchange for a US promise not to invade Cuba and the secret removal of US missiles from Türkiye."],["Health and education","Cuba’s government made free education and health care a priority. A national literacy campaign in 1961 sent volunteers across the countryside to teach reading and writing. Cuba has many doctors per person and has sent tens of thousands of doctors to work in other countries. Critics point to shortages of medicine and equipment."],["Economic hardship","When the Soviet Union collapsed in 1991, Cuba lost its main trading partner and aid, leading to a severe crisis known as the Special Period. In the 2020s, Cuba again faced shortages of food, fuel and medicine, power blackouts and the emigration of many people. The government blames the US embargo, while critics also blame state control of the economy."],["Music and dance","Cuba is a powerhouse of music, blending African and Spanish traditions into son, rumba, mambo, cha-cha-chá and the roots of salsa. The Buena Vista Social Club recording of 1997 made older Cuban musicians famous worldwide. Cuban rumba is recognized by UNESCO."],["Old Havana","Old Havana, a UNESCO World Heritage Site, has colorful colonial buildings, plazas, forts and the Malecón seaside promenade. Classic American cars from the 1950s, kept running for decades because new cars were hard to obtain, cruise its streets and have become a symbol of the city."],["Cuba and the Philippines","Cuba and the Philippines share a history as Spanish colonies that fought for independence in the 1890s and were affected by the Spanish–American War of 1898. José Martí and José Rizal were contemporaries who both used their writing to inspire their nations. Spanish words and Catholic traditions link both cultures."]],
sp:["José Martí","José Martí, born in Havana in 1853, was a poet, journalist and revolutionary who spent much of his life in exile, writing and organizing for Cuban independence. His poems include the verses later set to music in the famous song Guantanamera. He returned to Cuba to fight in 1895 and was killed in one of the first battles. He is honored as Cuba’s national hero, and Havana’s airport bears his name."],
g:[["Embargo","An official ban on trade with a country"],["Revolution","The overthrow of a government by its people"],["Nationalize","To transfer private businesses to state ownership"],["Taíno","Indigenous people of the Caribbean"],["Son","A Cuban style of music and dance"]],
f:["Cuba is the largest island in the Caribbean.","The Cuban Revolution triumphed in 1959.","The Spanish–American War affected both Cuba and the Philippines."],
fun:"Classic 1950s American cars are still driven on Havana’s streets.",
tl:"Ang Cuba ang pinakamalaking isla sa Caribbean. Naging malaya ito noong 1902 matapos ang Digmaang Espanyol–Amerikano, ang parehong digmaan na nagwakas sa pamumuno ng Espanya sa Pilipinas. Kilala ito sa musika, sayaw at baseball."});

ADDART({id:"venezuelacountry",t:"Venezuela",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🌊",w:"Venezuela",r:["waterfalls","latinind","amazon","colombiacountry","brazilcountry","inflation"],
p:[["Land of Angel Falls","Venezuela is home to Angel Falls, the world’s tallest waterfall."],["Oil giant","It has the largest proven oil reserves in the world."],["Birthplace of Bolívar","Simón Bolívar, the liberator of much of South America, was born in Caracas."]],
l:"Venezuela is a country on the northern coast of South America, with about 28 million people. Its capital is Caracas. Venezuela has spectacular landscapes, from Caribbean beaches and Andean peaks to the Orinoco River, the Amazon rainforest and the flat-topped mountains called tepuis, including the one from which Angel Falls plunges. It holds the world’s largest proven oil reserves, which made it one of Latin America’s richest countries in the 1900s. In recent years, it has suffered a severe political and economic crisis, and millions of Venezuelans have left the country.",
s:[["Land","Venezuela includes the northern end of the Andes, where Pico Bolívar rises to 4,978 meters; the Caribbean coast; the llanos, vast tropical grasslands; and the Guiana Highlands in the southeast, with their tepuis. The Orinoco River, one of South America’s largest, flows through the country to a huge delta on the Atlantic."],["History","Columbus reached Venezuela’s coast in 1498, and Spanish explorers named it Little Venice, perhaps after Indigenous houses built on stilts over water. Venezuela declared independence on 5 July 1811. Simón Bolívar, born in Caracas in 1783, led armies that freed Venezuela, Colombia, Ecuador, Peru and Bolivia from Spain."],["Oil","Large oil deposits were found in 1914, and by the 1920s Venezuela was one of the world’s top oil exporters. Oil wealth built modern cities and highways but made the economy dependent on a single product. Venezuela was a founding member of OPEC, the organization of oil-exporting countries, in 1960."],["Crisis","From 1999, President Hugo Chávez pursued socialist policies, using oil revenue for social programs. After oil prices fell in 2014, and amid mismanagement, sanctions and corruption, the economy collapsed. Hyperinflation, shortages of food and medicine and political repression led to a mass exodus. Elections, including in 2024, were disputed, and the country’s political crisis has continued."]],
k:[["Capital","Caracas"],["Population","About 28 million (2024)"],["Area","About 916,000 km²"],["Highest point","Pico Bolívar, 4,978 m"],["Currency","Bolívar"]],
d:[["1498","Columbus reaches Venezuela’s coast"],["1783","Simón Bolívar is born in Caracas"],["1811","Venezuela declares independence"],["1914","Major oil deposits are discovered"],["1960","Venezuela helps found OPEC"],["1999","Hugo Chávez becomes president"],["2014","An economic crisis begins as oil prices fall"]],
s2:[["Angel Falls","Angel Falls, in Canaima National Park, plunges 979 meters from the tepui Auyán-tepui, making it the world’s tallest uninterrupted waterfall. The Pemon people call it Kerepakupai Merú. The surrounding tepuis inspired stories of lost worlds and the animated film Up."],["Tepuis","Tepuis are flat-topped mountains with sheer cliffs rising from the rainforest of the Guiana Highlands. They are among the oldest rock formations on Earth, about 2 billion years old. Isolated on their summits, plants and animals evolved into species found nowhere else. Mount Roraima, on the borders with Brazil and Guyana, is the most famous."],["A mass exodus","Since about 2015, more than 7 million Venezuelans have left the country, one of the largest displacement crises in the world. Most have gone to neighboring countries such as Colombia, Peru, Brazil, Ecuador and Chile, as well as Spain and the United States. Their departure has reshaped communities across Latin America."],["Beauty queens and baseball","Venezuela is famous for winning international beauty pageants, with seven Miss Universe titles. Baseball is the most popular sport, and many Venezuelan players have starred in American Major League Baseball. In music, the joropo of the llanos, played with harp, cuatro and maracas, is a national tradition."],["El Sistema","In 1975 the musician José Antonio Abreu founded El Sistema, a program providing free music education and orchestras for children, many from poor neighborhoods. It has reached hundreds of thousands of young people and inspired programs in dozens of countries. Its most famous graduate is the conductor Gustavo Dudamel."],["Wildlife","Venezuela’s ecosystems are rich in wildlife, including jaguars, giant anteaters, capybaras, the world’s largest rodent, and the scarlet ibis. The llanos flood each rainy season, attracting huge numbers of birds. Lake Maracaibo is famous for the Catatumbo lightning, flashing storms that occur on many nights of the year."]],
sp:["The everlasting storm","Over the mouth of the Catatumbo River, where it enters Lake Maracaibo, thunderstorms occur on up to about 260 nights a year, producing thousands of lightning flashes in a single night. Warm, moist air from the lake meets cool winds from the Andes, creating the perfect conditions. The Catatumbo lightning holds the record as the place with the most lightning on Earth, and sailors once used it as a natural lighthouse."],
g:[["Tepui","A flat-topped mountain with steep cliffs"],["Llanos","Tropical grasslands of Venezuela and Colombia"],["OPEC","The Organization of the Petroleum Exporting Countries"],["Hyperinflation","Extremely rapid rise in prices"],["Exodus","A mass departure of people"]],
f:["Angel Falls is in Venezuela.","Venezuela has the world’s largest proven oil reserves.","Simón Bolívar was born in Caracas."],
fun:"Lake Maracaibo in Venezuela has more lightning than anywhere else on Earth.",
tl:"Ang Venezuela ay bansa sa hilagang baybayin ng Timog Amerika. Narito ang Angel Falls at ang pinakamalaking reserba ng langis sa mundo. Ipinanganak dito si Simón Bolívar. Dumanas ito ng malubhang krisis, at milyon-milyong Venezuelan ang umalis."});

ADDART({id:"algeriacountry",t:"Algeria",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🏜️",w:"Algeria",r:["sahara","desert","africacolon","decol","islam","francecountry"],
p:[["Africa’s largest country","Algeria is the largest country in Africa by area."],["Mostly desert","More than four-fifths of its land is part of the Sahara."],["Hard-won independence","Algeria won independence from France in 1962 after a long and bloody war."]],
l:"Algeria is a country in North Africa, the largest on the continent by area, with about 47 million people. Its capital is Algiers, on the Mediterranean coast. Most Algerians live in the fertile north, while the vast south is part of the Sahara Desert. Algeria has a rich heritage of Amazigh, also called Berber, Roman, Arab, Ottoman and French influences. After 132 years of French rule, it won independence in 1962 following a war of liberation. It is a major producer of oil and natural gas, supplying much of Europe.",
s:[["Land","Northern Algeria has a Mediterranean coast, fertile plains and the Atlas Mountains. South of the mountains lies the Sahara, covering more than 80 percent of the country, with sand seas called ergs, rocky plateaus and the Hoggar Mountains, where Mount Tahat rises to 2,908 meters. Oases support towns and date palms."],["Ancient history","The Amazigh peoples have lived in North Africa for thousands of years. Ancient kingdoms such as Numidia rose in the region, which later became part of the Roman Empire; ruins such as Timgad and Djemila survive. Arab armies arrived in the 600s, bringing Islam and Arabic. From 1516, Algiers was part of the Ottoman Empire."],["French rule","France invaded Algeria in 1830 and made it part of France, encouraging hundreds of thousands of European settlers. Algerian Muslims had few rights. Resistance began immediately, led by figures such as Emir Abdelkader, and continued in various forms throughout the colonial period."],["The war of independence","On 1 November 1954, the National Liberation Front, FLN, launched a war for independence. The war was extremely violent, involving guerrilla fighting, bombings, torture and the deaths of hundreds of thousands of people. Algeria became independent on 5 July 1962, and most European settlers left."]],
k:[["Capital","Algiers"],["Population","About 47 million (2024)"],["Area","About 2.38 million km²"],["Highest point","Mount Tahat, 2,908 m"],["Currency","Algerian dinar"]],
d:[["c. 200 BCE","The kingdom of Numidia flourishes"],["600s","Arab armies bring Islam to the region"],["1516","Algiers comes under Ottoman rule"],["1830","France invades Algeria"],["1954","The war of independence begins"],["1962","Algeria becomes independent"],["2016","Tamazight becomes an official language"]],
s2:[["Tassili n’Ajjer","In the Sahara of southeastern Algeria, the Tassili n’Ajjer plateau contains thousands of ancient rock paintings and engravings, some over 10,000 years old. They show elephants, giraffes, hippos and herds of cattle, revealing that the Sahara was once green and full of life. The site is a UNESCO World Heritage Site."],["The Amazigh","The Amazigh, or Berbers, are the Indigenous peoples of North Africa. Groups such as the Kabyle in northern Algeria and the Tuareg of the Sahara have their own languages and traditions. In 2016 Tamazight, the Amazigh language, became an official language of Algeria alongside Arabic. Yennayer, the Amazigh New Year, is a public holiday."],["The 1990s","In 1991, when an Islamist party was poised to win elections, the army cancelled the vote. A brutal conflict followed between the government and armed Islamist groups, in which an estimated 150,000 or more people died during the 1990s. A national reconciliation process ended most of the violence in the early 2000s."],["Oil and gas","Algeria is one of Africa’s largest producers of oil and natural gas, which provide most of its export income. Pipelines under the Mediterranean carry gas to Italy and Spain. The government is seeking to diversify the economy into farming, manufacturing and solar energy, which the Sahara could provide in huge amounts."],["The Casbah of Algiers","The Casbah, the old city of Algiers, is a maze of narrow streets, stairways, mosques and Ottoman-era houses climbing a hillside above the sea. It played an important role during the war of independence and is a UNESCO World Heritage Site."],["Football and famous Algerians","Football is hugely popular, and Algeria’s team won the Africa Cup of Nations in 1990 and 2019. The writer Albert Camus, a Nobel laureate, was born in Algeria. The French football star Zinedine Zidane is of Algerian descent. Raï music from Oran has become popular worldwide."]],
sp:["Rock art of a green Sahara","Thousands of years ago, the Sahara was far wetter than today, with lakes, rivers and grasslands. The rock art of Tassili n’Ajjer records this lost world: hunters chasing antelope, cattle herders with their animals, swimming figures and mysterious round-headed beings. As the climate dried about 5,000 years ago, the images began to show camels instead of cattle, recording one of the greatest environmental changes in human history."],
g:[["Amazigh","Indigenous people of North Africa, also called Berbers"],["Erg","A large sea of sand dunes"],["Oasis","A place in a desert with water"],["FLN","The National Liberation Front, which led Algeria’s independence war"],["Casbah","The old fortified quarter of a North African city"]],
f:["Algeria is the largest country in Africa.","More than 80 percent of Algeria is Sahara Desert.","Algeria became independent in 1962."],
fun:"Ancient rock paintings in Algeria show hippos and giraffes in what is now the Sahara.",
tl:"Ang Algeria ang pinakamalaking bansa sa Aprika. Mahigit apat na ikalima nito ay bahagi ng Sahara. Naging malaya ito mula sa Pransiya noong 1962 matapos ang mahaba at madugong digmaan."});

;
